gs":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#what-main-is-allowed-to-be","content":"`bazelisk test //:test-fast`"},"43":{"id":"/branching-41","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#what-main-is-allowed-to-be","content":"`bazelisk test //:lint --test_tag_filters=manual`"},"44":{"id":"/branching-42","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#what-main-is-allowed-to-be","content":"the full gate `bazelisk test //:test-all`"},"45":{"id":"/branching-43","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#what-main-is-allowed-to-be","content":"All are executed inside the golden image (`images/dev/Dockerfile`) that CI builds with\nbuildx and docker-runs — the toolchain comes from that image, not from anything installed\nonto the runner."},"46":{"id":"/branching-44","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#running-the-same-gate-locally","content":"Locally, run them the same way: inside the contributor container\n(contribute/devcontainer.md) or with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image --`. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` is the bootstrap\nfor the stack host, where the suites have to run on the host itself."},"47":{"id":"/branching-45","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#who-runs-the-checks-for-you","content":"Two things run those checks for you so the gate is not a thing to remember:"},"48":{"id":"/branching-46","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#who-runs-the-checks-for-you","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:pre-commit` on demand"},"49":{"id":"/branching-47","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#who-runs-the-checks-for-you","content":"once `bazelisk run //scripts:install-hooks` has pointed `core.hooksPath` at `.githooks`,\n`.githooks/pre-commit` on every commit and `.githooks/pre-push` before anything is pushed"},"50":{"id":"/branching-48","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#who-runs-the-checks-for-you","content":"`bazelisk run //:validate` runs the fast pair plus `docker compose config -q` and the\n`.env.sample`/`.env.example` comparison; the pre-PR gate is `//:test-all`, which\n`//:validate` and the pre-push hook both reach."},"51":{"id":"/branching-49","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#cut-delete-and-hotfix","content":"Cut `feat/…`, `fix/…`, `refactor/…` (and `docs/…`, `test/…`, `chore/…`, `ci/…`) from the\ncurrent tip of `main`, one branch per change, and delete it the moment it merges. A branch\nthat outlives its change is a branch nobody rebases any more."},"52":{"id":"/branching-50","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#cut-delete-and-hotfix","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:branch-hygiene` is the check for that sentence and for the two\nbelow:"},"53":{"id":"/branching-51","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#cut-delete-and-hotfix","content":"it lists every branch the trunk already contains, every unmerged branch past the branch\nlifetime, and every branch that carries an environment in its name, each printed with its\ntip SHA because that is the record a deletion has to leave behind."},"54":{"id":"/branching-52","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#cut-delete-and-hotfix","content":"It reports and never deletes - `--plan` prints the commands, and deleting stays the\noperator's call."},"55":{"id":"/branching-53","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#cut-delete-and-hotfix","content":"Pass `--fetch` before acting on what it names, so the remote rows are the remote's\ncurrent answer rather than the last one this checkout happened to see. That flag is the\nrun's only write: it is `git remote update --prune`, which refreshes and prunes this\ncheckout's own remote-tracking refs and deletes nothing on the remote."},"56":{"id":"/branching-54","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#cut-delete-and-hotfix","content":"`fix/…` means a defect that is already on `main`."},"57":{"id":"/branching-55","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#cut-delete-and-hotfix","content":"**A hotfix branches from `main` and lands on `main` first** — every environment deploys\n*from* `main` (or from a tag cut on `main`), so there is no `staging` or `production` branch\nto patch and then remember to merge back."},"58":{"id":"/branching-56","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#name-the-branch-and-the-commit","content":"Name the branch after the change, not the author, and keep the commit subject imperative\nwith one of `feat:`, `fix:`, `docs:`, `test:`, `chore:`, `ci:`, `refactor:`."},"59":{"id":"/branching-57","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#name-the-branch-and-the-commit","content":"Below the subject leave one empty line, then the bullet points, and leave an empty line\nbetween every two bullets, so each bullet is its own paragraph."},"60":{"id":"/branching-58","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#name-the-branch-and-the-commit","content":"The same rule is in `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, which is the text every workspace\nseeds into `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, so a commit written by an agent and one written by hand read the\nsame way in `git log`."},"61":{"id":"/branching-59","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"The branch and the commits are in place while the work happens, and the push waits. This is the\norder the agent seed teaches and the order a human contributor gets the most out of:"},"62":{"id":"/branching-60","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"**Branch first, then edit.** The work branch is cut as the first action of the change, off the\ntip of `main`."},"63":{"id":"/branching-61","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"`main` is not a place to work: an agent edits it only when the operator asked for that in\nwords."},"64":{"id":"/branching-62","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"Cut late and the branch is cheap to add anyway - `git switch -c` carries the working tree\nwith it - which is the fix, where `stash`, `reset --hard`, or `checkout -- .` to clean the\ntree is how an unfinished change is destroyed."},"65":{"id":"/branching-63","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"**Commit each unit as it lands.** A unit is what a reviewer reads as one step:"},"66":{"id":"/branching-64","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"a script with its Bats file"},"67":{"id":"/branching-65","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"a template with its twin"},"68":{"id":"/branching-66","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"a flag with the docs that name it"},"69":{"id":"/branching-67","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"Commit when that unit's own check is green, then start the next one.\n`git log --oneline main..HEAD` then shows the shape of the change, and a wrong turn is one\n`git revert` away instead of a lost afternoon."},"70":{"id":"/branching-68","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"**Stage by path.** `git add <path> <path>`, and read the index back with\n`git diff --cached --name-only`."},"71":{"id":"/branching-69","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"`git add -A`, `git add .`, and `git commit -a` are how a scratch file, a build artifact, or\nsomeone else's edit walks into a commit - and the secret hygiene rule above depends on the\nagent being able to say what is in each one."},"72":{"id":"/branching-70","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"**A local commit is free, a push is not.** Nothing leaves the machine until you push, so err\ntoward committing, including a `WIP:` checkpoint before a step that might break something\ngreen."},"73":{"id":"/branching-71","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#while-the-work-happens","content":"History is local until then, so amend the message, fold the checkpoint into the commit it\nbelongs to, and reorder there; that door closes at the push, after which a shared commit is\nnever rewritten."},"74":{"id":"/branching-72","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-push","content":"**Push once, when the pull request opens**, and pre-flight it:"},"75":{"id":"/branching-73","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-push","content":"the log above reads as one commit per unit with imperative prefixed subjects"},"76":{"id":"/branching-74","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-push","content":"`git diff --stat main...HEAD` matches what was meant"},"77":{"id":"/branching-75","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-push","content":"no `.env`, token, key, or workspace-local state is in any commit"},"78":{"id":"/branching-76","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-push","content":"Small commits cost nothing at the gate: `.githooks/pre-commit` runs the hermetic half on every\ncommit and `.githooks/pre-push` runs the full set before anything leaves the machine, so a stream\nof focused commits is held to the same bar as one large one. See `main` for installing\nthem."},"79":{"id":"/branching-77","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#issues-and-how-they-close","content":"Every change answers something, and the tracker is where the answer gets recorded. This is the\nsame etiquette `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` teaches every workspace, so a bug an agent\nfixes and a bug a human fixes leave the tracker in the same state."},"80":{"id":"/branching-78","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#before-the-work","content":"**Read the report before you plan.** `gh issue view <n> --comments` is the requirements and\nthe reproduction in one place, and it is the wording the change is measured against. An agent\nreads it through `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh ...` for the reason in\ntroubleshooting.md."},"81":{"id":"/branching-79","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#before-the-work","content":"**Search before you file.** `gh issue list --state open --search '<keywords> in:title,body'`,\nthen the same with `--state closed`."},"82":{"id":"/branching-80","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#before-the-work","content":"A duplicate becomes a link to the original"},"83":{"id":"/branching-81","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#before-the-work","content":"Something `main` already fixed becomes a comment naming the commit"},"84":{"id":"/branching-82","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#before-the-work","content":"Only a genuinely new report becomes `gh issue create` - with the reproduction and the\nverbatim output in its body"},"85":{"id":"/branching-83","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#before-the-work","content":"**Announce a long fix.** For anything past an obvious edit, a short comment saying what is\nabout to change lets a maintainer redirect the work early and keeps two people off the same\nfile."},"86":{"id":"/branching-84","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#recording-the-fix","content":"**Name the issue in the branch, close it in one place.** A bug fix branches as\n`fix/<n>-<slug>`, and the closing keyword goes in the pull request body only. GitHub\nauto-links `#<n>` in a commit subject too, so repeating `Fixes #<n>` down the branch\nmultiplies one link and hides which commit answers the report."},"87":{"id":"/branching-85","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#recording-the-fix","content":"**Let the merge close it.**"},"88":{"id":"/branching-86","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#recording-the-fix","content":"`Closes #<n>` / `Fixes #<n>` claim the pull request fully satisfies the report, and GitHub\ncloses the issue when it lands on the default branch - a merge a human performs."},"89":{"id":"/branching-87","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#recording-the-fix","content":"`Refs #<n>` is the keyword for a pull request that is one step of the work, or lands before\nthe fix."},"90":{"id":"/branching-88","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#recording-the-fix","content":"Nobody closes an issue by hand to announce that a pull request exists."},"91":{"id":"/branching-89","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#recording-the-fix","content":"**A bug fix ships with its proof.** The test that fails before the change and passes after it,\nwith the reproduction's failing output quoted in the pull request. \"Cannot reproduce\" is a\nfinding to post on the issue, and the issue stays open."},"92":{"id":"/branching-90","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#recording-the-fix","content":"**Triage belongs to a person.** Closing, reopening, reassigning, labelling and milestoning are\nthe maintainer's calls, and an agent makes them only when the operator asked for that in words."},"93":{"id":"/branching-91","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-ci-workflow","content":"The `ci` workflow (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) runs for `push` to `main` and for a\n`pull_request` whose **base** is `main` or one of the sanctioned work-branch prefixes\n(`feat/**`, `fix/**`, `refactor/**`, `docs/**`, `test/**`, `chore/**`, `ci/**`)."},"94":{"id":"/branching-92","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-ci-workflow","content":"`pull_request.branches` filters on the base rather than the head, so naming only `main` left a\nstacked pull request - a dependent branch that names its parent as its base, the shape the agent\nguidance in `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` tells you to open - with no gate at all: it\ncollected just the docs deploy its branch push earned from `deploy-docs`. Gate a stack the same\nway as any other pull request."},"95":{"id":"/branching-93","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-deploy-docs-workflow","content":"The other shipped workflow, `deploy-docs`, also runs on a `push` to any branch\n(`branches: [\"**\"]`) whose diff touches its `paths:` list - `docs/**`, `docs-site/**`, the two\n`scripts/ci/` helpers it publishes through (`publish-pages-tree.sh`, `docs-alias.sh`), and its\nown workflow file - and by `workflow_dispatch`."},"96":{"id":"/branching-94","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#branch-protection","content":"Branch protection on `main` should require a pull request before pushing, and the two\nBazel checks before merging — `//:test-fast` and `//:lint`, run inside the golden image\nthe workflow builds, so a green check means the suite passed on the Linux toolchain the\nstack actually ships rather than on whatever the runner image carried that day."},"97":{"id":"/branching-95","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#environments","content":"Environments are where future protection rules and secrets go, per environment:\n`development`, `staging`, `production`."},"98":{"id":"/branching-96","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#environments","content":"`production` stays restricted to `main` — or to tags — with required reviewers. See the\nheader comment in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`."},"99":{"id":"/branching-97","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#environments","content":"Those three words are environments and never branch names, and\n`bazelisk run //scripts:branch-hygiene` is what keeps that honest: a branch or a\nremote-tracking ref named `dev`, `staging`, `prod`, `uat` or the like - bare, or as the\nfirst segment of a path - is an error and exits 1."},"100":{"id":"/branching-98","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#environments","content":"It is an operator CLI rather than a CI step on purpose: the branches that break the rule\nlive on the remote, where a job checked out at `main` cannot see them."},"101":{"id":"/branching-99","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#stacked-pull-requests","content":"A stack is one change cut into layers that each review alone, where a layer's pull request\nnames the branch below it as its base instead of `main`. Every layer is still an ordinary\nshort-lived work branch, so everything under Work branches keeps applying\nto it."},"102":{"id":"/branching-100","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-base-chain","content":"Each layer names the branch below it as its base, and only the bottom layer names `main`.\nA reviewer then reads that layer's own commits rather than the change as a whole, which is\nthe reason to stack at all."},"103":{"id":"/branching-101","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-base-chain","content":"Open every layer as a draft, and mark one ready only when its own diff is complete and the\nlayer below is green. A layer whose parent is still moving is a diff that keeps changing\nunder the reviewer."},"104":{"id":"/branching-102","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-base-chain","content":"Name the chain in the bodies. A layer states which pull request it depends on and names that\npull request's base branch, and the pull request below says a child is stacked on it. The\nbody's own sections still carry the change."},"105":{"id":"/branching-103","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-base-chain","content":"A stack stays inside one repository: it cannot cross a fork or reach a second repository, so\na change that genuinely spans two is two stacks whose bodies link each other."},"106":{"id":"/branching-104","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#the-base-chain","content":"One logical change per stack. A stack splits a change a reviewer would read together; it is\nnot a way to bundle unrelated work behind one review."},"107":{"id":"/branching-105","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#protection-merging-and-deletion","content":"Protection is inherited, never duplicated. Branch protection and CODEOWNERS apply from the\nbase of the bottom pull request - `main` - so a layer carries no rules of its own, and a\nlayer is never a place to relax one."},"108":{"id":"/branching-106","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#protection-merging-and-deletion","content":"The gate runs on every layer. See The CI workflow for why the workflow\nhas to match the work-branch prefixes rather than `main` alone."},"109":{"id":"/branching-107","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#protection-merging-and-deletion","content":"Merge bottom-up, and only through the asynchronous merge endpoint: `PUT` on\n`/repos/{o}/{r}/pulls/{n}/merge-async`, then poll the matching\n`GET .../pulls/{n}/merge-async/{uuid}` until it reports the result. Each merge hands the\nlayer above a new base, so the stack unwinds one layer at a time from the bottom. The\nrule is GitHub's own words: \"a stack cannot be merged with the legacy synchronous merge\nendpoints **or mutations**\". The async merge merges every PR in the stack up to and\nincluding the one requested, runs in the background, is atomic, and reports a\nbranch-protection failure as a failed polling result rather than a submit-time error\n(https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/reference/stacked-pull-requests-apis-and-webhooks)."},"110":{"id":"/branching-108","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#protection-merging-and-deletion","content":"Never merge a layer with the plain merge call and never merge out of that order. GitHub\nre-targets the layers above a merged parent automatically, so an upper layer is not left\npointing at a base branch that no longer exists; what GitHub does not do is rebase it. A\nstack moved onto a new base keeps carrying the commits its old base already had, and\nwhile it sits retargeted-but-not-rebased, `push` and `submit` stop until `sync` or\n`rebase` runs\n(https://docs.github.com/en/pull-requests/reference/stacked-prs-cli-commands#retargeted-stacks)."},"111":{"id":"/branching-109","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#protection-merging-and-deletion","content":"Record a layer's tip SHA before it is deleted - the same record any deletion leaves behind,\nper Work branches - and delete only the layers the trunk already\ncontains."},"112":{"id":"/branching-110","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#enabling-it-and-the-two-clis","content":"Stacks are enabled per repository, and that is an operator action in the repository's\nsettings: no script can turn it on. Which switch is not named here, because no GitHub page\nin the cited set names its label: the CLI points a reader at Settings -> General -> the\nstacked-pull-requests feature. A stack call against a repository that has them off is\nreported as a HANDOFF note, so the reader knows a person has to act rather than the command\nbeing worth retrying."},"113":{"id":"/branching-111","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#enabling-it-and-the-two-clis","content":"Every call goes through `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh api`, for the reason in\ntroubleshooting.md."},"114":{"id":"/branching-112","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#enabling-it-and-the-two-clis","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:stack-prs` is the CLI for the chain: it records the layers in order,\nbottom to top, and performs the bottom-up merges through `merge-async` and nothing else."},"115":{"id":"/branching-113","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#enabling-it-and-the-two-clis","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:branch-hygiene` still audits the topology, because the layers are\nbranches: a merged-but-undeleted layer, or one past the branch lifetime, is still its\nfinding, and an environment-named layer is still the error Environments\nalready refuses."},"116":{"id":"/branching-114","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#scope","content":"A `fix/…` branch that also has to rename a template directory, retarget a `.bats` suite, and\nupdate `docs/` is one change: the rename is not green until the last reference follows."},"117":{"id":"/branching-115","page_id":"/branching","tags":["git","branching","ci"],"type":"text","url":"/branching#scope","content":"When a rename cannot be proven complete, the reference stays (a rename is only \"done\" when\nthe old name is gone outside historical docs) and the CI file says so in a comment."},"118":{"id":"/coder-templates","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"page","content":"Coder workspace templates","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates"},"119":{"id":"/coder-templates-0","page_id":"/coder-templates","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"type":"text","url":"/coder-templates","content":"Deep reference for `templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/`: what they build, how they get pushed, and what every variable means."},"120":{"id":"/coder-templates-1","page_id":"/coder-templates","type":"heading","tags":["coder","templates","workspaces","terraform"],"url":"/coder-templates#coder-workspace-templates","content":"Coder workspace templates"},"121":{"id":"/coder-templates-2","pag