,"page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Explaining why one workspace's `~/.grok` disagrees with another's"},"2015":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-131","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Knowing why the native `web_search` tool is parked and the SearXNG lane answers"},"2016":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-132","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Deep reference for what runs *inside* a workspace and in the Coder control plane: the\nterminal profiles, the per-user `~/.grok` profile, where the CLI and the chat agent get\ntheir models, and what the stack seeds for the agent."},"2017":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-133","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-coder-agents-and-the-workspace-terminal","content":"Related: coder-templates.md (the template files themselves),\nlitellm-routing.md (what the aliases route to), and\ntroubleshooting.md."},"2018":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-134","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#terminal-and-editor-defaults","content":"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `tmux`; the automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build and task commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."},"2019":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-135","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"The `tmux` profile gives each terminal of a folder its own pane. The base session\nname is unchanged: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>`."},"2020":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-136","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"A new terminal walks into one of that folder's sessions only while no client is\nattached to it, oldest first - that is the closed-tab rejoin, so a tab you closed\nor reloaded leaves its session unattached and the next terminal finds it with its\nhistory intact, and `ssh` + `tmux attach -t <session>` lands there too."},"2021":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-137","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"A terminal opened when every session of the folder already has a client takes the\nnext numbered sibling instead: `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ..."},"2022":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-138","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"Sessions persist unattached until the container restarts, so a reattached terminal\nstill reaches the output a closed tab produced."},"2023":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-139","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"`tmux ls` lists the names and `tmux list-clients` says who is looking at each."},"2024":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-140","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"Every target the helper gives tmux carries the `=` exact-match prefix (a bare name\nis answered by any session starting with it, so a closed `foo` could be answered by\na live `foo-2` and re-create the mirror), and a reopen whose session vanished\nmid-attach raises a fresh session rather than exiting along with the tab."},"2025":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-141","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"Two tmux behaviors shape that path: a session raised by a tab's own foreground\n`new-session` has its first pane closed when that client exits\n(superuser#575909),\nwhich is why the helper creates detached and attaches separately"},"2026":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-142","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"With the default `exit-empty` the server itself leaves when its last session\ndoes (tmux#2081), which is why a\nreopen probes the server before raising anything - a tab whose server is gone has\nended normally, and re-raising there would boot a server for a terminal nobody is\nlooking at (tmux has no server probe: `has-session` answers the same for a\nmissing session and a missing server, which is why upstream declined to add\n`check-server`, tmux#2523)."},"2027":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-143","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-new-terminal-picks-its-tmux-session","content":"No upstream issue accounts for the session loss: `destroy-unattached` is off, so a\ndetached session is meant to survive, and tmux gives a session no grace period\nafter it loses its last client\n(tmux#5821 is an open request for one)."},"2028":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-144","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"One pane per visible terminal is what that rule buys. Clients attached to one\ntmux session share that session's current window, so a design that always\nreattaches makes every extra browser tab a mirror of the same pane: another\nfull xterm.js in the Chrome tab re-parsing the same stream (each holding a\nline-slot array sized from `terminal.integrated.scrollback`, with the cell\ndata allocated per line as output arrives), and another full copy of the\nrendered stream through the pty."},"2029":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-145","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"Measured on one flooding pane, 16 mirrors took 78.7 MB out of the server over an\n8 s window where a single mirror took 4.9 MB, while the server's own CPU only moved\nfrom 420 ms to 630 ms - it renders the pane once, so a mirror is paid for in the\ntab."},"2030":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-146","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"A client activating or resizing repaints the window's other clients too."},"2031":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-147","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"Measured on this workspace: 14 attached clients against 2 real panes."},"2032":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-148","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"Two terminals opening in the same instant can still land on one session - that same\nmirror, and rarer than a keystroke."},"2033":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-149","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"**An attached client is not the same thing as a viewer.** code-server's pty\nhost keeps the pty open after the browser side of a tab goes away, so the tmux\nclient stays attached, keeps its session's `session_attached` at 1, and\nreceives nothing at all."},"2034":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-150","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"Measured on this workspace's live server: 7 of its 9 clients received 0 bytes\nacross a 4 s window of continuous pane output while the 2 tabs actually on screen\neach received all 12858 of them, and the quiet ones had reported no input for 30\nminutes to 36 hours."},"2035":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-151","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"That number is the whole reuse decision in the attach helper, so one of these is\nenough to make a free session look watched forever, and the folder pays a new\nsession for every later tab."},"2036":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-152","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"Both helpers now sweep their own sessions for a client that has been quiet past a\n6 h grace before they read the snapshot; `EVO_TMUX_STALE_AFTER` (seconds) moves the\nnumber."},"2037":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-153","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"Seeing the state yourself:"},"2038":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-154","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-mirrored-pane-costs","content":"Detaching one needs its tty: this tmux 3.4 answers `detach-client -t` for a\n`/dev/pts/…` path, refuses a pid with `can't find client`, and has no\n`kill-client` command at all."},"2039":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-155","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-terminal-session","content":"**Grok Build** keeps its own session, keyed off the repository: the helper walks up\nfrom the terminal's folder with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or uses the\nterminal's `$PWD` outside a clone), names the session `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`\nafter that root, and runs `grok --cwd <repo root>` inside it. A terminal opened\nanywhere inside the clone rejoins the one session the repository shares, and starts\nGrok Build at the repository root."},"2040":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-156","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-terminal-session","content":"That profile is deliberately the old always-reattach shape: watching the same agent\nrun from several tabs is the feature, and the mirrors pay what mirrors pay. What the\nseed gives them is `focus-events on`, so a TUI in the pane can idle its animations\nwhile its tab is blurred, and `escape-time 10`, which keeps typing responsive on\neach of them."},"2041":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-157","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-terminal-session","content":"Two things it does borrow from the attach helper:"},"2042":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-158","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-terminal-session","content":"the session is created detached and attached in a second call, because the tab\nthat brings the server up is otherwise the one that can take a running agent down\nwith it (the same 9-of-20 loss the helper documents)"},"2043":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-159","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-grok-build-terminal-session","content":"The stale viewers above are detached on the way in, which is where the\n8-clients-on-one-pane count came from."},"2044":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-160","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#when-tmux-is-not-available","content":"With no tmux (offline first boot) the helper execs a plain login shell, so the\ndefault terminal still opens."},"2045":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-161","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-web-terminal-app","content":"`display_apps.web_terminal` stays **off**: it would open a shell on the agent's\nworking dir (`/home/coder`). A custom command-type `coder_app` named **Web\nTerminal** (`order = 6`, after **VS Code Web** at `order = 5`, before **GitHub\nreauth** at `order = 7` and **Install GitHub App** at `order = 8`) takes that slot,\n`cd`s to the clone folder - an empty `repo_url` keeps it on `/home/coder` - and\nexecs a login shell. Like the built-in, it reads no `terminal.integrated.*`\nsettings."},"2046":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-162","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-code-server-install-and-its-pinned-release","content":"Neither template installs code-server through the Registry. Each `main.tf` carries\nan inlined fork (`coder_script.code_server` + `coder_app.code_server`), because the\nupstream module hardcodes `/icon/code.svg` and has no icon input. Both twins pin the\nrelease in one `code_server_version` local and pass it to the upstream installer as\n`--version`: without that flag `https://code-server.dev/install.sh` follows `latest`,\nand the editor would move under every workspace with no commit in this repo."},"2047":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-163","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-code-server-install-and-its-pinned-release","content":"`workspace` installs code-server only while that pinned release is missing from\n  `/home/coder/.local/share/code-server` (the guard names\n  `lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`, the directory the installer unpacks, not\n  the `bin/code-server` symlink, which points at the last release installed and would\n  hold a warm volume on its first one) and starts it with `--auth none --port 13337\n  --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`, passing no settings payload."},"2048":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-164","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-code-server-install-and-its-pinned-release","content":"`devcontainer` has no persistent prefix to reuse, so it installs the pinned release\n  fresh under `/tmp/code-server` on every start."},"2049":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-165","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-editor-folder-and-its-icon","content":"The editor opens on `http://localhost:13337/?folder=<urlencoded>`: the\n`coder_app` resource in `coder/coder` `~> 2.18` has no `folder` argument, so the\nfolder rides in the URL and as a positional argument to the code-server binary\nwith `--ignore-last-opened`."},"2050":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-166","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-editor-folder-and-its-icon","content":"`workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and `/home/coder` with no\nrepository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a built dev container\nand `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the plain container."},"2051":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-167","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-editor-folder-and-its-icon","content":"On first start the inlined `coder_script.code_server` creates that folder and waits\nup to 60 s for `.git` (skipped when the folder is `/home/coder`) so the clone can\nland before code-server launches."},"2052":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-168","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-editor-folder-and-its-icon","content":"`coder_agent.dir` stays unset: the agent would chdir there before `startup.sh`, and\na missing clone folder would fail the agent. With the sample `REPO_URL` that folder\nis `/home/coder/workspace/evo-t1-coder-stack`."},"2053":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-169","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-editor-folder-and-its-icon","content":"The icon travels as a `data:image/svg+xml;base64` data URL read from\n`templates/common/vscode-alt.svg`: Coder serves icons only from its bundled\n`/icon/*` set or a full `https`/`data` URL, and that icon is not bundled.\n`workspace_apps.icon` is `varchar(256)` on Coder v2.36, so the SVG is a compact\nribbon whose data URL fits that column; a larger file fails `templates push`\nwith `pq: value too long for type character varying(256)`."},"2054":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-170","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"One clipboard is in play: the one on the computer you are typing on. The seed runs\n`set -g mouse off` and unbinds every key and every click that could move text into\nor out of tmux's own buffer, so the terminal client keeps each button and does the\ncopying itself, the way it does in a native terminal on that machine."},"2055":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-171","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"OSC 52 is the one route that still depends on what sits at the other end of the\nterminal, and it belongs to an app inside the pane rather than to the operator."},"2056":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-172","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Where each claim below comes from:"},"2057":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-173","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"the tmux ones were read off the shipped tmux 3.4 on this box (what an attach\nwrites to the client, and what `list-keys` still holds once the payload is sourced)"},"2058":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-174","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"the setting names, values and defaults were read out of the shipped code-server\n4.145.0's own config registry (VS Code base 1.145.0)"},"2059":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-175","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"the two Clipboard-API observations -- that a client selection fires\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText`, and that `document.execCommand('copy')` is the\nfallback that still lands on an insecure origin -- come from the earlier\nheadless-Chromium pass over that build with the rendered `settings.json` twin and\nthe tmux payload loaded, when `mouse` was still on and the copy out was a\nShift+drag"},"2060":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-176","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"That path is the client's own selection, which is what a plain drag drives now; the\nplain drag is due the same pass before it is restated as measured under `mouse off`."},"2061":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-177","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Out of a pane: drag, and it is already on the clipboard.** tmux asks the outer\nterminal for mouse reporting only while `mouse` is on: measured on tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient, and an attach with it off writes none of them."},"2062":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-178","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"With the seed's `mouse off` tmux never sees a button, so a plain drag is the\nclient's own highlight and the seed's `terminal.integrated.copyOnSelection: true`\nputs that highlight on the machine clipboard with no chord (the harness recorded\n`navigator.clipboard.writeText` firing on a client selection, before this switch)."},"2063":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-179","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Right-click and middle-click paste that clipboard.** The seed sets\n`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` - copy while there is a\nselection, paste when there is none - and pins\n`terminal.integrated.middleClickBehavior: \"paste\"`, because the config registry in\nthe shipped 4.145.0 gives that setting the default `\"default\"`, which pastes only\non the Linux build. tmux has no middle-click paste of its own to answer first:\n`MouseDown2Pane` is unbound in the root table."},"2064":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-180","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**tmux keeps no buffer, so there is nothing to paste out of one.** `Prefix-[`\nstill enters vi copy-mode and it is a viewer: the cursor keys, `PPage`/`NPage`,\n`Ctrl-u`/`Ctrl-d` and `k`/`j` walk history, `q` leaves, and `Escape` clears a\nselection."},"2065":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-181","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Every copy that used to end a selection is unbound - Enter, `C-j`, `D`, `A`, the\nword and line copies a double- or triple-click fires, the copy a drag release ran,\nand the `y` the earlier seed bound itself - along with the prefix paste `]` and the\nbuffer picker `=`."},"2066":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-182","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Selecting inside copy-mode with `Space`, `V` or the cursor keys stays bound and is\ninert, with no copy command left for it to end on."},"2067":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-183","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Proof: sourcing the payload on a scratch server leaves `list-keys` with no copy\ncommand in `copy-mode-vi`, no `paste-buffer` or `choose-buffer` in `prefix`, and no\n`MouseDown2Pane` in `root`."},"2068":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-184","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"The pointer's other jobs keep their prefix keys: `Prefix-o` or `Prefix-q` to select\na pane, and `Prefix-Alt-arrow` to resize (plain `Prefix-arrow` selects a pane in the\nshipped 3.4's own default table)."},"2069":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-185","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**`Prefix m` is the wheel, not a copy route.** One press hands the pointer to\ntmux so the wheel walks pane history, the next hands it back."},"2070":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-186","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"It is the escape hatch for a long scroll, because tmux paints in the alternate\nscreen - it sends `ESC[?1049h` either way - so the client holds no scrollback of its\nown and a wheel over a pane has nothing to walk."},"2071":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-187","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Output that has scrolled away is copied by bringing it on screen first: `Prefix-[`,\nscroll it into view, select it with a client drag, `q`."},"2072":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-188","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"A drag while `Prefix m` has the pointer on selects nothing, since the copy bindings\nstay unbound either way."},"2073":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-189","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**An app inside the pane drives the machine clipboard over OSC 52; the browser\nbuild drops it.** `set -g set-clipboard on` with `set -g allow-passthrough on`\nstay in the seed for that one route: vim's `+` register, and anything else that\nemits the escape, reach the machine clipboard the way a native terminal hands them\nto it."},"2074":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-190","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Over ssh from Ghostty, kitty, WezTerm or iTerm2 that copy lands."},"2075":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-191","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"code-server's browser build drops the escape before any clipboard call: a\nhand-written `printf '\\033]52;c;<base64>\\a'` was just as silent with\n`set-clipboard off` as with it on, which puts the drop in the browser and not in\ntmux."},"2076":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-192","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Upstream\nmicrosoft/vscode#318254 is\nopen with two unmerged fixes, and the shipped 4.145.0 registers no OSC 52 setting\nat all, so there is nothing here to turn on."},"2077":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-193","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Nothing on the tmux side can spoil that clipboard either: tmux has no copy left to\nsend, so an accidental selection in the pane cannot overwrite what the client just\nput there."},"2078":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-194","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Into the pane: `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click, on a secure context.** Both\narrive bracketed (`ESC[200~ ... ESC[201~`) in a bash terminal and in a tmux\npane alike, and a three-line paste arrives whole without a warning dialog."},"2079":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-195","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Both chords ask the browser's clipboard API, which the browser only exposes to a\nsecure context, so the URL the tab was opened on decides it."},"2080":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-196","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"On the TLS public name they work; on `http://<LAN-address>` `navigator.clipboard`\nis absent, right-click stops with VS Code's own toast (\"This browser doesn't support\nthe clipboard.readText API needed to trigger a paste, try Ctrl+V instead.\"), and the\nbrowser's native paste event is what still delivers."},"2081":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-197","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"A Clipboard site permission that was once denied breaks both API chords the same\nway, with `NotAllowedError: Read permission denied`."},"2082":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-198","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Neither is a tmux route: the prefix paste `]` and the buffer picker `=` are\nunbound, so the client's own clipboard is what arrives in the pane."},"2083":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-199","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**`Ctrl+V` on a secure origin is not a paste.** VS Code binds terminal paste to\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` on Linux and `Cmd+V` on macOS, so `Ctrl+V` reaches the pane as\nthe `\\x16` byte (readline's quoted-insert, vim's visual-block). The seed\nre-binds nothing there on purpose: a TUI in the pane owns that chord."},"2084":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-200","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"**Two scroll-back stores, two different budgets.** `terminal.integrated.scrollback`\nin the settings seed is the xterm.js buffer, which is what a plain `bash`\nprofile scrolls through; tmux's `history-limit` is what `Prefix-[` walks\ninside a pane. They read 2000 and 100000 lines here, and the two numbers\ndiffer on purpose because the two stores are billed differently."},"2085":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-201","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"What VS Code preallocates from the setting is the buffer's array of line slots -\nxterm.js builds `new Array(rows + scrollback)` for it up front\n(CircularList.ts,\nBuffer.ts)"},"2086":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-202","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"The cell data lands one `BufferLine` at a time as output arrives, at 3\n`Uint32` words per column per line\n(BufferLine.ts),\nagainst a VS Code default of 1000\n(terminalConfiguration.ts)."},"2087":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-203","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"The setting costs a pointer array per terminal in the tab either way, and real\nmemory only for the lines a pane actually emits - which is why a busy build log is\nwhere a large value is felt, and an idle prompt is not."},"2088":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-204","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"tmux holds its history once per pane, in the server, and every client of that pane\nshares the one buffer. Hence the split: the deep store is tmux's and the browser\nkeeps a short viewport."},"2089":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-205","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Inside a tmux pane the client's wheel has nothing to walk - tmux paints in the\nalternate screen, so that buffer holds no pane history - and the 100000 lines live\nbehind `Prefix-[`, paid for in the server and not in the tab."},"2090":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-206","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"Measured on tmux 3.4 with one pane pushed to its limit, 100000 lines of 120-column\noutput holds the tmux server at 381 MB RSS; the same 100000 lines of plain 80-column\noutput costs 60 MB."},"2091":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-207","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"The same sweep repeated at 120 columns came out close to linear at about 3 KB of\nserver per history line - 10 MB at 2000, 35 MB at 10000, 160 MB at 50000, 308 MB at\n100000 - so this is the one terminal number that turns a chatty pane into real\ncontainer memory, and it is a deliberate trade: the deep store is what lets\n`Prefix-[` still reach the start of a build."},"2092":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-208","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#copy-and-paste-between-a-tmux-pane-and-the-machine-you-type-on","content":"tmux reads `history-limit` when a pane is created, so an existing pane keeps the\nnumber it was born with."},"2093":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-209","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"`grok doctor` checks terminal, clipboard and microphone support without starting a\nsession, and `/doctor` inside a session adds the checks that need a live one. Each\nitem in the **Findings** list carries its own fix, and `--json` prints the same\nreport for a script. What the report should read here:"},"2094":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-210","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**tmux options.** `templates/common/tmux.conf` seeds the four options Doctor's\nown fixes write, `set -g set-clipboard on`, `set -g allow-passthrough on`,\n`set -g extended-keys on` and the `*:RGB` `terminal-features` entry, plus the\n`xterm*:extkeys` entry Doctor leaves to the operator."},"2095":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-211","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"Those last two decide what survives the multiplexer: tmux rewrites 24-bit color down\nto the palette an attached client advertises, which is what makes a theme look\nwashed out in the browser, and extkeys is what makes Shift+Enter and Ctrl+. arrive\ndistinct from Enter."},"2096":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-212","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"A client picks up `terminal-features` when it attaches, so after a reseed run\n`tmux source-file ~/.tmux.conf` and then detach and reattach (a `refresh-client`\nkeeps the client on its old features)."},"2097":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-213","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"Proof: `tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'` lists `RGB` and `extkeys`.\n`tests/bats/tmux-fidelity.bats` boots a scratch server on the seed and reads all of\nit back."},"2098":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-214","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`terminal.limited-color` during an agent turn.** A tool command launched by a\nrunning Grok session inherits `NO_COLOR=1`, `FORCE_COLOR=0`, `CLICOLOR=0` and\n`CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never` from that session, so a `grok doctor` run from inside\nan agent turn reads color as off. The `grok` process itself and the tmux server\ncarry none of those variables, so run it from a plain terminal for the real\ncapability: the line reads `color truecolor` and `themes all`."},"2099":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-215","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`voice.no-input-device`.** Expected in a workspace. The container has no\ncapture device at all (`/dev/snd` and `/proc/asound` are absent) and no\nrecorder on PATH."},"2100":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-216","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"The probe only looks for a recorder binary, so installing `alsa-utils` moves the\nline to `available` while capture still has nowhere to read from, and\n`features.voice_mode = false` leaves the finding in place."},"2101":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-217","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"Neither would be a fix, so the stack ships neither and the finding stands as an\naccurate report of a headless container. Dictation belongs on a laptop-side `grok`."},"2102":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-218","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"To give a workspace dictation instead: run PulseAudio on the workstation with its\nTCP module enabled, export `PULSE_SERVER=tcp=<workstation>:4713` into the workspace,\nand install `pulseaudio-utils` there for `parec`."},"2103":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-219","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`terminal.ssh-wrap`** is a recommendation about the computer you type `ssh`\non, since the wrapper has to sit in front of that client: run\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there, or `grok wrap ssh <host>` per connection. It\nforwards copies to the local clipboard and restores terminal modes when the\nlink drops. `/settings` -> **Show contextual hints** -> **SSH wrap** silences\nthe tip."},"2104":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-220","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-doctor-findings-in-a-workspace","content":"**`unavailable` tmux and runtime probes.** `tmux.version`,\n`tmux.client-features` and the `runtime.*` lines come back `unavailable`\nwhenever Doctor has no interactive client of its own, which covers a piped run\nand a run from an agent tool call. Read them by hand (`tmux -V`,\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`) or from `/doctor` in a live\nsession."},"2105":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-221","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-cli-is-baked-in-and-pinned","content":"Baked in at `/usr/local/bin/grok`, with an `agent` link beside it, **outside** the\n`/home/coder` home volume, so a rebuilt volume cannot lose it."},"2106":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-222","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-cli-is-baked-in-and-pinned","content":"Pinned as `GROK_VERSION` in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` (1.0.77 at the time of\nwriting, mirrored as the Dockerfile `ARG` default). The image build unpacks that\npinned `.zst` and runs `grok --version` in two PATH contexts as a build-time smoke\ncheck."},"2107":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-223","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-cli-is-baked-in-and-pinned","content":"Each template's startup script probes `command -v grok` on every start and\nre-downloads from `https://x.ai/cli/install.sh` - bounded, with retries - only\nwhile `grok` is missing from PATH. That installer follows the `stable` pointer, not\nthis pin. It deliberately does **not** fetch on every boot: the bake-in under\n`/usr/local/bin` is the upgrade path, so shipping a new CLI means bumping the pin,\nrebuilding the image (`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image`) and re-pushing the templates."},"2108":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-224","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-cli-is-baked-in-and-pinned","content":"A self-installed CLI under `~/.grok/bin` stays first on the login-shell PATH, so it\nwins over the baked copy."},"2109":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-225","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-upstream-learn-question","content":"**No published source names an upstream `/learn`, and the bundled skill set never\nlands here**: the `xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of\ndocs.x.ai, every first-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog carry zero occurrences of it."},"2110":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-226","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-upstream-learn-question","content":"The bundled platform skills are a separate set, enumerated by\n  `FORMER_PLATFORM_SKILL_HASHES` in `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/builtin.rs`\n  (help, imagine, create-skill, check-work, check, xlsx, pptx, docx, code-review,\n  best-of-n, create-workflow), and neither `learn` nor `skillify` is among them."},"2111":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-227","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-upstream-learn-question","content":"That set is downloaded at runtime through the CLI's proxy endpoints config\n  (`cli-chat-proxy.grok.com`, gated by `GROK_SKILLS_BASE_URL`) and needs an x.ai\n  sign-in: the upstream `product_skills*` fetch in\n  `crates/codegen/xai-grok-shell/src/session/slash_commands.rs` is gated on\n  `xai_grok_login::GrokAuth`."},"2112":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-228","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-upstream-learn-question","content":"This stack has no x.ai credential: LiteLLM is the sole model transport, with\n  `env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"` and `base_url` `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`, so\n  `~/.grok/bundled/` is never created and there is no bundled copy for `grok inspect` to\n  label."},"2113":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-229","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-upstream-learn-question","content":"The one surface nobody can read anonymously is the authenticated skills\n  feed (`x.ai/skills/*` answers 403), so the honest claim is that no published\n  source names `/learn`, not that it cannot exist behind that feed."},"2114":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-230","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-upstream-learn-question","content":"Nothing writes `~/.grok/skills/` either, but\n  that is a stack choice, not a CLI limit: the upstream user guide\n  (`crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/docs/user-guide/08-skills.md`) documents both tiers -\n  `~/.grok/skills/` is the User scope and `<repo_root>/.grok/skills/` the Repo one, and\n  `grok inspect` labels each definition by its actual source (`user`, `project`,\n  `bundled`) - and startup discovery skips project skills and commands in untrusted\n  folders, so a project-tier folder has to be trusted first or its skill is withheld."},"2115":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-231","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-upstream-learn-question","content":"What separates the two consumers is that the **Coder Agents** chat agent reads\n  `~/.coder/skills/` only, so reaching both means shipping both homes."},"2116":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-232","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-stacks-own-skill-commands","content":"**The stack ships its own three skill commands as `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify` and\n`/ez-create-skill`**, so they are stack payloads like the delegation skill, not something\nto install, and the `ez-` prefix keeps them clear of the upstream names they sit\nbeside: `/create-skill` is one of the CLI's bundled platform skills and\n`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory` are its built-in memory commands, while no\npublished source names a `/learn` or a `/skillify` at all."},"2117":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-233","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-stacks-own-skill-commands","content":"A user-scope skill overrides a same-named bundled one, so a seed named\n  `create-skill` would have hidden the bundled copy."},"2118":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-234","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-stacks-own-skill-commands","content":"`/ez-learn` writes memory files - it reproduces the upstream `/flush`, `/dream`,\n  `/remember` and `/memory`; the other two write skill files."},"2119":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-235","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-stacks-own-skill-commands","content":"The three payloads - `templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n  `ez-create-skill.SKILL.md` - are seeded at workspace start by\n  `coder_script.stack_skills`: each is written into `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` and\n  twin-copied to `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`, each only while that file is absent."},"2120":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-236","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-stacks-own-skill-commands","content":"To take a copy over, edit it and delete the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker\n  line: a marker-free file is user-owned, and the seeder leaves it alone unless you pass\n  `--force`. A deletion is undone - the boot writes the file again whenever it is absent,\n  and `refresh-configs` re-seeds an absent target, so the next boot restores the stack's\n  copy."},"2121":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-237","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-stacks-own-skill-commands","content":"To hide the slash command, set `disabled = [\"ez-learn\"]` under `[skills]` in\n  `~/.grok/config.toml`."},"2122":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-238","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-stacks-own-skill-commands","content":"A seeded `SKILL.md` shows up in `grok inspect --json` as `userInvocable: true`, and a\n  live session advertises it in both `slash_commands` and `skills`, so it works on\n  whichever CLI the image baked at `/usr/local/bin/grok` and this needs no image rebuild;\n  the **Grok Build** terminal profile gets it because that profile execs the same binary."},"2123":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-239","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-repository-conversion-commands","content":"**The stack ships its own three repository-conversion commands as `/ez-bazel-first`,\n`/ez-strong-types` and `/ez-trunk-based-repo`**, and these stand in for nothing upstream:\nthey convert whatever repository the workspace happens to hold, which is why they carry no\npath that only exists in this stack."},"2124":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-240","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-repository-conversion-commands","content":"They are seeded the same way, by\n`coder_script.stack_repo_skills`, from `templates/common/ez-bazel-first.SKILL.md`,\n`ez-strong-types.SKILL.md` and `ez-trunk-based-repo.SKILL.md`, plus the three python\nhelpers they shell out to. Same absent-only contract, same marker-based takeover rule,\nsame `[skills] disabled` escape hatch."},"2125":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-241","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dispatch-plan-command","content":"**A fourth command rides that same body: `/ez-micro-plan`.** It converts nothing - it is\nthe parent's dispatch protocol for a fan-out of subagents: a repo-local plan at\n`.grok/plans/<slug>/plan.md`, the change split into micro tasks, layered context briefs\nbeside it, and a check that refuses a plan too narrow to fill the session's subagent\nslots."},"2126":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-242","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dispatch-plan-command","content":"It shares the body because that body exists to stay under the one-argument ceiling\n(coder-templates.md), not because the\nfour commands do the same job; three of the four carry a python helper next to their\n`SKILL.md` and `ez-trunk-based-repo` carries none."},"2127":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-243","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dispatch-plan-command","content":"Its payloads are `templates/common/ez-micro-plan.SKILL.md` and\n`templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py`, seeded into `~/.coder/skills/` and\nits `~/.agents` twin on the same absent-only contract, the helper under `scripts/`\nlike the trio's."},"2128":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-244","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dispatch-plan-command","content":"What the plan carries and what the check prints is under\nThe fan-out seam."},"2129":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-245","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-retired-learn-seed","content":"**A leftover `/learn` seed retires itself.** This skill was seeded under the name\n`learn` until it moved, and a copy left standing keeps offering that old name beside\n`/ez-learn` for the life of the volume; there is no bundled `learn` for it to hide,\nbecause no published source names one."},"2130":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-246","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-retired-learn-seed","content":"Both startup scripts delete `~/.coder/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and\n`~/.agents/skills/learn/SKILL.md` and prune the directory each leaves, but only while\nthe file still carries the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` marker; a marker-free\n`learn/SKILL.md` is user-owned and stays."},"2131":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-247","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-retired-learn-seed","content":"`refresh-configs`, `storage-clean` and `seed-dev-env` retire the same two paths on\nreseed."},"2132":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-248","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-retired-learn-seed","content":"That removal only finishes the rename: there is no upstream `/learn` waiting on the\nname, because no published source names one."},"2133":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-249","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"Rendered per workspace from `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` (and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy of the same payload), and re-rendered idempotently\nwhile the managed block is intact."},"2134":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-250","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"At top level the render sets `disable_web_search = true`, which parks Grok's native\n`web_search` tool: no transport this box ships serves that tool type, so the\nself-hosted `searxng` MCP server is the search lane. The search lane section below\ncarries the measurement and how to flip the key back."},"2135":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-251","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rendered-grok-config","content":"`[models] default` points at the `grok_default_model` alias - `agent` unless the\ntemplate variable or the create form's **Grok default model** dropdown names\nanother. `[models] default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`. The Spark lane answers\n`low` / `medium` / `xhigh`; `high` is never advertised."},"2136":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-252","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-alias-set-and-the-reasoning-levels","content":"The twelve aliases (`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`,\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review`, `agent-xhigh-fast`, `agent-swarm-fast`, `coder`,\n`coder-fast`, `chat`) are defined with `base_url` set to `litellm_url` and\n`env_key = \"LITELLM_API_KEY\"`. The key is injected as an environment variable, so\nit never appears in a file."},"2137":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-253","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-alias-set-and-the-reasoning-levels","content":"`agent` carries three `[[model.\"agent\".reasoning_efforts]]` tables (`xhigh` is\nthe default). The `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` / `agent-swarm` /\n`agent-explore` / `agent-review` / `agent-xhigh-fast` / `agent-swarm-fast` aliases\nset `supports_reasoning_effort = false` because LiteLLM bakes the level for\ncallers that have no reasoning-effort field."},"2138":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-254","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-reader-lane-and-the-skeptic-lane","content":"The `agent-explore` / `agent-review` pair is the\nreader and the skeptic: the `explore` type and the seeded `explorer` ride\n`agent-explore` (`low`), and the seeded\n`reviewer` and `security-auditor` ride `agent-review` (`xhigh`). What each lifts, what\nit costs, and the `--var` that rolls it back are in\nlitellm-routing.md."},"2139":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-255","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-fast-compaction-pair","content":"The `agent-xhigh-fast` / `agent-swarm-fast` pair is their parent lane with one call of the\nconversation answered elsewhere: on those two aliases only, the proxy callback\n`litellm/compaction_router.py` re-serves the CLI's conversation-summary call from the Arc\ngroup `compaction-fast` and leaves every other call of the session on its Spark box.\nNothing in either `[model.*]` block names that target, because no client key could - the\nhop is server-side, and it forks on `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (24000 tokens\nestimated):"},"2140":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-256","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-fast-compaction-pair","content":"within it the summary goes to the Arc group"},"2141":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-257","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-fast-compaction-pair","content":"above it to the Spark group `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` (default `agent-low`)"},"2142":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-258","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-fast-compaction-pair","content":"because the Arc weights answer inside a 32768-token window and a bigger input would be\ntruncated into a useless summary of the history the call exists to preserve."},"2143":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-259","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-fast-compaction-pair","content":"Both are selectable as `grok_default_model` and neither is a Coder Agents chat model. The\nmechanism, the five `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION*` knobs, and the 107 s to 1915 s Spark-side\ncompactions that motivated it are in\nlitellm-routing.md."},"2144":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-260","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-session-pin-header","content":"`[models]` also carries `extra_headers` with\n`x-litellm-session-id: ws-<workspace-id>` - the key LiteLLM uses to pin a\nworkspace's traffic to one healthy Spark box. Each workspace renders its own\nkey: `config.toml` is that workspace's own file."},"2145":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-261","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-subagent-width-and-depth-keys","content":"`[subagents]` sets how wide a fan-out goes and `[subagents.models]` which lane each\ntype rides, both rendered from template variables: `grok_subagents_lane` (default\n`agent-swarm`) for the writers, three more for the reader, planner and skeptic roles named\nbelow, and `grok_subagent_max_concurrent` (default 15, the variable's own shipped\nnumber; `null` is kept as the opt-in that hands the width to `grok_swarm_width`, the\nfleet's own ceiling), and `grok_subagent_max_depth` (default 2,\nvalidated 1..3) sets how deep the tree of children may go."},"2146":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-262","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-subagent-width-and-depth-keys","content":"The width and the ceiling are two numbers on purpose, and `main.tf` is where they meet:\n  `grok_subagent_max_concurrent = var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent != null ? var.grok_subagent_max_concurrent : var.grok_swarm_width`\n  So the width is the variable's own shipped 15 whichever lane the writers ride, and the\n  `: var.grok_swarm_width` arm answers only a caller that passes null on purpose;\n  `--var grok_subagents_lane=agent` is the rollback that puts the writers back on\n  the parent's pinned box and leaves that width alone, and an explicit\n  `--var grok_subagent_max_concurrent=N` always wins."},"2147":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-263","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-subagent-width-and-depth-keys","content":"The variable's own validation accepts any whole number 1..32 it is given, plus that\n  explicit null,\n  because past the fleet's ceiling a bigger number is only a longer queue."},"2148":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-264","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-pinned-lane-costs","content":"A parent session and every subagent it spawns\ncarry the same `x-litellm-session-id`, so on a pinned lane the whole fan-out resolves\nto one deployment and competes for that box's `PARALLEL` streams - 5 by default, set in\n`scripts/spark-configure.sh` - so the width that is useful on one box is about 4: that\nbox's `PARALLEL` minus the parent's own stream, which is why an operator who pins\neverything to one box narrows the queue by setting `grok_subagent_max_concurrent`\nrather than by switching lanes."},"2149":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-265","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-swarm-lane-buys","content":"The `agent-swarm` default is where widening pays: that\ngroup carries no pin, so each subagent request is placed on\nwhichever reachable box has the fewest requests in flight and the ceiling becomes\n`PARALLEL` per box rather than per workspace - 3 boxes x 5 = 15, which asks the fleet\nfor every stream it has, so treat 15 as a one-workspace-at-a-time number."},"2150":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-266","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-swarm-lane-buys","content":"`sampling_limit` is deliberately left unset: it defaults to `max_concurrent`, so the\ntwo caps cannot drift apart. `limit_behavior = \"queue\"` below them holds the overflow\ninstead of failing the spawn, so overshoot costs a subagent latency rather than its\nlife."},"2151":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-267","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-deep-the-tree-of-children-may-go","content":"`max_depth` rides beside them, rendered from `grok_subagent_max_depth` and shipped\nat 2, and at 2 a child may spawn: `reviewer`, `security-auditor` and `tester` name\n`spawn_subagent` in their `tools` (`templates/common/reviewer.AGENT.md:5`), so a skeptic\nor a tester can fan read-only mappers out one level under itself."},"2152":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-268","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-deep-the-tree-of-children-may-go","content":"Each of those grandchildren is its own session with its own `max_concurrent`, and what\nit costs the\nfleet is one model stream, so a tree is budgeted by summing the in-flight `1 + spawns`\nover every task and keeping that total at or below the fleet's stream count (`fleet:` in\nthe plan, 15 by default, `templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py:42`). That total\nis a separate number from the session cap above, which counts one parent's own children."},"2153":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-269","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-deep-the-tree-of-children-may-go","content":"`max_depth = 1` is the rollback to no nesting, and it restores the behaviour the fleet\nshipped with before a child could spawn."},"2154":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-270","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-lane-variables","content":"`[subagents.models]` pins every subagent type a session can get to a lane of its\nown, and there are four variables behind those pins, not one: `grok_subagents_lane`\n(default `agent-swarm`) for the writers, `grok_explore_lane` (default `agent-explore`) for the\nmappers, `grok_plan_lane` (default `agent`) for the planner, and `grok_review_lane`\n(default `agent-review`) for the skeptics."},"2155":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-271","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-lane-variables","content":"Concretely the block renders `general-purpose`, `implementer` and `tester` from the\n  writers' lane, `explore` and\n  `explorer` from the reader's, `plan` and `architect` from the planner's, and `reviewer`\n  and `security-auditor` from the skeptic's - the last six keys are the seeded role\n  definitions named in the seeded fleet suite, and a key no\n  session selects is inert."},"2156":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-272","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-lane-variables","content":"Splitting the block that way is what lets one role go back on the parent's lane without\n  moving the others: a wave of mappers can spread over the whole\n  fleet while the planner that steers the next wave stays pinned to one box."},"2157":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-273","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-lane-variables","content":"Pinning only the two read-only types was the defect\nthis block fixes: a spawn that names no type gets\n  `general-purpose`, and that is the type every writer in a fan-out runs as, so those\n  children inherited the parent's model and queued behind the one box a pinned parent\n  resolves to while the swarm-named readers spread over three."},"2158":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-274","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-lane-variables","content":"What those four variables decide is placement only:\nthe in-flight width comes from `grok_subagent_max_concurrent`\n  (its own shipped 15) on every lane, so `grok_subagents_lane` remains the knob a workspace\n  turns on to fan out, and narrowing that width is the same variable rather than the lane."},"2159":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-275","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-feature-flags","content":"`[features]` carries `telemetry = false`, `web_fetch = true` and\n`active_agent_messages = true`, the last of them giving the parent\n`send_subagent_message`: a child already running can be handed a new instruction and\nkeep its context, which is cheaper than killing it and dispatching a replacement. All\nthree keys are static in `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:368-373` and none\nis rendered from a template variable."},"2160":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-276","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-mcp-server-blocks","content":"`github_mcp` (default `true`, so the block renders unless you park it with\n`GITHUB_MCP=false` and re-push) adds the `github` HTTP MCP server\n(`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`), whose `Authorization` header references\n`${GH_TOKEN}` **by name**, so no token value is ever part of a template variable.\nThe agent environment fills it from the Coder External Auth provider (id `github` by default); a\nworkspace whose owner connected no GitHub account renders the block anyway and pays\none failed handshake per session."},"2161":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-277","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-mcp-server-blocks","content":"`context7_mcp` (default `true`, parked the same way with `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`) adds\nthe cached `context7` stdio MCP server."},"2162":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-278","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-mcp-server-blocks","content":"Grok launches `/opt/npm-global/bin/mcp-cache-proxy` with\n`--config /home/coder/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/config.json`, and that proxy fronts the\nofficial `/opt/npm-global/bin/context7-mcp`; both are pre-installed in the golden\nimage, so nothing is fetched with `npx` at launch."},"2163":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-279","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-mcp-server-blocks","content":"The proxy config names `$CONTEXT7_API_KEY` and nothing else: the key reaches the\nworkspace environment from the sensitive template variable `context7_api_key`, filled\nby the push from `CONTEXT7_API_KEY` in the host `.env` - not from Coder External Auth,\nwhich is what fills `GH_TOKEN` above."},"2164":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-280","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-verbatim-escape-hatch","content":"`grok_config_extra` appends whatever the create form's **Grok config extras** field\nholds, verbatim under its own comment header."},"2165":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-281","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-approval-default-is-a-stack-decision","content":"`[permission]` holds the guardrails the stack owns. The approval mode is a stack\ndefault now rather than a per-user call: the render writes\n`[ui] permission_mode = \"always-approve\"` unless the create form's **Grok approval\nmode** field (`grok_permission_mode`, whose accepted values are `default`, `ask`,\n`auto`, `always-approve`) names another, and the contributor seed takes the same\nchoice from `GROK_PERMISSION_MODE`."},"2166":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-282","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-approval-default-is-a-stack-decision","content":"What relaxes is prompting, not the guardrails: a\n`deny` rule still outranks every other list under always-approve, so the dotenv reads\nand the `rm -rf` deny still refuse."},"2167":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-283","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dotenv-deny-and-the-template-allow-list","content":"`Bash(rm -rf *)` blocks the destructive\nform, and the dotenv family is split by what a file actually holds:"},"2168":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-284","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dotenv-deny-and-the-template-allow-list","content":"the live `.env` and the populated suffixed shapes (`.env.local`, `.env.*.local`,\n`.env.production` / `.env.prod`, `.env.staging` / `.env.stage`, `.env.development` /\n`.env.dev`, `.env.test` / `.env.testing`, `.env.ci`, `.env.override`) are denied for\n`Read` and `Edit`"},"2169":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-285","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dotenv-deny-and-the-template-allow-list","content":"the secret-free templates a repo ships (`.env.example`, `.env.sample`,\n`.env.template`, `.env.dist`, `.env.defaults`, plus the `.env.*.example` /\n`.env.*.sample` spellings) are named in `allow`, so an agent maintains the env key\nlist without a prompt in any mode."},"2170":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-286","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dotenv-deny-and-the-template-allow-list","content":"Neither list is the old blanket `**/.env.*`: deny outranks\nallow, so the blanket form blocked the templates along with the live files and\nleft the key list -- the file that keeps a real `.env` honest -- to drift."},"2171":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-287","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-dotenv-deny-and-the-template-allow-list","content":"`.gitignore` carries the matching half on the git side: the whole family is\nignored except the names `allow` lists."},"2172":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-288","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-git-verbs-the-branch-first-policy-needs","content":"`allow` also carries the verbs the branch-first, commit-per-unit policy needs:\n`Bash(git switch *)`, `Bash(git checkout -b *)` and `-B`, `Bash(git add *)`,\n`Bash(git commit *)`."},"2173":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-289","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-git-verbs-the-branch-first-policy-needs","content":"A grant that cannot be had is how that policy fails quietly:\nan unattended run (`grok -p`, how the delegation skill launches its children)\nanswers \"headless run cannot ask for approval\" and drops the call, so a child\nwould finish a whole task with its work uncommitted. Everything on that list is\nlocal to the checkout and undone by another git command."},"2174":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-290","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-git-verbs-the-branch-first-policy-needs","content":"`git push` is absent on purpose - the one step that leaves the machine keeps a human in\nit - and so is\nanything that can lose work (`git branch -D`, `git restore`,\n`git reset --hard`). The deny side closes the two ways a commit could land with\nno gate having seen it:"},"2175":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-291","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-git-verbs-the-branch-first-policy-needs","content":"`Bash(git commit --no-verify *)` / `-n`, past the hermetic pre-commit hook"},"2176":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-292","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-git-verbs-the-branch-first-policy-needs","content":"`Bash(git add -f *)` / `--force`, past `.gitignore`"},"2177":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-293","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-git-verbs-the-branch-first-policy-needs","content":"Both are prefix globs, so they narrow that door rather than seal it, and the\n`Stage by path` rule in the seed text is the rest of the defence."},"2178":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-294","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#why-the-recursive-delete-deny-catches-benign-cleanups","content":"`Bash(rm -rf *)` is a prefix glob, so it catches a benign per-path scratch cleanup\nalong with the destruction it is there to prevent. Agents run into that constantly:\nacross 271 workspace sessions, 86 recorded at least one denied tool call and 56\nattempted an `rm -rf`, and 55 sessions did both."},"2179":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-295","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#why-the-recursive-delete-deny-catches-benign-cleanups","content":"The shapes that are never denied are per-path deletions - `find <dir> -type f -delete`, or\n`rm -f` naming each path - so clean scratch that way."},"2180":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-296","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#why-the-recursive-delete-deny-catches-benign-cleanups","content":"An `allow` entry cannot rescue `rm -rf`, because deny outranks allow. Narrowing the pattern\nis an operator call and a deliberate one: it is the only thing between an agent and a\nrecursive delete on a path it got wrong."},"2181":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-297","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"The self-teardown family is denied alongside it, because an agent that closes its own\nterminal cannot finish the task that closed it. The following each match a `deny` rule:"},"2182":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-298","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"`tmux kill-server` / `kill-session` / `kill-window` / `kill-pane` / `restart-server`"},"2183":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-299","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"`pkill` and `killall` naming `tmux`, `node`, `code-server`, `grok` or `bash`"},"2184":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-300","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"those killers aimed at a whole user or parent (`pkill -u`, `-U`, `-P`)"},"2185":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-301","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"the shell forms that signal a whole process group (`kill 0`, `kill -- -<pgid>`,\n`kill -9 -1`)"},"2186":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-302","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"The reason is the shape of the workspace: the Bash tool runs inside the tmux session\nthe agent was launched in, so a sweep that tidies up \"stray\" sessions or processes\ntakes the agent, its tool shell and the in-flight reply down together, and the editor\ntab dies with `terminated with exit code: 1` over work left half-done."},"2187":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-303","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"An agent stops one process it started with the `kill_command_or_subagent` tool; bringing\ndown a whole tmux server or editor host stays an operator call."},"2188":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-304","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-self-teardown-deny-family","content":"These are globs over the parsed command text, so an abbreviated tmux verb\n(`tmux kill-sess`) or a bracketed pattern (`pkill -f [t]mux`) walks past them - the seed\nrule that names the prohibition is the other half, and the plain `bash` profile is how a\nsession reaches a shell once they are all gone."},"2189":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-305","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-marker-that-keeps-the-config-stack-owned","content":"Stack ownership of the file rests on either marker spelling: the line-1\n`managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment or the top-level `__evoT1Stack = true` key.\nBoth twins emit both, because the comment alone does not last - `grok mcp add`\npersists its own settings by rewriting the file and deletes every comment line, so a\ncomment-only marker handed the config to the user with no human in the loop, and the\ndefault lanes silently stopped arriving."},"2190":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-306","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-marker-that-keeps-the-config-stack-owned","content":"A hand-edited config is left alone only\n  while it carries one of them, so takeover means deleting **both** lines; delete the\n  file and restart to hand it back."},"2191":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-307","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-marker-that-keeps-the-config-stack-owned","content":"When the guard does re-render, the CLI-owned tables `[cli]`, `[ui]` and `[marketplace]` are\n  carried over rather than wiped. The `[ui]` copy comes across minus the keys the render owns,\n  and `permission_mode` is one of them now: the startup strips it from the operator's copy so\n  the render's own line cannot land beside it, which would hand one `[ui]` table two identical\n  keys and the CLI an invalid TOML file."},"2192":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-308","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-marker-that-keeps-the-config-stack-owned","content":"The strip says what it took - when it drops a mode the start prints one `warning:` line\n  naming the value it dropped (`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl:248`), so an approval\n  mode lost at a restart is lost in the open. A workspace that quietly starts prompting, or\n  stops prompting, is otherwise a report nobody can reproduce."},"2193":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-309","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-marker-that-keeps-the-config-stack-owned","content":"And the create form's four are what the `[ui] permission_mode` key documents, not the whole\n  vocabulary: the CLI's own `--permission-mode` flag spells more\n  (`templates/workspace/main.tf:389-392`). One of those belongs in the operator's `[ui]` block\n  by hand, and the next start replaces it with the form's answer and says so on the startup\n  log."},"2194":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-310","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-fan-out-seam","content":"Three stack-owned numbers decide whether a fan-out of subagents is parallel here at all:\nhow many slots one session gets, which lane those children's traffic rides, and whether\nthe work was cut finely enough to fill the slots. The first two are the rendered\n`[subagents]` keys just above; the third is a seeded skill and one command the parent runs\nbefore wave 1."},"2195":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-311","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"**How a session learns its own ceiling.** `[subagents] max_concurrent` is the per-session\nnumber, and `sampling_limit` is left unset because it defaults to that same number - one\nvariable, so the two caps cannot drift apart. The parent reads it once per session and\ndispatches to it:"},"2196":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-312","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"The rulebook (`~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, from\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`) states the ceiling as that key rather than as a\nnumber of its own: the prompt used to hardcode a smaller fleet and to tell the parent to\nrun two or three children per wave, and both are gone, because a cap the prompt invents is\nwrong on both lanes - it leaves slots empty on a swarm session and overbooks a pinned\none."},"2197":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-313","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"Overflow is held, not failed (`limit_behavior = \"queue\"`), so an under-wide wave is\nsilent and counting it stays the parent's job."},"2198":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-314","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"The same grep prints the `max_depth` line\nbeside `max_concurrent`, and that key is now rendered from `grok_subagent_max_depth`\n(default 2, validated 1..3) by `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:429`."},"2199":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-315","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"At the shipped 2 a child may spawn: `reviewer`, `security-auditor` and `tester` name\n`spawn_subagent` in their `tools`, so a skeptic can put a wave of read-only mappers under\nitself and get its evidence without a round trip through the parent."},"2200":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-316","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"Each grandchild is its own session, so it is not charged to the slot count above; what it\ndoes cost is a model stream, which is why a tree is counted by summing the in-flight\n`1 + spawns` over every task and keeping that total at or below the fleet's stream count\n(`fleet:` in the plan, 15 by default)."},"2201":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-317","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"`max_depth = 1` is the rollback: no child spawns, and one parent's slot count again\ndescribes the whole tree."},"2202":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-318","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"**Two numbers, one rule.** The same grep opens its four lines of context with\n`# fleet-width: 15` - the fleet's own ceiling, rendered into the same table from\n`var.grok_swarm_width` (`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:427`, fed at\n`templates/workspace/main.tf:558`) so an explicit width never erases it - while the number\nbelow it is `grok_subagent_max_concurrent`, the variable's own shipped 15. The pair is\nwhat the width gets read against:"},"2203":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-319","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"Equal numbers mean the session is sized for the fleet: every slot it holds is a stream a\nbox can actually serve."},"2204":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-320","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"A width below its fleet-width means a persisted value - on the template, from a raw\n`coder templates push --var`, or on the workspace, from `coder start --parameter` /\n`coder update --parameter` or the Variables tab (`--var` is a push-only flag;\n`coder start` has none) - unless an operator set it there on purpose to sit inside one\npinned box."},"2205":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-321","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"A width above its fleet-width is the pair a one-knob fleet change leaves behind: the two\nkeys are separate numbers, so a workspace that shrank the fleet with\n`--var grok_swarm_width=10` moved the ceiling alone and kept the shipped 15."},"2206":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-322","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"That pair is not a stale stored value, it is a fleet that got smaller under a width that\nnever moved."},"2207":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-323","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:subagent-check` reads both and names which of the three readings you\nhave, and counts the above one as a `note:` beside the ok items, since past the fleet's\nstreams the extra spawns queue in LiteLLM rather than failing."},"2208":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-324","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ceiling-and-the-fleet-width","content":"The same comparison runs at every start, in stage 6 of\n`templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl`, where a narrower width is one `note:` line on the\nstartup log and a wider one prints nothing. Neither value can be cleared from inside the\nworkspace, because both sit in the Coder database on the host. So shrinking a shared fleet\nis a two-knob job: dropping `grok_swarm_width` moves the ceiling alone, and\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent` has to go with it in the same push, or a 15-wide session\nmeets `NoRoom` on a 10-stream fleet."},"2209":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-325","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"**What the lane choice buys.** `grok_subagents_lane` is the knob for the writers and it\ndecides placement, not size: the in-flight width comes from\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent` (the variable's own shipped 15) on every lane, and only a\nworkspace that sets it to `null` on purpose takes the width from `grok_swarm_width` instead\n(its validation accepts any whole number 1..32 plus that explicit null)."},"2210":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-326","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"That 15 is the fleet's arithmetic rather than taste."},"2211":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-327","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"A parent and every child it spawns carry the same `x-litellm-session-id`, so a pinned lane\nplaces the whole fan-out on one box and the useful width is that box's `PARALLEL` - 5 by\ndefault, from `scripts/spark-configure.sh` - minus the parent's own stream; past that, extra\nslots are LiteLLM queue depth."},"2212":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-328","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"`agent-swarm` is one of four Spark groups with no session pin -\n`agent-explore`, `agent-review` and `agent-swarm-fast` lift it the same way, and\nlitellm-routing.md\nis where what each lane buys is worked out - so each child lands on whichever reachable\nbox has the fewest requests in flight and the ceiling\nbecomes `PARALLEL` per box: 3 boxes x 5 = 15, which is every stream the fleet has and\ntherefore a one-workspace-at-a-time number."},"2213":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-329","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"The pins no longer put every type on that one lane: `[subagents.models]` renders"},"2214":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-330","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"`general-purpose` and the seeded `implementer` and `tester` from `grok_subagents_lane`"},"2215":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-331","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"`explore` and the seeded `explorer` from `grok_explore_lane` (default `agent-explore`)"},"2216":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-332","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"`plan` and the seeded `architect` from `grok_plan_lane` (default `agent`, still pinned,\nbecause one planner is a conversation that steers every wave after it)"},"2217":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-333","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"the seeded `reviewer` and `security-auditor` from `grok_review_lane` (default\n`agent-review`)"},"2218":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-334","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"What has not changed is why the block exists at all: every writer in a fan-out is a\n`general-purpose` child, and while only the two read-only types were pinned those writers\ninherited the parent's model, so a swarm-named wave still queued its edits behind one box's\nstreams."},"2219":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-335","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"The lane is therefore a config decision: the guide documents `[subagents.models]` as the per-type\noverride that \"applies for any parent\", and documents the spawn call's own `model`\nargument as the agent's choice of one child's model, without saying which of the two wins\nwhen both name one. Set the pin when the lane matters."},"2220":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-336","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"**The review pass is a panel.** The independent check that closes a fleet run is a wave of\nskeptics dispatched in one turn, each riding `agent-review` through the `grok_review_lane`\npin above, each owning no file, and each re-running the producers' own `done:` commands\nrather than reading their reports. The default panel is three angles:"},"2221":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-337","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"requirements, which answers every acceptance check kept or broken"},"2222":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-338","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"proof, which runs the `done:` commands and quotes what they printed"},"2223":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-339","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"adversary, which looks for the regression, the edge state and the flag-off path"},"2224":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-340","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"Each may fan out up to three read-only mappers of its own under the depth above."},"2225":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-341","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"`templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py:227` accepts a `review:` key\nnaming the whole panel, comma or space separated, and holds each reviewer to owning nothing\nwhile needing every task that ships a file."},"2226":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-342","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"A contributor home gets the same seam from the environment of the seed, so a laptop\nmatches what a template `--var` renders:"},"2227":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-343","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"`GROK_SUBAGENTS_LANE` names the writers' lane and `GROK_SUBAGENT_MAX_CONCURRENT` the width,\nwhich defaults to the same `default = 15` the variable ships in both twins' `coder.tf`, so\nthe seeder and the template agree on the width without a second knob."},"2228":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-344","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"`GROK_SWARM_WIDTH`\n(default 15) now feeds only the `# fleet-width:` line the seed writes directly above the\nwidth, `scripts/seed-dev-env.sh:286`, so a contributor home carries the same pair a\nworkspace renders; `GROK_EXPLORE_LANE` (default `agent-explore`),\n`GROK_PLAN_LANE` (default `agent`) and `GROK_REVIEW_LANE` (default `agent-review`) name the\nthree role lanes the same way."},"2229":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-345","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#lane-choice-and-the-review-panel","content":"A value the seeder cannot use is refused in place and\nthe default goes in, so a typo is a line in the output rather than a broken key in\n`~/.grok/config.toml`:"},"2230":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-346","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"**How the work is cut.** `/ez-micro-plan` is the parent's protocol for splitting one\nmulti-file change across that fleet, and it writes its own document, separate from Grok\nBuild's `/plan`: `/plan` lives at `~/.grok/sessions/<cwd>/<session-id>/plan.md`, is the\nPlan Mode preview, and dies with the session, while this one is repo-local at\n`.grok/plans/<slug>/plan.md` and is what the parent and every child read from."},"2231":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-347","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"It carries `## Goal` with numbered acceptance checks, `## Dispatch` (lane, `slots:`, where the slot\ncount came from, `parent keeps:` for the hotspots the parent merges itself), `## Lanes`,\n`## Tasks`, `## Ledger`, and `## Not doing`. Beside it sit the context briefs and the\nchild reports:"},"2232":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-348","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"| file        | answers                                    | written by | read by     | cap      |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------ | ---------- | ----------- | -------- |\n| `ctx/L0.md` | what is being built, and every pinned seam | parent     | every child | 80 lines |","table":"table-0"},"2233":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-349","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"| file               | answers                                      | written by | read by              | cap      |\n| ------------------ | -------------------------------------------- | ---------- | -------------------- | -------- |\n| `ctx/L1-<lane>.md` | that lane's file map and the pattern to copy | parent     | that lane's children | 40 lines |","table":"table-0"},"2234":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-350","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"| file              | answers                           | written by           | read by   | cap      |\n| ----------------- | --------------------------------- | -------------------- | --------- | -------- |\n| `ctx/L2-T<nn>.md` | the one fact only this task needs | parent, just in time | one child | 20 lines |","table":"table-0"},"2235":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-351","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"| file               | answers                          | written by | read by | cap      |\n| ------------------ | -------------------------------- | ---------- | ------- | -------- |\n| `results/T<nn>.md` | what happened and what was found | child      | parent  | 12 lines |","table":"table-0"},"2236":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-352","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"One writer per layer is the rule that keeps a child from inheriting the parent's session:\nchildren write only their own `results/T<nn>.md`, never a brief, and the parent is the\nonly thing that moves a fact sideways from one child to the next (a child reports\n`found:`, the parent writes it into the lane brief before the next wave)."},"2237":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-353","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"`ctx/L0.md` is frozen before wave 1, because children already running read the copy that\nwas there when\nthey started - editing it mid-run is a re-dispatch, not an edit."},"2238":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-354","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"A task is micro when all of this holds:"},"2239":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-355","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"one deliverable"},"2240":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-356","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"three or fewer files in `owns` from one directory family"},"2241":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-357","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a `calls:` budget of 15 or fewer counting the reads a child needs to get oriented"},"2242":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-358","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"one `done:` command a reviewer can run"},"2243":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-359","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"no other ready task sharing a path"},"2244":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-360","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"`needs:` naming only finished tasks"},"2245":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-361","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"Below that bar the plan costs more than it saves, which is why the skill opens by asking\nwhether the change touches about five files at all."},"2246":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-362","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"**How it is dispatched, and what the check prints.** `plan_check.py` is the gate, and it\nis the same file in both skill homes:"},"2247":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-363","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"It prints one `error:` or `warning:` line per finding and then a tally, so a parent reads\nsix lines instead of a plan:"},"2248":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-364","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"The bare ids after the summary are the wave to spawn - `--ready` prints the tasks whose\n`needs:` are all satisfied, capped at `slots`, and the parent takes that set rather than\nthe plan's order and spawns the whole wave in one turn."},"2249":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-365","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"The refusals are the collisions a fan-out otherwise discovers at wave 3; a plan with two\nready tasks on the same file and a\nread-only role handed that file prints:"},"2250":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-366","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"It also refuses:"},"2251":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-367","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a missing task key"},"2252":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-368","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a task with no `done:` check"},"2253":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-369","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a `calls` budget over 25 (over 15 is a warning - 15 is the bar for a micro task, 25 is\nwhere the plan is wrong)"},"2254":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-370","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a dependency cycle or an unknown dependency id"},"2255":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-371","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a lane or context file that does not exist on disk"},"2256":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-372","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a task without a `## Ledger` row"},"2257":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-373","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"a first wave narrower than `slots` (compared against `min(slots, task count)`, so a small\nplan is not scolded for being small, but a coarse one is)"},"2258":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-374","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"The flags:"},"2259":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-375","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"`--slots N` overrides the plan's number"},"2260":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-376","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"`--json` prints the same findings as an object with `plan`, `slots`, `tasks`, `lanes`,\n`ready`, `errors` and `warnings` for a script to read"},"2261":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-377","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"`--no-files` skips the on-disk existence check for the briefs"},"2262":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-378","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"`--strict` makes warnings fail"},"2263":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-379","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"Exit is 0 clean, 1 with violations, 2 when the plan path is missing or unreadable."},"2264":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-380","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"The plan is a work artifact rather than the record, so its tree stays out of git:\n`notes_ensure_excludes` in `templates/common/notes-setup.sh` adds `.grok/plans/` to the\nstack's global excludes file (`~/.grok/notes/exclude`, or the `core.excludesFile` you already\npoint at) next to `notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, and `results/` is deleted once the\nchange is committed."},"2265":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-381","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cutting-the-plan-and-dispatching-the-wave","content":"The seam is pinned by `bazelisk test //tests:bats_grok_subagent_slots_test`\n(both twins' `[subagents]` block, the `coder.tf` validation, the `main.tf` derivation, and\nthe rulebook's slot-filling rule) and `bazelisk test //tests:bats_ez_micro_plan_test`\n(the checker driven over a scratch plan tree)."},"2266":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-382","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-seeded-fleet-suite","content":"The seam above is what a fan-out costs; this is who is in it. The family has its own seeding\n`coder_script`, `stack_fleet` (`templates/workspace/main.tf:753`,\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf:804`), which puts thirteen payloads in place on every start\nfrom `templates/common/`, and `//scripts:seed-dev-env` seeds the same thirteen into a\ncontributor home through the `~/.grok/agents`, `~/.grok/personas` and fleet-skill rows of\n`seed_seed_targets` (`scripts/lib/config_seed.sh:130-147`, seventeen of them; the\n`documenter.md` row at 136 sitting in the middle belongs to the advisor family below)."},"2267":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-383","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-seeded-fleet-suite","content":"The body that writes them is\n`templates/common/stack-fleet.sh.tftpl`, its own body for the same one-argument reason the\ntwo skill families have theirs\n(coder-templates.md). Thirteen is six agent\ndefinitions, three persona overlays, and four parent-side skills."},"2268":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-384","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-six-agent-definitions","content":"Each is a markdown file with YAML frontmatter whose body is the child's system prompt; the\nseeds are `templates/common/<name>.AGENT.md` and they land at\n`~/.grok/agents/<name>.md`. A definition is what fixes a role's model, tools and prompt\nmode, which a persona deliberately does not (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:77`)."},"2269":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-385","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-six-agent-definitions","content":"| Definition | What it delivers                                                                                                | Baked lane      |\n| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `explorer` | a digest of numbered claims, each with the `path:line` that proves it, plus what it looked for and did not find | `agent-explore` |","table":"table-1"},"2270":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-386","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-six-agent-definitions","content":"| Definition  | What it delivers                                                                               | Baked lane |\n| ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| `architect` | the approach, the single-writer slices, and the seam a consumer needs before it can be written | `agent`    |","table":"table-1"},"2271":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-387","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-six-agent-definitions","content":"| Definition    | What it delivers                                              | Baked lane    |\n| ------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n| `implementer` | one owned slice as a minimal patch, with its own check quoted | `agent-swarm` |","table":"table-1"},"2272":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-388","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-six-agent-definitions","content":"| Definition | What it delivers                                                              | Baked lane    |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------- |\n| `tester`   | the verdict on one `done:` line: the command run verbatim and what it printed | `agent-swarm` |","table":"table-1"},"2273":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-389","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-six-agent-definitions","content":"| Definition | What it delivers                                                                    | Baked lane     |\n| ---------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |\n| `reviewer` | findings ordered by what breaks if they stand, each with the command that proves it | `agent-review` |","table":"table-1"},"2274":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-390","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-six-agent-definitions","content":"| Definition         | What it delivers                                                                    | Baked lane     |\n| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------- |\n| `security-auditor` | secrets, injection, authorization and egress hunts, ranked by what an attacker gets | `agent-review` |","table":"table-1"},"2275":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-391","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-edit-tools-each-definition-names","content":"Within these six, only `implementer` names `search_replace`, and only `implementer` and\n`tester` name `write` at all; the other four name no edit tool and each says in its own body\nthat it edits nothing. The seventh definition `templates/common/` holds, `documenter`, is the\nadvisor family's rather than this one's and names both `search_replace` and `write`\n(`templates/common/documenter.AGENT.md:5`), because the deliverable of that role is an edit to\na page - see The five advisor commands."},"2276":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-392","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-edit-tools-each-definition-names","content":"`explorer` is the one definition here that inherits the MCP catalog, because it is the one\nwhose tools name\n`search_tool` and `use_tool`; the other five set `mcpInheritance: none`,\nthe frontmatter key the guide documents for that choice\n(`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:228`), on the seeds' own reasoning that an\ninherited catalog with no MCP tool in `tools:` would be empty anyway."},"2277":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-393","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-spawn-ceiling","content":"Three of the seven name `spawn_subagent`: `reviewer`, `security-auditor` and `tester`\n(`templates/common/reviewer.AGENT.md:5`, `templates/common/security-auditor.AGENT.md:5`,\n`templates/common/tester.AGENT.md:5`). `explorer`, `architect`, `implementer` and\n`documenter` name none of it, so a mapper cannot recurse and the wave a spawner raises one\nlevel down is made of read-only mappers. The `tools` list is what enforces that ceiling; the\nbody prose of each seed only explains it."},"2278":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-394","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-layer-names-a-definition","content":"**How one is selected.** Four layers name an agent definition, and they are not the same\nknob:"},"2279":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-395","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-layer-names-a-definition","content":"`[agent] name` in `~/.grok/config.toml` picks the definition for an interactive `grok`\nrun; a missing name falls through to `GROK_AGENT`, which takes an absolute path or a bare\nagent name (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:124-132`, `:810`)."},"2280":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-396","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-layer-names-a-definition","content":"`--agent-profile` overrides both and loads no file, so that session runs the default agent\nunless the client names one (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/15-agent-mode.md:69`)."},"2281":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-397","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-layer-names-a-definition","content":"A child's type names its definition: `spawn_subagent` offers project and user agents in\nits `subagent_type` enum, which is how a parent asks for `reviewer` by name\n(`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:69-71`). A depth-1 child that spawns resolves\nits own children through these same layers, so a grandchild reaches the reader lane\nthrough the one `grok_explore_lane` pin and needs nothing set on the spawner."},"2282":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-398","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-layer-names-a-definition","content":"`[subagents.models]` pins the lane for that type, and it is what the workspace renders -\nsee The fan-out seam."},"2283":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-399","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-layer-names-a-definition","content":"The four layers can name different lanes: the guide ranks the per-type pin as applying \"for\nany parent\" without ranking it against a definition's own `model:`\n(`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:287`). All six bake the same alias their\npin defaults to, `implementer` and `tester` among them, so a workspace that wants its\nwriters somewhere specific should set the variable rather than rely on the file.\n`/config-agents` (alias `/agents`) is where a person sees and toggles the set."},"2284":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-400","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-persona-overlays","content":"`thorough-researcher`, `senior-implementer` and `skeptical-reviewer` ship as\n`templates/common/<name>.PERSONA.toml` and land at `~/.grok/personas/<name>.toml`, where the\nfile stem is the persona name (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:93`). Discovery runs\nthe project's `.grok/personas/`, then this user directory, then the bundled catalog, and an\ninline `[subagents.personas]` table outranks all three (`:89-93`)."},"2285":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-401","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-persona-overlays","content":"A persona is an overlay only: its `instructions` reach the child as a\n`<system-reminder>` and move tone, output format and task focus, never the agent type, the\nmodel, or the tools (`:77`) - which is why none of the three names either, and why the header\ncomment in each file says so. `thorough-researcher` and `skeptical-reviewer` also declare\n`[[inputs]]` and `[[outputs]]` contracts, so a parent knows what to hand each role and what\nartifact to expect back; `senior-implementer` declares none."},"2286":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-402","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-persona-overlays","content":"The caveat worth knowing before you wire one in: Grok Build applies personas through subagent\nresolution and roles, not through a `spawn_subagent` parameter - the parent does not pass a\npersona name when it spawns a child (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:97`). A persona\nis therefore configured, not handed over: it attaches through a role or through\n`[subagents.personas]`, and `/personas` is where the catalog is read."},"2287":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-403","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-skills","content":"`templates/common/<name>.SKILL.md` for `feature-implementation`, `skeptical-review`,\n`security-audit` and `bug-hunt`, each seeded twice - `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for\nthe Coder Agents chat and `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the Grok Build CLI, the\nsecond always a `cp` of the first:"},"2288":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-404","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-skills","content":"`/feature-implementation <request-or-issue> [--slots N] [--dry-run]` takes one request\nfrom Plan Mode to a verified change: map with read-only explorers, hand the split to\n`/ez-micro-plan`, fan the writers out, close with an independent tester and reviewer."},"2289":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-405","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-skills","content":"`/skeptical-review <diff-spec|pr-number> [--focus <risk>] [--json]` takes the author's\nclaim as unverified, re-runs the exact commands the producer said proved it, attacks the\nchange from several independent angles, and reports each requirement kept or broken with\nthe line that decides it."},"2290":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-406","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-skills","content":"`/security-audit <path-or-diff> [--scope secrets|injection|authz|egress] [--json]`\ninventories sources and sinks separately, traces each source to a sink with read-only\ntracers, and ranks findings by severity with one checkable line of proof each."},"2291":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-407","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-skills","content":"`/bug-hunt <symptom-or-issue> [--bisect <good-ref>] [--no-fix]` reproduces before\ntouching anything, localises with explorers and a real bisect, tests competing hypotheses\nwith a fleet, and ships the test that fails before the fix and passes after it."},"2292":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-408","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-four-skills","content":"All four name only the local aliases - `agent-explore` for their mapping lanes,\n`agent-swarm` for their writers, `agent-review` for their skeptic - and none of them reaches\nfor a cloud model id, which is the rule in\nlitellm-routing.md rather than a preference."},"2293":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-409","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"Five more commands ride a `coder_script` of their own, `stack_advisors`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:784`, `templates/devcontainer/main.tf:835`), whose body is\n`templates/common/stack-advisors.sh.tftpl`.\nFour are advisors that each gate a different kind of work, and the fifth writes the local\nmap described at the end of this section."},"2294":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-410","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"Same contract and same two homes as the four above:\n`~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the chat and the\n`cp` twin at `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the CLI, each written only while\nabsent, and `//scripts:seed-dev-env` seeds the same copies into a contributor home\nthrough the twelve advisor rows of `seed_seed_targets`\n(`scripts/lib/config_seed.sh:148-159`)."},"2295":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-411","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"`/documenter <commit-ish|path|PR-number> [--surface docs|readme|rulebook|comments]` documents\na change that already exists: read the diff, fan read-only mappers over every documentation\nsurface on `agent-explore` to find the text the change made wrong, write one file per writer,\nthen put the result past an independent skeptic on `agent-review` that runs every command the\nnew prose names."},"2296":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-412","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"`/performance-optimizer <path|command> [--metric <name>] [--no-change]` takes a baseline with\nan instrument that is already local (`time`, `rg --stats`, Bazel `--profile`), hunts the cost\nwith read-only mappers, ranks proposals by measured cost rather than how bad the code looks,\nlands at most one change, re-measures, and ends its skeptical pass on `agent-review`."},"2297":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-413","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"`/debugger <symptom|failing-test|issue-number> [--no-fix]` is the gate against the\nguess-and-patch pass: no fix is written until a read-only `agent-explore` child has quoted the\nfailing command and its real output, and the skeptic re-runs both the red and the green run."},"2298":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-414","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"`/migrator <dep>@<version>|<api-change>` pins where a version actually lives by `path:line`,\nreads the upstream delta from a research lane rather than from memory, writes the plan and the\nrollback before touching anything, then fans the edits out and answers to a skeptic."},"2299":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-415","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"`/ez-code-index [repo-path]` writes the local map described below."},"2300":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-416","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-five-advisor-commands","content":"One new role definition ships with this family rather than with the fleet: `documenter`\n(`templates/common/documenter.AGENT.md`, landing at `~/.grok/agents/documenter.md`) is the\nlight read-and-write role `/documenter` dispatches, and it rides the same body as the skill so\na start has both or has neither."},"2301":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-417","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-local-code-index","content":"The code index is a file, not a service:"},"2302":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-418","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-local-code-index","content":"It writes `code-index.json` for a program and `code-index.md` for a person under\n`~/.grok/notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/` - the same notes directory `ez-learn` owns, outside the\nclone, carried by the owner's `grok-notes-<owner-id>` volume, so nothing new runs and\nnothing is committed."},"2303":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-419","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-local-code-index","content":"`--home DIR` points it at another profile home instead of `$HOME`, and `--json` prints\nthe whole record instead of the one `ok: N files, M symbols -> ...` line. The helper is\n`templates/common/ez-code-index.code_index.py`, seeded into a `scripts/` subdir of the same\nskill directory the way the other helper-carrying skills ship theirs. The `code-index.md`\nheader records the `sha` it indexed, so compare that line against `git rev-parse HEAD` before\ntrusting a `path:line` the map hands you; a stale map is a fresh `rg`."},"2304":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-420","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-saved-workflows","content":"`.grok/workflows/fleet-verify.js` is the same three phases as a saved workflow rather than a\nprocedure an agent follows by hand: **Map** puts two read-only explorers on `agent-explore`,\n**Implement** puts one writer on `agent-swarm`, and **Verify** puts an independent skeptic on\n`agent-review` that re-runs the producer's own command and returns a structured verdict of\n`kept`, `broken` or `mixed` with the evidence behind each finding. Its three model ids are\naliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, so `grok inspect` is the check after an alias edit - a name\nthe catalog no longer carries fails the run rather than degrading it."},"2305":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-421","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-saved-workflows","content":"Two more sit beside it in the same directory, invocable by name under the same rule that every\nid in them is a local alias:"},"2306":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-422","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-saved-workflows","content":"`implement-reviewed` (`{ goal, question, verify }`) is `fleet-verify` with the repair round\nadded: map on `agent-explore`, fan the change out on `agent-swarm`, close on `agent-review`,\nthen one bounded fix round for the findings the skeptic marked `broken` and one more skeptic\npass. Without `verify` the writer names the proof and the skeptic challenges that choice."},"2307":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-423","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-saved-workflows","content":"`debug-fix` (`{ symptom, issue, bisect }`) is the defect shape: evidence on `agent-explore`\nbefore any edit, because **Reproduce** quotes the failing command with the output it really\nproduced and no reproduction means no fix; two read-only explorers then localise on disjoint\nhypotheses (one bisecting when a known-good ref is given), one writer on `agent-swarm` lands\nthe fix with the test that is red before the change and green after, and a skeptic on\n`agent-review` re-runs both runs."},"2308":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-424","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#hooks","content":"Six payloads in `templates/common/` become one manifest and five files under `~/.grok/hooks/`:\n`evo-t1-hooks.json` plus, under `hooks/bin/`, the four guards and the deny list the command\nguard reads."},"2309":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-425","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#hooks","content":"Grok Build reads `~/.grok/hooks/*.json`, and a handler `command` that is a relative path\nresolves against the manifest's own directory and is started directly, so each script is seeded\n`0755` with the payload's `#!` line still on row 1. Every one reads one line of event JSON on\nstdin. The two PreToolUse guards always print a parseable decision there - `{\"decision\":\"allow\"}`\nor `{\"decision\":\"deny\",\"reason\":...}` - and a deny additionally exits 2; a PostToolUse handler\ncannot block at all, so it stays silent on stdout unless it has something to tell the model."},"2310":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-426","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#hooks","content":"No guard exits non-zero on its own account, so a broken one cannot wedge a session."},"2311":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-427","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-secret-guard","content":"`PreToolUse`, matcher `Edit|Write|search_replace|write` -> `bin/secret-guard.sh`: denies a\nwrite whose text carries an AWS access-key id, a GitHub token, a Slack token, a Google API\nkey, a `-----BEGIN ... PRIVATE KEY-----` block, a URL that carries both a user and a\npassword, or a credential-keyword assignment (`api_key`, `secret`, `token`, `password`, and\ntheir upper-case SDK spellings) whose value carries a digit."},"2312":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-428","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-secret-guard","content":"It sweeps the fields a write puts text into - the flat `new_string`, `content`, `contents`,\n  `command` and `text`, and\n  `new_string`, `new_text`, `content`, `contents` and `text` inside each entry of a batched\n  `edits` array - one level deep and no deeper, so an unknown nesting is not swept and the\n  truncation check is what covers it."},"2313":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-429","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-secret-guard","content":"It leaves the read-side `old_string`/`old_text` alone, so a secret an older commit wrote\n  cannot convict every later edit beside it."},"2314":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-430","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-secret-guard","content":"A value that names the environment instead of carrying a secret is the correct pattern, not a\n  hit, so\n  `os.environ/X`, `os.environ[\"X\"]`, `process.env.X` and `getenv(\"X\")` are folded to one short\n  token before the scan. The deny reason names the pattern\n  class and never the matched text."},"2315":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-431","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-secret-guard","content":"It fails open on an event it cannot parse, and denies when\n  the runner reports `toolInputTruncated`, because that is text the scan never saw\n  (`templates/common/hooks.secret-guard.sh`)."},"2316":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-432","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-command-guard","content":"`PreToolUse`, matcher `Bash|run_terminal_command` -> `bin/dangerous-command-guard.sh`: tests\n`toolInput.command` against the list beside itself and denies, naming the file, the line\nnumber and the pattern that matched."},"2317":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-433","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-post-edit-formatter","content":"`PostToolUse`, matcher `Edit|Write|search_replace|write` -> `bin/post-edit-format.sh`: the\nformatters the golden image already ships, at the same invocations `//:fix` uses for shell and\nBazel files - `shfmt -w -s -i 2 -ci` for `.sh`, `buildifier -mode=fix` for `BUILD`,\n`BUILD.bazel`, `MODULE.bazel` and `.bzl` - plus `ruff format --quiet` for `.py`. It refuses:"},"2318":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-434","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-post-edit-formatter","content":"anything outside `CLAUDE_PROJECT_DIR`, addressed directly or reached through a link (the\ncontainment test runs again on the resolved path)"},"2319":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-435","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-post-edit-formatter","content":"anything under `.git/`, `bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `bazel-testlogs/`, `node_modules/` or\n`__pycache__/`, `.env` and `.env.*`"},"2320":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-436","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-post-edit-formatter","content":"any path containing `/.grok/`, `/.agents/` or `/.coder/`"},"2321":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-437","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-post-edit-formatter","content":"It only ever answers, never blocks: a rewrite is reported as `additionalContext`, and silence\nmeans nothing changed or the formatter is not on PATH."},"2322":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-438","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-selective-test-runner","content":"`PostToolUse`, matcher `Edit|Write|search_replace|write` -> `bin/selective-test-runner.sh`:\n**off unless `EVO_T1_HOOK_TESTS=1`**. When it is on, the hook derives one Bazel label -\n`//tests:bats_<stem>_test` straight from a `tests/bats/<stem>.bats` path, otherwise the first\nlabel `bazelisk run //scripts:bazel-map -- tests-for <path>` prints - and starts\n`bazelisk test <label>` detached with `nohup`, logging to\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/evo-t1-hook-tests/<sanitized-label>.log`."},"2323":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-439","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-selective-test-runner","content":"It returns one context line naming the label and that log; it never runs a test inline, so the\nedit turn does not wait on the suite."},"2324":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-440","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#extending-the-deny-list","content":"The deny list is the extension point. `~/.grok/hooks/bin/deny-patterns.txt` holds one extended\nregex per line, `#` starts a comment and blank lines are ignored, so an operator forbids one\nmore command by appending one line - and because there is no allow list, deleting a line is the\nonly way to permit something again."},"2325":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-441","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#extending-the-deny-list","content":"That copy is stack-owned: the boot rewrites it from\n`templates/common/hooks.deny-patterns.txt` while its `managed-by:` marker stands, so a line\nappended in a workspace is gone at the next start."},"2326":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-442","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#extending-the-deny-list","content":"Land the rule in the seed (with the Bats case\nin `tests/bats/stack-hooks.bats` that denies it), or delete the marker from that one file to keep\na copy of your own, which the boot then leaves alone. A line that does not compile as an ERE is\nskipped with a\nsingle note on stderr rather than blocking every shell call, which is why the file's own header\ncarries the one-line compile check to run before you commit a rule:\n`LC_ALL=C grep -E -q -- '<pattern>' </dev/null`."},"2327":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-443","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"Three limits are worth knowing before you trust a clean scan, and each is a choice rather than\nan oversight."},"2328":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-444","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"The secret guard's generic assignment scan requires a digit on the matched line, which is\nwhat keeps a variable name from reading as a credential."},"2329":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-445","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"The cost runs in both directions: `api_key = XyZaBcDeFgHiJk` (a name the list\ncarries, no digit) walks through, and so does\n`github_token_string = Abcd1234EFGH5678` (a digit, a name the list does not carry)."},"2330":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-446","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"Only the five known-format scans (AWS, GitHub, Slack, Google, private-key block)\nplus the credential-bearing URL are exact, and they run on the text before the\nenvironment-reference fold, so a vendor key pasted inside `getenv(\"...\")` still\ndenies."},"2331":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-447","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"What the fold does excuse is entropy sitting in the name position of a reference -\n`os.environ/KEY_XyZaBc12345678` - because that position is where the correct\npattern lives and the guard's whole job is to keep it writable."},"2332":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-448","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"A missed shape is a line in `hooks.secret-guard.sh`, not a reason to trust the ones\nit does catch."},"2333":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-449","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"The command guard matches the whole command string, case-sensitively and byte-wise, so a\ncommand that *quotes* a denied form is refused the same way a real one is: `awk '/dd if=/'\nnotes.txt` denies, and so does a commit message that names `rm -rf` followed by a space."},"2334":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-450","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"Stripping quoted regions before matching would clear those and would catch a quoted dot\n(`find \".\" -type f -delete`) for free, and it would equally blind the guard to `sh -c \"...\"`\nand `bash -c \"...\"`, the indirection a command actually travels through most - so the string\nis read as written, and the few rules that have to see inside a quote spell the quote\nthemselves."},"2335":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-451","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"The remedy for the rest is to rewrite the quoted sentence; the stack's own\n`Bash(rm -rf *)` permission deny behaves the same way for the same reason."},"2336":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-452","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#three-limits-of-a-clean-scan","content":"The deny list is a floor, not a fence. It catches the forms this repo has actually measured,\nand a novel destructive command walks straight through it. Nothing in a hook reads the state\nthe command will act on, which is why \"confirm before an action that is hard to undo\" stays a\nrulebook rule that no pattern can replace."},"2337":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-453","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#who-seeds-the-hooks","content":"Two things seed them, and both are wanted. `coder_script \"stack_hooks\"`\n(`templates/workspace/main.tf:810`, `templates/devcontainer/main.tf:861`) runs\n`templates/common/stack-hooks.sh.tftpl` on every workspace start (`run_on_start`, gated on\n`data.coder_workspace.me.start_count`), and `scripts/lib/config_seed.sh:160-165` carries the\nsame six targets for a host tree or a workspace that missed a boot."},"2338":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-454","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#who-seeds-the-hooks","content":"This family is **marker-aware** rather than absent-only like the skills: a copy that still\ncarries the stack marker - the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key in the manifest, which has to stay\nparseable JSON, and the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` comment in the shell files - is\nrewritten on every start."},"2339":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-455","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#who-seeds-the-hooks","content":"That is deliberate, because a guard that drifted from the seed is a silent behaviour change:\nthese scripts decide what a session may run and what it may write. Delete the marker and the\ncopy is yours, and the boot leaves it alone."},"2340":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-456","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rule-the-suite-exists-to-enforce","content":"Every multi-file change ends with an independent review panel that wrote none of it, on\n`agent-review`, before anything is reported done: three angles sent in one turn\n(requirements, proof, adversary), each owning no file, where one skeptic over all three\nangles is enough for a single-file fix."},"2341":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-457","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rule-the-suite-exists-to-enforce","content":"The rulebook states it as a requirement, not a suggestion\n(`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, \"Mandatory independent review\"),\nand its reasoning is one sentence: a delegated result is a claim, and a review that trusts\nthe claim rather than the artifact is not a review."},"2342":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-458","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-rule-the-suite-exists-to-enforce","content":"Each panelist re-runs the producers'\n`done:` commands rather than reading their reports of them, which is why `tester`,\n`reviewer` and `security-auditor` exist as separate definitions whose spawn tool reaches\nread-only mappers, rather than as instructions tacked onto the writer."},"2343":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-459","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-seeded-file-becomes-yours","content":"The family splits by home, and the split is deliberate:"},"2344":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-460","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-seeded-file-becomes-yours","content":"The fleet's six `~/.grok/agents/*.md` definitions and its three\n`~/.grok/personas/*.toml` overlays are **marker-aware**, the way\n`~/.grok/AGENTS.md` is: a copy that still carries `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack` is\nstack-owned and gets the current seed text on every start, so a stale role definition\ncannot silently change how every child in every workspace behaves. Delete the marker line\nand the file is the person's - the boot leaves a marker-free copy alone."},"2345":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-461","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-seeded-file-becomes-yours","content":"`~/.grok/hooks/` is **marker-aware** on the same rule, with two spellings because its two\nkinds of file have two readers: the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` key, which is the only marker\nspelling that leaves the manifest parseable JSON, and the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack`\ncomment in the four scripts and the deny list. A guard that drifted from the seed is a\nbehaviour change nobody sees, so this family gets the refresh rather than the first-write\nrule. See Hooks."},"2346":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-462","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-seeded-file-becomes-yours","content":"The skill copies are **absent-only**, like the `ez-` families: a `SKILL.md` that\nalready exists belongs to the user and is never rewritten - the fleet's eight, and the\nadvisor family's twelve, which are five `SKILL.md`s plus `code_index.py` in each of the two\nhomes. The advisor family's `~/.grok/agents/documenter.md` rides that absent-only contract\nwith the skill it serves rather than the refresh the fleet's six keep, on the body's own\nreasoning that a stale piece of advice misleads one run while a stale guard decides what a\nsession may do."},"2347":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-463","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-seeded-file-becomes-yours","content":"A managed path that is a **symlink** is left alone in both families. The marker test\nreads the file a path points at, so writing through a link would replace a file the\nstack does not own - a link aimed at `~/.grok/AGENTS.md` would have the seed overwrite\nthe rulebook. A link a person planted stays theirs, and one symlinked root stops the\nseed before its first write."},"2348":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-464","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-a-seeded-file-becomes-yours","content":"Either way the fix is the seed under `templates/common/` and a re-materialize, never a patch\nto the copy that is running - the same rule the two skill families above live by\n(the three stack-seeded skill commands)."},"2349":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-465","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-state-shared-state-per-workspace-config","content":"Every workspace gets its own `/home/coder` volume, and `~/.grok` sits on it as an\nordinary directory, `config.toml` included."},"2350":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-466","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#grok-build-state-shared-state-per-workspace-config","content":"What is shared is **state**: three nested mounts inside that home volume, one per concern,\neach a Docker volume named for the owner (`grok-notes-<owner-id>`,\n`grok-memory-<owner-id>`, `grok-sessions-<owner-id>`), referenced by name rather than\ndeclared as a Terraform resource, which every workspace of that owner mounts read-write at\nits own path **while that workspace is in `shared` mode**."},"2351":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-467","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"`shared` (the default here): the three state directories ride their owner's\nvolumes, so the notes, the memory and the sessions are the same files in every\nworkspace that account owns, and they survive a rebuild. `config.toml` is not part\nof that share - each workspace renders its own."},"2352":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-468","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"`private`: no per-owner volume at all. The notes, the memory and the sessions live\non that workspace's own home volume, differ from its siblings, and die with the\nworkspace. `config.toml` is per workspace either way."},"2353":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-469","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"Set in two places: the template variable `grok_profile_mode`\n(`GROK_PROFILE_MODE=shared|private` in `.env`, forwarded by `push-template.sh` as\n`--var grok_profile_mode`), and the create form's per-workspace **Grok profile**\nfield, which defaults to the variable. One workspace can therefore sit outside the\nshared state while its siblings stay inside it."},"2354":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-470","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-modes","content":"Changing the mode needs a **rebuild** - the three nested mounts either exist or they\ndo not. A workspace that goes from `shared` to `private` starts on whatever those\nthree directories already hold on its own home volume: nothing is copied, and the\nowner's volumes are left alone."},"2355":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-471","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"In `shared` mode, three directories: `notes/` (the canonical copy of each\nrepository's private scratch `notes.md`), `memory/`, and `sessions/` - one volume\neach, mounted at `~/.grok/notes`, `~/.grok/memory` and `~/.grok/sessions`. Nothing\nelse under `~/.grok` is shared: `config.toml`, `skills/`, the MCP warm caches and a CLI\nyou installed yourself under `~/.grok/bin` are regular files on this workspace's own\nhome volume, and those die with the workspace."},"2356":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-472","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"The CLI's own skill directories,\n`skills/` and `bundled/`, are never created here without an x.ai sign-in; the stack's\nskill seeds live under `~/.coder/skills` and `~/.agents/skills`, outside this tree."},"2357":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-473","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-is-shared","content":"The whole-profile volume this template used to mount over all of `~/.grok`,\n`grok-profile-<owner-id>`, is still mounted in `shared` mode, but **read-only** and at\n`~/.grok-legacy`, purely so the first start after that split can copy its `notes/`,\n`memory/` and `sessions/` across once: `notes_hoist_legacy` in\n`templates/common/notes-setup.sh` takes a concern only when the destination is missing\nor empty, and nothing reads that mount afterwards. A later `chore:` removes it."},"2358":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-474","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-notes-file-and-its-watcher","content":"`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/notes.md` is keyed per git remote, so every clone a\nworkspace holds — the primary and each additional repository — has its own file. What you\nedit is the regular file at the clone root: that is what the editor, the terminal and a\nnested dev container all see, since the inner container mounts the clone bind and has\nits own `~/.grok`."},"2359":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-475","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-notes-file-and-its-watcher","content":"The copy runs both ways, and not only at boot."},"2360":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-476","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-notes-file-and-its-watcher","content":"Startup copies the live file here and restores it when a recreate leaves the clone with\nnothing or with only the seeded stub line, and then leaves **one background watcher per\ncontainer** that repeats the back-copy about every 30 seconds (`NOTES_SYNC_INTERVAL`, in\nseconds) over every clone under `$HOME/workspace`; the devcontainer template watches its\n`/srv/coder-devcontainers` bind root instead, since those clones sit there rather than\nunder the workspace home."},"2361":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-477","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-notes-file-and-its-watcher","content":"The watcher is single-instance behind a non-blocking `flock` on\n`$HOME/.notes-sync.lock`, in the container's own home rather than the shared volume,\nbecause each of the owner's workspaces needs its own."},"2362":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-478","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-recreate-keeps","content":"That is what makes \"survives workspace recreate\" true rather than a hope: an edit is on the\nvolume within roughly one interval, so a recreate no longer waits on someone restarting the\nworkspace to pick up what was typed since the last boot. What is not promised is the tail"},"2363":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-479","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-recreate-keeps","content":"a file written in the last two seconds is left for the next tick, because copying it\nwhole could archive a half-saved file."},"2364":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-480","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-recreate-keeps","content":"Going the other way, two guards stop a recreate from erasing notes: a live file that is\nempty or still holds only the seeded stub never replaces\na canonical copy carrying real notes, and a real canonical copy restores over such a stub\nin a clone."},"2365":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-481","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-a-recreate-keeps","content":"Before any substantive canonical copy is replaced it is archived as\n`notes/<host>/<owner>/<repo>/backups/notes-YYYYmmdd-HHMMSS.md`, keeping the newest\n`NOTES_BACKUP_KEEP` (10 by default), and that directory is where an operator recovers notes\nafter an accident. `grok_profile_mode=private` mounts no shared volume, so those notes die\nwith that workspace; `shared` is the default. Like the memory tree, the last workspace\nstarted for a given user and repository wins."},"2366":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-482","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-boot-writes","content":"Two things about that start are worth knowing before you go looking for a vanished\nsetting. Boot **writes `git config --global core.excludesFile`** — but only when you\nhave not set it yourself. Left unset it points at `~/.grok/notes/exclude`, which lists\n`notes.md` and `.scratch-notes.md`, so from then on *every* git repository in that\nworkspace ignores both names at any depth; if you already set the option, those two\nlines are appended to the file it names and nothing else about it is touched."},"2367":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-483","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-boot-writes","content":"Boot also drops the same two lines into each clone's own `.git/info/exclude`, which is the\nlayer that works even with no global config at all."},"2368":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-484","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-boot-writes","content":"And when a repository tracks a `notes.md`\nof its own, the scratch file is written as `.scratch-notes.md` beside it instead — your\nclone is never asked to delete somebody else's committed file."},"2369":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-485","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-out-of-band-session-archive","content":"Beside the three state mounts there is one more volume, and it shares nothing with\nthem: `grok-session-archive`, mounted read-write at `/var/lib/grok-session-archive`.\nIt is deliberately outside `~/.grok`, and it is host-wide rather than per-owner - every\nworkspace on that daemon mounts the same volume, each writing under its own\n`workspaces/<hostname>/` leaf."},"2370":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-486","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-out-of-band-session-archive","content":"Like the per-owner volumes it is a plain string name in `main.tf`, not a\n`docker_volume` resource, so Docker creates it on the first container start that\nnames it and reuses it afterwards."},"2371":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-487","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-archive-is-written","content":"The copy is made by a background `rsync` that stage 9 of `startup.sh.tftpl` starts\nand leaves running for the life of the container: every 180 seconds it mirrors\n`~/.grok/sessions/` into `workspaces/<leaf>/`, skipping `*.tmp` and `*.lock`. It\nruns without `--delete`, so a session pruned from the live tree keeps its archived\ncopy. `flock -n` on a per-leaf lock file in the volume root is what keeps the stage\nidempotent: Coder re-runs the startup body on every start, and a second loop over the\nsame leaf would only double the write rate."},"2372":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-488","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-archive-is-written","content":"The stage is best-effort: it warns and\ncarries on when the volume root or the leaf is not writable, or when `flock` is\nmissing, and re-tests for the sessions directory each tick because the CLI creates\nthat directory on its first run, which can be long after boot."},"2373":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-489","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-archive-is-written","content":"The leaf is the container hostname with anything outside `[A-Za-z0-9._-]` folded to\n`-`, so a name carrying a slash cannot write outside `workspaces/` and an empty one\ncannot collapse every workspace into a single mirror. The sanctioned reader accepts\nexactly that character set, so the write side and the read side agree."},"2374":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-490","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#how-the-archive-is-written","content":"A fresh named volume arrives `root:root` while the agent runs as `coder`, so the\nstage chowns the mount point to the home owner first, exactly as the `~/.grok` and\n`~/.shared` volumes above do."},"2375":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-491","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#who-may-read-the-archive","content":"**Nothing reads it back.** `~/.grok/sessions` stays the only session state the CLI,\nthe rulebook, and Coder's session management know about; no config key and no startup\nlogic points at the archive, and the hooks deny list\n(`templates/common/hooks.deny-patterns.txt`) refuses any agent command that names the\npath, the volume, or a globbed form of either. The point is a copy an operator can\ninspect later, from the host, after the workspace that made it is gone."},"2376":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-492","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#who-may-read-the-archive","content":"Read it with `bazelisk run //scripts:session-archive -- list`. Every verb mounts the\nvolume read-only, so the reader cannot write it either, and there is no pruning verb:\nthe volume grows until an operator removes it."},"2377":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-493","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#who-may-read-the-archive","content":"The raw `docker run -v <volume>:ro` and\n`docker volume rm <volume>` forms are host-shell commands, and the same deny list that\nprotects the archive from a workspace agent refuses them there - run them in a host\nshell, not from an agent prompt."},"2378":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-494","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#who-may-read-the-archive","content":"One consequence of the volume being host-wide rather than per-owner: the leaf is named\nfor the workspace, and Coder workspace names are unique per organization. Two owners\nwho both call a workspace `dev` mirror into the same `workspaces/dev`, and every\nworkspace user is uid 1000, so nothing on the filesystem separates them inside the\nvolume. That is acceptable for the archive's only purpose - an operator reading their\nown box's history - and is why no agent is ever given a route to it. Give the leaf an\nowner component if a box ever holds more than one trusted operator's sessions."},"2379":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-495","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"Keyed by **owner id**, never host-wide and never cross-user: another user's\nworkspace mounts a differently named volume, and a stop or start of a *different\nuser's* workspace never sees the volume at all."},"2380":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-496","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"The memory and index files are SQLite, opened live by every workspace holding the\nmount, so two workspaces writing memory at once can race. *Roughly in sync* is the\nbar, not transactional sharing."},"2381":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-497","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"Two workspaces of one owner that both hold a clone of the *same* repository each run a\nnotes watcher over the same canonical path, so there too the last write wins, tick by\ntick. The stub and empty guards and the `backups/` archive are what bound the damage a\nlosing tick can do; they do not merge two diverging files."},"2382":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-498","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#caveats-honestly","content":"There is no longer one `config.toml` to set once for a whole account: each\nworkspace renders its own from the template on start. A hand edit there lasts until\n**that** workspace restarts and touches no other workspace, and the next start after\na template re-push brings the file back from the new template."},"2383":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-499","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#search-lane-and-mcp-servers","content":"`searxng` (SearXNG metasearch, host port 8181) is the search backend the stack runs\nfor itself: keyless engines only, no key, no account, no request logging. A\nworkspace reaches it through the host gateway, so the workspace networks stay\nuntouched."},"2384":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-500","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#why-the-native-search-tool-is-parked","content":"It is **not** a model transport, and the native tool is parked: both twins render\n`disable_web_search = true` at top level, so a session starts with no `web_search`\ntool at all. Measured 2026-10-25: no model transport this box ships serves that\ntool type."},"2385":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-501","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#why-the-native-search-tool-is-parked","content":"The `agent` group is LiteLLM in front of the Spark boxes\n  (`openai/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next`), and it refuses the request before anything is\n  fetched - `tools of type 'web_search' are not supported: this server runs function\n  tools only`, HTTP 400."},"2386":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-502","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#why-the-native-search-tool-is-parked","content":"`supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it, because the client-side\n  fallback still hands the model alias a `web_search`-typed tool, which is the exact\n  shape that endpoint rejects. The fallback lane fails too: it is the `coder` alias,\n  Ollama `qwen2.5-coder:32b`, and that model is not pulled on the host."},"2387":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-503","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#why-the-native-search-tool-is-parked","content":"So `web_search = \"agent\"` and `supports_backend_search = false` stay as\n  they are - the role stays filled so re-enabling is a one-key flip - but while the\n  key reads `true` nothing dials it."},"2388":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-504","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-mcp-search-lane-and-its-probe","content":"The `searxng` MCP stdio server is what answers instead, and it is the search lane\nrather than a fallback: both templates render that block gated on `search_mcp`,\nwhich defaults to true. It dials `$SEARXNG_URL` (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:8181`), the same key `main.tf` puts in the agent\nenvironment."},"2389":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-505","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-mcp-search-lane-and-its-probe","content":"It carries nine tools - `search`, `image_search`, `news_search`,\n`video_search`, `music_search`, `paper_search`, `fetch_content`, `autocomplete`,\n`list_engines` - and an agent's two workhorses are the first and the seventh:\n`search` for anything that is not a library question, `fetch_content` for a URL\nalready in hand. Measure the self-hosted lane with:"},"2390":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-506","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-mcp-search-lane-and-its-probe","content":"An empty result page and \"no answer\" are different answers, which is why the probe\n  reports which one it got."},"2391":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-507","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-mcp-search-lane-and-its-probe","content":"It also names every engine the instance has parked and\n  the reason its backend gave (`parked by its backend: google: access denied`),\n  read out of the reply body whether or not that reply found results - an instance\n  running on half its engines is visible there before it starts answering empty."},"2392":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-508","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-mcp-search-lane-and-its-probe","content":"A park is temporary: SearXNG holds an engine out for `search.suspended_times`\n  seconds after it refuses, so re-run before writing one off."},"2393":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-509","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-mcp-search-lane-and-its-probe","content":"`--json` adds `engines_parked`. It checks the service hop; `grok mcp doctor searxng`\n  inside the workspace checks the MCP hop."},"2394":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-510","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-mcp-search-lane-and-its-probe","content":"The binary it launches\n  (`/opt/npm-global/bin/searxng-mcp-server`) is baked into the golden image, so a\n  probe that answers with results plus a failed handshake is a server, URL or port\n  problem, not a missing package."},"2395":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-511","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#turning-native-search-back-on","content":"To put native search back on a transport that really serves the tool: flip the key\nto `false` in the seed - both `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/`'s copy - and re-push, which applies it fleet-wide. For one\nworkspace without a push, the create form's **Grok config extras** field appends\nfree-form TOML verbatim to that workspace's file. Either way start a new session,\nand expect the same 400 back if the endpoint still answers function tools only."},"2396":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-512","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"**MCP servers: the default render declares three - `github`, `context7` and the\ngated `searxng` server, which is the search lane itself.** Each is a boolean gate in\nthe template, so one env flag parks exactly one block and a template re-push applies\nit fleet-wide: `GITHUB_MCP=false`, `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`, `SEARCH_MCP=false`."},"2397":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-513","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"The `searxng` block names the pre-installed `searxng-mcp-server` binary (pinned in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image), is gated on\n`search_mcp` (default true) and dials the service above through `SEARXNG_URL`."},"2398":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-514","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"That URL is one of six keys the render sets in `[mcp_servers.searxng.env]`; the\nothers pin the bridge's own tuning rather than taking its compiled-in defaults:"},"2399":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-515","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"`SEARXNG_TIMEOUT_MS` (20s, above the instance's per-engine budget and equal to the\nprobe's `--timeout`)"},"2400":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-516","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"`SEARXNG_CACHE_TTL_MS` (15 minutes, which switches on the bounded 128-entry response\ncache that ships disabled, so a repeated query stops re-dialling backends that already\nrefused this instance)"},"2401":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-517","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"`SEARXNG_MAX_RESULTS` (a ceiling that keeps one search inside the MCP output cap - on the\npinned 1.0.77 CLI that cap rides `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`, because `grok inspect`\nstill answers `[mcp] — unrecognized config key` and never applies it)"},"2402":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-518","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"`SEARXNG_DEFAULT_SAFESEARCH` (mirrors `search.safe_search` in `searxng/settings.yml`)"},"2403":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-519","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-declared-mcp-servers","content":"`SEARXNG_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE` (`all`, so an image bump cannot quietly start geo-filtering\nevery agent search)"},"2404":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-520","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-offline-stdio-servers-stay-undeclared","content":"The five offline stdio servers (`filesystem`, `memory`, `sequential-thinking`,\n`git` and `time`) stay pre-installed in the image (`npm -g` for the first\nthree, `uv tool` for the Python two), but the rendered `config.toml` names\nnone of them: the session traces collected on this machine called none of\ntheir tools, and a declared-but-unused server still costs every session its\nhandshake time and tool schemas. Declare one by hand and check a stubborn\nserver with `grok mcp doctor <name>` inside a workspace."},"2405":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-521","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-github-server","content":"The `github` server (`https://api.githubcopilot.com/mcp/`) renders by default even\nthough it needs a token and outbound network: with GitHub tools on, the agent can\ndrive the repository it is working in, and the cheaper failure is the block that\nrenders with no credential and pays one failed handshake per session. `github_mcp`\n(default true) is the gate; `$GH_TOKEN` comes from Coder External Auth, never from a\nPAT."},"2406":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-522","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-context7-server-and-its-cache","content":"The `context7` server (library and API docs) also renders by default, and is the\nonly cached one. `context7_mcp` (default true) is its gate, and the block it\nrenders runs `mcp-cache-proxy` in front of the official `context7-mcp` - two packages\npinned in `images/dev/tool-versions.env` and baked into the golden image, so parking\nor restoring it is a flag plus a template push, while changing the packages\nthemselves takes an image rebuild."},"2407":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-523","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-context7-server-and-its-cache","content":"The proxy keeps its SQLite cache under\n  `~/.grok/mcp-cache-proxy/`, which sits on this workspace's own\n  home volume, so each workspace warms its own cache, and the cache TTL is 12 h: the first\n  lookup of a\n  library reaches the Context7 API and needs network, while an identical lookup inside\n  the TTL is answered locally."},"2408":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-524","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-context7-server-and-its-cache","content":"`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is optional and the lane does not\n  depend on it: an empty value sends no `Authorization` header at all, so the server\n  handshakes and answers at the public rate limit, with private libraries the only\n  thing out of reach. A key in `.env` plus a re-push fills the env."},"2409":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-525","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-context7-server-and-its-cache","content":"What *parks* the\n  lane on purpose is `CONTEXT7_MCP=false` in `.env` (relayed by `push-template.sh`) or\n  `--var context7_mcp=false` on a template push; a `[mcp_servers.context7]` block\n  missing from a render that got neither of those is a defect, not a park. The lookup\n  order is fixed - `resolve-library-id` first, then `query-docs` on the id it returns -\n  and `search_tool` lists the live tool names rather than a hardcoded list."},"2410":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-526","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-context7-server-and-its-cache","content":"Prove it\n  with `grok mcp doctor context7` inside a workspace."},"2411":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-527","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#proving-the-lanes-with-mcp-check","content":"Prove all three at once with `bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check`: it reads the\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`, reports whether each lane is declared, whether its\n`command=` binary is present, whether the lane's credential and cache config are\nthere, and what each server's handshake says. A binary the box cannot find is image\ndrift (the running `evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake), and the check prints the\npinned `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command that restores it."},"2412":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-528","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-failure-shapes","content":"A lane an agent needs fails in exactly three shapes, and that check tells them\napart, so run it before editing anything:"},"2413":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-529","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-failure-shapes","content":"**The block is missing from the rendered config.** That is a failure, not a\nwarning: the default render declares the lane, so something took it away - a push\npassed its gate `false`, the workspace runs an older template, or the file lost\nits stack marker and nothing re-renders it."},"2414":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-530","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-failure-shapes","content":"Remedy: clear the gate and re-push, then re-materialize the file with\n`bazelisk run //scripts:refresh-configs -- --container coder-<owner>-<ws>` from\nthe stack host, or `bazelisk run //scripts:seed-dev-env -- --home <dir>` for a\nhost home."},"2415":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-531","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-failure-shapes","content":"**The binary is missing from the golden image.** A warning: the running\n`evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake. Remedy: rebuild the image and recreate the\nworkspace, or run the `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command the check prints\nto install the pinned package into the running box (pins in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."},"2416":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-532","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-failure-shapes","content":"**The credential is missing.** Also a warning, and per lane it means something\ndifferent. `github` with `$GH_TOKEN` empty is a real gap: connect GitHub External\nAuth (the **GitHub reauth** app) and start a new session. An empty\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is not a gap - see the key's paragraph above, the lane answers\nkeyless at the public rate limit."},"2417":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-533","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-rulebook-tells-the-agent","content":"What the agent is told about all this lives in `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, which\n`coder_script.agents_seed` renders from `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` into a\nbody of its own (see \"The one-argument ceiling\" in\ncoder-templates.md). Two sections cover\nthe lanes. **Research\nlanes** makes a lookup part of the normal loop rather than a last resort, with the\nrouting fixed by question type:"},"2418":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-534","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-rulebook-tells-the-agent","content":"a library, framework, CLI or setup question goes to `context7` (resolve the library id\nfirst, then query docs on that id)"},"2419":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-535","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-rulebook-tells-the-agent","content":"anything else on the web goes to the `searxng` `search` tool"},"2420":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-536","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-rulebook-tells-the-agent","content":"a URL already in hand goes to `searxng`'s `fetch_content`, because Grok's native\n`web_fetch` is unavailable here too - `features.web_fetch` is on, but on a non-xAI\nendpoint that tool only runs through `toolset.web_fetch.proxy_endpoint`, and this stack\nsets none"},"2421":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-537","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-rulebook-tells-the-agent","content":"It also states that native `web_search` is parked so nobody spends a turn rediscovering\nthe 400.\n**Tool output stays out of the parent context** makes that\ntool use delegated work: the child returns a digest - numbered findings, each one\nclaim, one source URL and at most two lines of support, no raw result dumps,\nconfidence stated - and keeps the raw pages to itself."},"2422":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-538","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cline-roo-and-the-shared-editor-settings","content":"Cline and Roo/Kilo are pre-wired to the LiteLLM proxy (endpoint from `litellm_url`,\ndefault `http://host.docker.internal:4000/v1`; model alias `agent`, with\n`agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`/`agent-xhigh-fast`/`agent-swarm`/`agent-swarm-fast`/`coder`/`coder-fast`/`chat`\nlisted in `__evoT1StackModels`)."},"2423":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-539","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cline-roo-and-the-shared-editor-settings","content":"The seed is\n`settings.json.tftpl`, copied onto the per-owner volume\n`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` (mounted at `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`) only\nwhile that file is absent, where it stays as the shared overlay. What code-server\nactually reads, `~/.local/share/code-server/User/settings.json`, is a real file on\nthis workspace's own home volume, which `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl`\nrewrites on every start in three passes:"},"2424":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-540","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cline-roo-and-the-shared-editor-settings","content":"the per-workspace render as the base"},"2425":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-541","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cline-roo-and-the-shared-editor-settings","content":"Every key of that overlay on top so an API key entered once still wins"},"2426":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-542","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cline-roo-and-the-shared-editor-settings","content":"`workbench.colorTheme` re-asserted from the render last, so a theme put in the\nshared overlay loses to this workspace's own theme (the **Editor colour theme** field,\n`vscode_color_theme`, default `Default Dark Modern`)"},"2427":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-543","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cline-roo-and-the-shared-editor-settings","content":"Startup chowns that volume to the home owner before the first write (empty named volumes\nare `root:root` 755)."},"2428":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-544","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#cline-roo-and-the-shared-editor-settings","content":"Edits to the overlay stick, and every workspace that owner has gets them layered over its\nown render; delete it to copy the template in again. From the\nhost it is the volume's `settings.json`:"},"2429":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-545","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-built-dev-container-bakes-the-seed","content":"A built dev container sees neither the volume nor that overlay, so it is the one\nplace the seed is **baked** rather than layered: the inlined\n`coder_script.code_server` fork bakes a base64 copy of it and writes it over the\ndev container's private `User/settings.json` (or merges over a copy that survived a\nrebuild, when `jq` is on PATH; without `jq` the existing file is kept)."},"2430":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-546","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-built-dev-container-bakes-the-seed","content":"Edits inside a built dev container therefore survive neither a rebuild nor a look at\nthe shared file. Everywhere else no settings map is passed at all, because a merge\nwould replace the symlink."},"2431":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-547","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#installing-the-extensions-by-hand","content":"Installing Cline, Roo, the Red Hat YAML extension and Material Icon Theme is a\nmanual step in the Extensions panel (Open VSX by default). If an extension build\nnames its settings differently, set the OpenAI-compatible endpoint and key in its\nUI."},"2432":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-548","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#coder-agents-control-plane","content":"Coder v2.36.6 ships *Coder Agents*: a coding agent that lives in the Coder **server** -\na chat UI and an API beside your workspaces, not inside one. A chat runs its tools\n(`read_file`, `edit_files`, `execute`, `spawn_agent`) against a workspace over that\nworkspace's agent connection, and consults two pieces of per-workspace context first:\nan **instructions** file and any **skills** it discovers. The agent itself is upstream\nCoder, not a home-grown agent."},"2433":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-549","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"The chat agent is compiled into coderd unconditionally."},"2434":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-550","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"`CODER_EXPERIMENTS` in `.env` (empty by default since 2026-10-08) has never gated\nanything about it: the old `agents` default was not a recognized experiment name,\nand the server logged `ignoring unknown experiment` and ignored it on every boot."},"2435":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-551","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"`workspace_external_agent`, the entitlement the docs name, gates *external* agents\n(Claude Code, Codex, ...), not this one, and nothing here needs it."},"2436":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-552","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#it-is-always-on","content":"If you know a real experiment you want (`chat-advisor`, `chat-virtual-desktop`,\n...), list it in `.env` and restart the coder service. Empty means no experiments\non."},"2437":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-553","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-seeder-command","content":"Scripted, idempotent, re-runnable, and already started from `prod-stack` /\n`dev-stack` `up` (`--apply --wait`, default 1800 s). Registering the first account\nis the only operator step: the seeder continues once an owner exists, and the Agents\npage is usable with no extra Coder UI setup. Print the plan:"},"2438":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-554","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-seeder-command","content":"Apply it (or re-run after a skip):"},"2439":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-555","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-seeder-command","content":"`--help` prints the same two commands with every flag and every `CODER_AGENTS_*`\noverride, which is the list to reach for from a shell."},"2440":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-556","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-config-is-the-default","content":"The deployment default follows the health gate. `agent` is registered as the default,\nand when LiteLLM reports no healthy deployment behind the Spark alias the Arc `chat`\nconfig takes the default in its place. That matters because a chat opened without an\nexplicit model pick has only the default to resolve to, so a stack that comes up while\nthe Spark lane is dark still gets an Agents chat that answers."},"2441":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-557","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-config-is-the-default","content":"An existing config is left as it stands, since this API surface has no PATCH: the\nscript names the Admin\nscreen to correct it when a stored row drifted."},"2442":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-558","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-config-is-the-default","content":"The closing summary counts how many of the four auxiliary lanes carry a pin, and a lane\nleft without one prints a `warning:` that names the lane."},"2443":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-559","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-wait-window-and-the-seeder-log","content":"`--wait` (or `--wait=SECONDS`) polls for that first owner instead of skip-0. The\nwindow is counted from the database's first \"no owner\" answer, so a slow buildinfo\nprobe or a slow first query cannot spend it. `SKIP_CODER_AGENTS=1` skips bootstrap\nand stack-up seeding. `CODER_AGENTS_WAIT_SECS`\nis what stack `up` forwards as `--wait=`."},"2444":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-560","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-wait-window-and-the-seeder-log","content":"Stack `up` starts that seeder in the background and keeps its output in\n`${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/coder-agents-seeder.log` (`CODER_AGENTS_SEED_LOG` moves it, and the\n`note:` line stack `up` prints names the path it chose). Read that file first when\nthe Agents chat is dead after a boot: the seeder's most common outcome is a `skipped:`\nline that exits 0 - no coder container yet, no db container, nobody registered - and\nan unwritable log path degrades to the old discard with a `warning:` instead of\nfailing the boot."},"2445":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-561","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-the-script-writes","content":"A chat turn needs an enabled chat model config - the chat's own pick, else the\ndeployment default, else it errors before the first tool call - and that wiring is\nwhat the script writes. It mints a short-lived admin API token from the stack's own\nPostgres (`token_name 'stack-coder-agents'`, deleted by the trap, never printed) and\ndrives the server's experimental chat API:"},"2446":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-562","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-provider-row","content":"an `openai-compat` AI provider named **`litellm`** (not `litellm-auto`), base URL\n`http://litellm:4000/v1` - LiteLLM over the compose network, *server-side*,\nauthenticated with `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY`. The agent dials the in-process AI Gateway\n(aibridge), which forwards to the provider's `base_url` + `/chat/completions`, so\n`/v1` has to be part of the base URL and the key has to match the live proxy."},"2447":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-563","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-provider-row","content":"With `.env` leaving the key empty, compose still boots the proxy on its\n`change-me-litellm` interpolation default, and that default is what gets sent.\nCreated when absent, drift-patched when an existing provider carries a drifted\n`base_url` or key."},"2448":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-564","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-model-config-rows","content":"chat model configs `agent` (context limit 262144, set as the default; LiteLLM bakes\n`reasoning_effort` `xhigh`), `agent-low` / `agent-medium` / `agent-xhigh` (same\nlane with a baked thinking level, not default), `agent-swarm` (same lane and the same\nbaked level with the session pin lifted for that group, not default), and `chat`\n(32768), for models not already registered."},"2449":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-565","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-model-config-rows","content":"`coder` and `coder-fast` stay out on purpose: on this stack's IPEX-LLM runtime those\n  weights return tool calls as plain text with no `tool_calls`, and an agent turn without\n  callable tools cannot browse or edit, so registering them would only add a silently\n  broken model to the pickers."},"2450":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-566","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-model-config-rows","content":"`context_limit` is the agent's own compaction budget (a 70 per cent default\n  compaction threshold applies), which is why it is pinned per alias. The\n  two fast-compaction lanes are absent too, for a mechanical reason rather than a measured\n  one: the hop keys on the summary instruction the Grok CLI appends, the aibridge behind\n  these pickers never sends one, and `compaction-fast` is no client alias at all, so an\n  Agents chat on `agent-xhigh-fast` would be a chat on `agent-xhigh` under a longer name."},"2451":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-567","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-model-config-rows","content":"Before that sweep the script asks the proxy `GET /v1/model/info` and `GET /health`\n  and joins them on `model_id`, so it knows which aliases still have a healthy\n  deployment."},"2452":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-568","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-model-config-rows","content":"An alias with none is not registered: `chat` goes unhealthy the moment\n  `qwen2.5:7b` is not pulled on the Arc, and a dead entry in the model picker is a chat\n  that errors on the first send."},"2453":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-569","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-model-config-rows","content":"`/health` is read without `?check_deployments=true` because that form fires a live\n  completion per deployment and evicts the Spark prompt caches the health-check notes\n  in `litellm/config.yaml` warn about."},"2454":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-570","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-model-config-rows","content":"The gate fails open: a proxy that will not answer leaves every alias\n  registerable, and `CODER_AGENTS_LIVE_ALIASES=\"agent chat\"` overrides it outright."},"2455":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-571","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-auxiliary-pins","content":"pins for the four auxiliary lanes - `general` and `explore` (the two subagent\ntypes) prefer `agent`, `compaction` and `title_generation` (the helper calls)\nprefer `chat` - chosen only from the aliases the health gate left standing, and\nwritten when a lane carries no pin or is pinned to one of these configs whose alias\nhas since gone unhealthy."},"2456":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-572","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-auxiliary-pins","content":"A pin aimed at a config the script did not create is the\nadmin's and is left alone."},"2457":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-573","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-auxiliary-pins","content":"Both helper lanes landing on a dead `chat` is what made\nconversations lose their titles and die at the compaction threshold, which is why a\nstale pin now moves instead of being kept forever."},"2458":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-574","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-auxiliary-pins","content":"Neither preference list names `agent-swarm`: that config is registered for the model picker\nonly, because these lanes\nserve the control-plane chat agent, which is one conversation, and one conversation\nwants the pin."},"2459":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-575","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-read-only-drift-check","content":"a read-only drift check on the stored configs: a config that is disabled, is not\nthe deployment default, or carries a `context_limit` that does not match the alias's\nserving window is reported with a `warning:` and the screen to fix it on. The\nexperimental chat API has no PATCH for model configs, so these are named rather than\nwritten."},"2460":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-576","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-read-only-drift-check","content":"a three-line system-prompt addendum, appended after Coder's default prompt text\n(`include_default_system_prompt: true`), that keeps the delegation and\nsecret-hygiene rules which must hold even when a chat has no workspace attached."},"2461":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-577","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-ai-gateway-stays-off","content":"Coder's optional AI Gateway proxy stays off - LiteLLM remains the model transport,\nwhich is why no AI-Gateway keys exist in `docker-compose.yml`."},"2462":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-578","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"The `compaction` pin above belongs to Coder's chat agent and to nothing else."},"2463":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-579","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"It is one of the four auxiliary lanes the seeder writes into Coder's own model-config\nstore, so it decides which alias serves *that* agent's summariser and title call."},"2464":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-580","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"It never reaches a Grok Build CLI: the CLI reads no Coder table, and its compaction\nsummary is an **in-session call on the session model** - the `[models] default` alias,\n`agent` here - which is why the summary inherits that lane's output ceiling (measured\non the wire at `max_tokens: 32768`) instead of a cheap helper lane's."},"2465":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-581","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"`[models] session_summary` is not that call either; the key serves titles and the\nsession recap."},"2466":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-582","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"So the two compactions fail for unrelated reasons, and the fix for one is not the fix\nfor the other:"},"2467":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-583","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"Coder's chat dies at its context budget when `compaction` and `title_generation` both\nsit on a dead `chat` alias. `bazelisk run //scripts:coder-agents -- --apply` moves the\nstale pin, as above."},"2468":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-584","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"A Grok Build session logging `compact failed: exceeded wall-clock budget 300s\n(runaway generation)` is the session-model summary hanging before its first token on\nthe lane that also serves the whole agent loop. Its only live bounds are\n`GROK_COMPACTION_WALL_CLOCK_SECS`, rendered into the agent `env` because no TOML table\ntakes it, and the proxy's `request_timeout`; troubleshooting.md\nowns the measurement and the retry split that stops one hang being bought three times."},"2469":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-585","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#whose-compaction-is-whose","content":"The thresholds are separate as well. `context_limit` above is Coder's own compaction\nbudget with its 70 per cent default, while the CLI fires at\n`[session] auto_compact_threshold_percent` (70) of its 262144-token window."},"2470":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-586","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-fast-compaction-lanes","content":"That is still the shape on the two fast-compaction lanes, and it is why the remedy is\nserver-side rather than a client setting: a session on `agent-xhigh-fast` or\n`agent-swarm-fast` sends the same in-session summary call on its session model, and\n`litellm/compaction_router.py` moves that one request onto the Arc group\n`compaction-fast` before the proxy picks a deployment. Nothing in `~/.grok/config.toml`\nasks for it, every other call of that session still rides the Spark lane, and a session on\nany other alias compacts as it always has."},"2471":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-587","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-two-fast-compaction-lanes","content":"The limit is the Arc's own 32768-token window:\nabove `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (24000 estimated tokens) the summary goes to\n`agent-low` instead, which keeps the 262144-token window and drops the baked `xhigh`,\nbecause a slow summary beats one Ollama silently clamped. The key cannot raise that limit:\nthe target group's declared `max_input_tokens` caps it and logs one line when it does."},"2472":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-588","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-delegation-sketch","content":"Chat on the Agents page -> if the ask needs more tooling than chat has, Coder Agents\nprovisions or uses a workspace through `execute` -> big multi-file tasks are\n*delegated inside* that workspace: a headless Grok Build CLI run, started backgrounded\nand polled:"},"2473":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-589","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-delegation-sketch","content":"Backgrounded and polled because `execute` waits only a short window per tool call\n(about a minute), so a synchronous foreground `grok -p` would die mid-run on any big\ntask. Coder Agents then reviews the delegated diff, runs the project's tests, and\ncommits. Subagents spawned by Coder Agents cannot create workspaces: provisioning\ndecisions stop at the top-level chat."},"2474":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-590","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"Twenty-six files seed into every new workspace, on first boot and only while the file is\nabsent (the fleet family adds seventeen more paths under `~/.grok/agents`,\n`~/.grok/personas` and the two skill homes, on the contracts in\nThe seeded fleet suite):"},"2475":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-591","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/AGENTS.md`"},"2476":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-592","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"},"2477":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-593","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/INSTRUCTIONS.md`"},"2478":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-594","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/grok-build-delegation/SKILL.md`"},"2479":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-595","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"},"2480":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-596","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-learn/SKILL.md`"},"2481":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-597","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"},"2482":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-598","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-skillify/SKILL.md`"},"2483":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-599","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"},"2484":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-600","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-create-skill/SKILL.md`"},"2485":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-601","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-bazel-first/SKILL.md`"},"2486":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-602","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-bazel-first/SKILL.md`"},"2487":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-603","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/owned_files_check.py`"},"2488":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-604","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/owned_files_check.py`"},"2489":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-605","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/glob_budget.py`"},"2490":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-606","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-bazel-first/scripts/glob_budget.py`"},"2491":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-607","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-strong-types/SKILL.md`"},"2492":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-608","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-strong-types/SKILL.md`"},"2493":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-609","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-strong-types/scripts/type_baseline.py`"},"2494":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-610","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-strong-types/scripts/type_baseline.py`"},"2495":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-611","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-trunk-based-repo/SKILL.md`"},"2496":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-612","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-trunk-based-repo/SKILL.md`"},"2497":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-613","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-micro-plan/SKILL.md`"},"2498":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-614","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-micro-plan/SKILL.md`"},"2499":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-615","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.coder/skills/ez-micro-plan/scripts/plan_check.py`"},"2500":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-616","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-seeds-the-agents-context","content":"`~/.agents/skills/ez-micro-plan/scripts/plan_check.py`"},"2501":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-617","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-each-numbered-path-is-for","content":"1 and 2 are the Coder agent's *default* discovery paths."},"2502":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-618","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-each-numbered-path-is-for","content":"3 and 4 are the same text under the Grok Build CLI's convention, and the CLI is\nwhat `execute`-lane delegation drives. The chat agent dedupes skills by name,\nfirst match wins, home scope before a working-dir path, so one agent never loads a\nskill twice: the twins exist for the CLI, not as a second source for chat."},"2503":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-619","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-body-writes-each-family","content":"1-4 are written by `startup.sh.tftpl`. 5-10 are the stack's three skill commands and are\nwritten by `coder_script.stack_skills`, whose own `execve` carries the three payloads;\nthey ride their own script body rather than the startup argument because that argument\nhas a single-argument ceiling of its own\n(coder-templates.md). Each `.agents`\ncopy is a `cp` of the managed `.coder` copy, never a second inline body, so the pair\ncannot drift."},"2504":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-620","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#which-body-writes-each-family","content":"11-26 are the four commands of that second family - the three repository conversions\nplus the `/ez-micro-plan` dispatcher - and are written by\n`coder_script.stack_repo_skills`, a second body with its own ceiling, for the same\nreason and because this family also ships the python helpers 13-14, 16, 19-20 and 25-26\nthat three of its four bodies invoke by `~/.agents` path. Same absent-only contract and\nthe same `cp` twin rule; the helper directories are made by the body, so a workspace that\nalready has the `SKILL.md` but lost a helper still gets the helper back."},"2505":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-621","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-memory-and-skill-trio","content":"5 and 6 are `/ez-learn`: this stack's copy of the upstream memory commands `/flush`,\n`/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`. It turns the current session into durable facts -\nflush the conversation into a session log under the workspace's `~/.grok/memory/`\ndirectory, dream those logs into an organized `MEMORY.md`, save one note, or browse and\nclear what was stored. Reaching past those logs into the raw transcripts under\n`~/.grok/sessions/` is opt-in depth, done only when the operator asks for it. Its\ndefault mode is report-only, and it writes memory files, never a skill."},"2506":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-622","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-memory-and-skill-trio","content":"7 and 8 are `/ez-skillify`, which turns the session that just worked into a skill from its\nown transcript, and 9 and 10 are `/ez-create-skill`, which scaffolds one from what the\noperator describes."},"2507":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-623","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-memory-and-skill-trio","content":"The three payloads are\n`templates/common/ez-learn.SKILL.md`, `ez-skillify.SKILL.md` and\n`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`, on the same absent-only contract."},"2508":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-624","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-memory-and-skill-trio","content":"`/ez-create-skill` stands in for the bundled platform skill\n`create-skill`, which arrives only in the skills\nbundle behind an x.ai sign-in this stack does not have; `/ez-skillify` has no published\nupstream counterpart at all; and the memory family `/ez-learn` copies is upstream\n*built-ins*: `/flush`, `/dream` and `/memory` are gated behind cross-session memory,\nwhich is off by default, while `/remember` is a pager command with no such gate. Either\nway the seed is what makes the three commands exist."},"2509":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-625","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-memory-and-skill-trio","content":"Each ships under its own `ez-` name to stay clear\nof those upstream names, since a user-scope skill overrides a\nsame-named bundled one and a seed named `create-skill` would hide the bundled copy on a\nworkspace that ever does sign in."},"2510":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-626","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-memory-and-skill-trio","content":"The boot and the reseed delete a leftover\nmarker-carrying `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from before the rename. See the CLI section\nabove."},"2511":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-627","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-orchestration-protocol-text","content":"The text of 1-4 is the orchestration protocol (fan-out / fan-in, the edit-conflict gate,\nprobe-one-worker-first, verify-what-a-worker-actually-produced, the git cadence that\ncuts the branch before the first edit and commits each unit as it lands, holding the push\nfor the pull request, and the issue etiquette that reads the report before planning, puts a\nsingle closing keyword in the pull request body, and leaves the actual close to the merge)."},"2512":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-628","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-orchestration-protocol-text","content":"It travels as\n`templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, staged beside the staged `main.tf` and\nread back with `file()` / `filebase64()`; `templates/common/` keeps one copy for\nboth templates, and the push stages it into each template's pushed copy."},"2513":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-629","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-orchestration-protocol-text","content":"`devcontainer` and `workspace` both seed it, so the seed is *not* fetched from\nGitHub any more: no `web_search`/`web_fetch` call is needed to inspect this repo,\nand the instruction text tells the agent so."},"2514":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-630","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-orchestration-protocol-text","content":"Both `main.tf` files deliberately set **no** `CODER_AGENT_EXP_*` env keys on\n`coder_agent.main`: those keys *replace* the agent's discovery defaults, and the\ndefaults already cover exactly the seeded paths - the `~/.coder` roots plus a\nworking-dir-relative `.agents/skills`, which resolves inside the agent's working dir"},"2515":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-631","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-orchestration-protocol-text","content":"(`/home/coder` in `workspace`; in `devcontainer` with the toggle on, the\ncloned folder under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/...`, where a dev container built on\nthe sidecar carries its own home and no seeded path is visible from inside it\nanyway)."},"2516":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-632","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#where-the-marker-sits-in-each-seed","content":"Each seeded file carries the stack marker - the `managed-by: evo-t1-coder-stack`\ncomment, or the `\"__evoT1Stack\": true` / `__evoT1Stack = true` key in a JSON or TOML\nfile - in `SKILL.md` directly under the `---` frontmatter, since the agent's skill\nparser requires frontmatter on line 1 and silently drops anything else."},"2517":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-633","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#where-the-marker-sits-in-each-seed","content":"Delete a file to take it over; edit it and the stack leaves it alone.\n`~/.grok/config.toml` is the one seed where deleting a file is not the way: it needs\nboth marker lines gone, see\nThe rendered `~/.grok` config."},"2518":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-634","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#where-the-marker-sits-in-each-seed","content":"Either way the marker never reaches the model: the agent strips HTML comments from\ninjected instruction text."},"2519":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-635","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#where-the-marker-sits-in-each-seed","content":"The seeds live per workspace, on that workspace's own home volume, and seeding\ndoes not care whether the three shared state mounts exist."},"2520":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-636","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"One line each: `/ez-learn` turns a session into durable memory - flush it into a session\nlog, dream the logs into organized memory, remember one note, or browse and clear the\nstore; `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked, working from its own\ntranscript; `/ez-create-skill` builds a skill from what the operator describes. The last\ntwo write skill files; `/ez-learn` writes memory files."},"2521":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-637","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"Three things about skills matter in practice here:"},"2522":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-638","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"Write a specific `description` and `when-to-use`. That text is what the model matches\non, so a vague one leaves the skill reachable only by its slash name."},"2523":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-639","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"Set `disable-model-invocation: true` when only an explicit `/name` should run it.\nAnything that deploys, pushes or resets belongs behind that key."},"2524":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-640","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"After creating or editing anything, check discovery and provenance with `grok inspect`,\nand `grok inspect --json` for each skill's `source` path and `userInvocable`. `/skills`\nopens the same set as a menu, where a toggle takes a skill out of the slash menu\nwithout deleting the file. Discovery reloads from disk, so an edit lands next turn\nwith no restart."},"2525":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-641","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-stack-seeded-skill-commands","content":"This is workflow curation, separate from the memory system, which stores durable facts\n(upstream `/remember`, `/memory`, `/dream`, shipped here as `/ez-learn`), and from\n`AGENTS.md`, which is the always-loaded rules file. A skill loads when its `description`\nmatches, and holds a procedure."},"2526":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-642","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-repository-conversion-commands","content":"One line each: `/ez-bazel-first` puts every tracked file behind a build target, budgets the\n`glob()`s and drives the gate to full file coverage; `/ez-strong-types` pins a strict\nchecker profile per language and turns the broad-type census into a ratchet that fails on\nany widening; `/ez-trunk-based-repo` retires environment branches, lands unfinished work\nbehind a flag that defaults off, and presents the leftover branches for deletion with their\ntip SHAs. All three take a repository path and work on that checkout, so they are useful in\na workspace cloned against any of the fleet's repositories."},"2527":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-643","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-repository-conversion-commands","content":"They differ from the authoring trio in one structural way: two of them shell out to a\npython helper, so their seed ships `scripts/owned_files_check.py`, `scripts/glob_budget.py`\nand `scripts/type_baseline.py` beside the `SKILL.md`. Those helpers are stack payload like\neverything else here, which means the copy in a workspace is stack-owned while it carries the\nmarker, and the way to fix a helper is to fix the seed under `templates/common/` and\nre-materialize, never to patch the copy that is running."},"2528":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-644","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-repository-conversion-commands","content":"This repo keeps its own project-scoped `/trunk-refactor` under\n`skills/trunk-refactor/SKILL.md`, which is the rule of record for *this* repository and\ncites its gates by label. `/ez-trunk-based-repo` is the portable version of the same model:\nit discovers a target repo's own gates at inventory time rather than assuming Bazel, so it\nworks in a Compose, K3s, Ansible or Terraform repository too."},"2529":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-645","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-three-repository-conversion-commands","content":"The fourth payload `coder_script.stack_repo_skills` seeds is not a converter.\n`/ez-micro-plan` is the parent's protocol for splitting one change across a fleet of\nsubagents and proving the split dispatches; how its slots, lane and check fit together is\nThe fan-out seam."},"2530":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-646","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-session-reviewer","content":"`/reflect-and-evolve` reviews past Grok Build sessions and turns what it finds into planned changes.\nIt reads the per-session `signals.json` under `~/.grok/sessions/<escaped-cwd>/`, ranked by the bundled\n`skills/reflect-and-evolve/scripts/session_signals.py`, plus the `/ez-learn` session logs and the notes file.\nIt changes the seeds in this repo, never the copies a workspace booted with. Repo-scoped like `/trunk-refactor`\nand not seeded into a workspace home: canonical at `skills/reflect-and-evolve/SKILL.md`, discovered through\nthe `.grok/skills/reflect-and-evolve/SKILL.md` symlink, pinned by `tests/bats/reflect-and-evolve-skill.bats`."},"2531":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-647","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-repository-reviewer","content":"`/reflect-and-evolve-repo` is its sibling against a different subject: where\n`/reflect-and-evolve` improves the agent setup, this one reviews past work in *this\nrepository* and turns what it finds into planned improvements to code, project\nconventions, scripts, docs and configs."},"2532":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-648","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-repository-reviewer","content":"It reads the same ranked `signals.json` table through the same bundled\n`skills/reflect-and-evolve/scripts/session_signals.py`, which stays the only sanctioned\nway into `~/.grok/sessions/`, and it adds what a repo run has and a session run does\nnot: git history, and the repo's own tooling and gate."},"2533":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-649","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-repository-reviewer","content":"Repo-scoped like `/trunk-refactor` and not seeded into a workspace home: canonical at\n`skills/reflect-and-evolve-repo/SKILL.md`, discovered through the\n`.grok/skills/reflect-and-evolve-repo/SKILL.md` symlink, pinned by\n`tests/bats/reflect-and-evolve-repo-skill.bats`."},"2534":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-650","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-this-is-not","content":"Coder Agents is control-plane-native. This stack does not wrap Grok Build: the wiring\nabove *teaches* (skill + instructions) and *equips* (the workspace-side CLI with its\nLiteLLM alias) the native Coder agent to drive Grok Build when work is too big to\nhand-drive. Delegation is a taught habit, not the product."},"2535":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-651","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#watching-those-sessions-from-anywhere","content":"The sessions above are tmux panes, which means they outlive the browser tab that\nopened them and are visible to nothing else. `//scripts:coder-sessions` is the door\nfor the operator who is not at the desk:"},"2536":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-652","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#watching-those-sessions-from-anywhere","content":"`send`, `launch` and `stop` change things; the rest read. `watch` blocks: it returns\n0 as soon as a session stops working and 4 when its budget runs out with the session\nstill `running`, which is what makes \"tell me when it's done\" a single call an\nassistant can follow with a push. Global options\n(`--json`, `--container`, `--timeout`, `--dry-run`) go before the subcommand, and\n`--json` makes `list`, `show` and `watch` printable by a phone client."},"2537":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-653","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-credential-question","content":"**Why the daemon and not the Coder API.** A workspace that can host a session is a\n*running* one, and a running workspace is a labelled container: both templates stamp\n`coder.workspace_id` and `coder.workspace_name`, the same labels\n`scripts/github-auth.sh` enumerates."},"2538":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-654","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-credential-question","content":"Reading the daemon needs no credential, which matters because the only Coder token this\nstack mints is the root-equivalent one `scripts/push-template.sh` creates for the length\nof a push and deletes on the way out."},"2539":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-655","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#the-credential-question","content":"A chat turn should not mint one to answer \"what is running\", so where a verb belongs\nto Coder rather than to the daemon the CLI prints the hand-off command."},"2540":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-656","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#reading-the-right-container","content":"**Two details that make the answer correct.** Each workspace creates a\n`coder-<id>-dind-sidecar` carrying the same labels; it runs a Docker daemon and no\nshell, so it is dropped by name. And a tmux server belongs to one uid and listens on\n`/tmp/tmux-<uid>/default`, so the sockets are enumerated per uid - `docker exec`\nlands as the image's user, and asking *that* user for its default server reports\n\"no sessions\" for an agent the operator can see on screen."},"2541":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-657","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-each-state-word-means","content":"**The state words** come from `state_word` in the script and are what the\n`coding-agents` Hermes skill teaches: `running` (output within 30 s), `quiet` (an\nagent loaded and silent for 30 s - finished, or parked on a question, which is what\n\"it's stuck\" usually is), `waiting` (a client attached at a plain shell - a person\nis already in that pane), `idle-shell`, `detached-shell`, and `unknown`. A state\nword is a pointer at the pane, never a verdict: `tail` before you say done."},"2542":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-658","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-each-state-word-means","content":"**From the phone.** The same file runs inside the Hermes container, whose image has\nno bazelisk, straight off the identical-path checkout mount:"},"2543":{"id":"/grok-build-and-agents-659","page_id":"/grok-build-and-agents","tags":["grok","agents","workspace","mcp"],"type":"text","url":"/grok-build-and-agents#what-each-state-word-means","content":"That is the whole integration - see hermes.md for the doors Hermes\nopens, and the `coding-agents` skill for the order the assistant works them in."},"2544":{"id":"/hermes","page_id":"/hermes","type":"page","content":"Hermes host assistant","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes"},"2545":{"id":"/hermes-0","page_id":"/hermes","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"type":"text","url":"/hermes","content":"The on-host assistant with two doors - the Web Dashboard on :9119 and the host terminal via hermes-shell: how to reach it, what it may touch, and how approvals gate a mutating command from either."},"2546":{"id":"/hermes-1","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#hermes-host-assistant","content":"Hermes host assistant"},"2547":{"id":"/hermes-2","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#access","content":"Access"},"2548":{"id":"/hermes-3","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-public-name","content":"The public name"},"2549":{"id":"/hermes-4","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#signing-in","content":"Signing in"},"2550":{"id":"/hermes-5","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-chat-tab","content":"The Chat tab"},"2551":{"id":"/hermes-6","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-homepage-card","content":"The Homepage card"},"2552":{"id":"/hermes-7","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#from-the-host-terminal","content":"From the host terminal"},"2553":{"id":"/hermes-8","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#subcommands-and-options","content":"Subcommands and options"},"2554":{"id":"/hermes-9","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#one-container-not-two","content":"One container, not two"},"2555":{"id":"/hermes-10","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#check-the-approval-gate-and-the-tty","content":"check, the approval gate, and the tty"},"2556":{"id":"/hermes-11","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#what-it-can-see-and-where-it-may-write","content":"What it can see, and where it may write"},"2557":{"id":"/hermes-12","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#from-a-phone-or-anywhere","content":"From a phone, or anywhere"},"2558":{"id":"/hermes-13","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-four-doors","content":"The four doors"},"2559":{"id":"/hermes-14","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-api-server-door","content":"The API server door"},"2560":{"id":"/hermes-15","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-push-door","content":"The push door"},"2561":{"id":"/hermes-16","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-questions-you-ask-and-the-verbs-that-answer-them","content":"The questions you ask, and the verbs that answer them"},"2562":{"id":"/hermes-17","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#audit-and-drift","content":"Audit and drift"},"2563":{"id":"/hermes-18","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-commands","content":"The commands"},"2564":{"id":"/hermes-19","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#where-its-facts-come-from","content":"Where its facts come from"},"2565":{"id":"/hermes-20","page_id":"/hermes","type":"heading","tags":["hermes","dashboard","terminal","operate","safety"],"url":"/hermes#the-check-names-and-the-exit-codes","content":"The check names, and the exit codes"},"2566":{"id":"/hermes-21","page_id":"/hermes","