ravel by CLI"},"3873":{"id":"/operate/secrets-32","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"When Infisical is unreachable"},"3874":{"id":"/operate/secrets-33","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-case-that-corrupts-state","content":"The case that corrupts state"},"3875":{"id":"/operate/secrets-34","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-coder_pg_password","content":"Rotating CODER_PG_PASSWORD"},"3876":{"id":"/operate/secrets-35","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-verb-writes","content":"What the verb writes"},"3877":{"id":"/operate/secrets-36","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#why-the-predicate-reads-the-inventory-in-shell","content":"Why the predicate reads the inventory in-shell"},"3878":{"id":"/operate/secrets-37","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-next-up-is-what-applies-it","content":"The next up is what applies it"},"3879":{"id":"/operate/secrets-38","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-postgres-first-init-trap","content":"The Postgres first-init trap"},"3880":{"id":"/operate/secrets-39","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-infisical_db_password","content":"Rotating INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD"},"3881":{"id":"/operate/secrets-40","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"The credential boundary"},"3882":{"id":"/operate/secrets-41","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#where-a-value-may-travel","content":"Where a value may travel"},"3883":{"id":"/operate/secrets-42","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#what-a-log-line-may-say","content":"What a log line may say"},"3884":{"id":"/operate/secrets-43","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#files-traps-and-verification","content":"Files, traps, and verification"},"3885":{"id":"/operate/secrets-44","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#never-source-the-clis-own-dotenv-output","content":"Never source the CLI's own dotenv output"},"3886":{"id":"/operate/secrets-45","page_id":"/operate/secrets","type":"heading","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"What is deliberately not here"},"3887":{"id":"/operate/secrets-46","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"**What's on this page**"},"3888":{"id":"/operate/secrets-47","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"What the integration is, and the one property it deliberately does not change"},"3889":{"id":"/operate/secrets-48","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The flag that decides whether the bundle exists, and the second gate on the workspace side"},"3890":{"id":"/operate/secrets-49","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"What the workspace boot publishes into `~/.infisical/agent.env`, what it refuses, and the residual it cannot refuse"},"3891":{"id":"/operate/secrets-50","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The three `secrets`-profile containers, and the CLI verb that reads the flag"},"3892":{"id":"/operate/secrets-51","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The adoption sequence, one command per step, with the container each step touches"},"3893":{"id":"/operate/secrets-52","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The twelve managed keys, who consumes each one, and what breaks when it is wrong"},"3894":{"id":"/operate/secrets-53","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The three runners that refresh the env file from the vault before compose renders"},"3895":{"id":"/operate/secrets-54","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"What a vault outage does and does not do to `up`, to `pull`, and to a workspace boot"},"3896":{"id":"/operate/secrets-55","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"The `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` rotation path, the volume-init trap under it, and the probe defect that blocks the verb today"},"3897":{"id":"/operate/secrets-56","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"**What this enables**"},"3898":{"id":"/operate/secrets-57","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Holding the stack's credentials in one place instead of in twelve lines of a dotenv file"},"3899":{"id":"/operate/secrets-58","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Rotating a key without opening an editor, and knowing when the running service picks it up"},"3900":{"id":"/operate/secrets-59","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#secrets-with-infisical","content":"Deciding whether adopting the vault is worth it, from what is actually wired"},"3901":{"id":"/operate/secrets-60","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"Self-hosted Infisical is the source of record for twelve of this stack's credentials,\nbehind a flag that defaults off. Three Compose services under profile `secrets` hold the\nvault, its Postgres and its Redis; one operator CLI moves keys between the vault and the\nhost's dotenv file; and every service keeps reading credentials the way it always did."},"3902":{"id":"/operate/secrets-61","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"That last sentence is the design, so it is worth stating exactly:"},"3903":{"id":"/operate/secrets-62","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"Compose interpolates `${VAR:-default}` against the `.env` beside `docker-compose.yml`,\nand nothing in the compose layer ever opens a connection to a secrets service."},"3904":{"id":"/operate/secrets-63","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"An unreachable vault must not be able to stop `up`, so the integration does not replace\nthe dotenv chain - it feeds it."},"3905":{"id":"/operate/secrets-64","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"`pull` writes the managed keys into `.env` and compose reads that file exactly as it\nalways has. The vault is an editor for `.env`, not a dependency of the project."},"3906":{"id":"/operate/secrets-65","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"Read that as the whole delivery path: a credential change reaches a service through the\nenv file and a recreate, never through a live call to the vault."},"3907":{"id":"/operate/secrets-66","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"The one place the deploy path dials the vault at all is the runners' own refresh before\ncompose interpolates, and that call is best-effort - one `warning:` and the bring-up\ncontinues on whatever the file already held."},"3908":{"id":"/operate/secrets-67","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-integration-is","content":"This is the **free self-hosted tier**. No dynamic secrets, no cross-project sharing, and\nno `LICENSE_KEY` anywhere in the checkout: both of those features are paid, and the\nbundle ships neither. What is here is static shared secrets in one project, exported as\ndotenv."},"3909":{"id":"/operate/secrets-68","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-flag","content":"One key decides whether the bundle exists: `INFISICAL_ENABLED`, default `false`, in\n`.env.example` beside its own comment block. Owner: `platform`. Expiry: `2026-12-31`."},"3910":{"id":"/operate/secrets-69","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-truthiness-rule","content":"Unlike `OBSERVABILITY_ENABLED`, which ships on, **an unset key means off**. The\nacceptance test is `truthy()` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` - `on`, `1`, `true`, `yes` - and\nanything else, including the key being absent entirely, is off."},"3911":{"id":"/operate/secrets-70","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-truthiness-rule","content":"Off means:"},"3912":{"id":"/operate/secrets-71","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-truthiness-rule","content":"the three containers are never selected (`profiles: [\"secrets\"]`)"},"3913":{"id":"/operate/secrets-72","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-truthiness-rule","content":"the runner CLIs pass no profile argv (`compose_infisical_args` in\n`scripts/lib/infisical.sh` prints nothing, mirroring `compose_observability_args`)"},"3914":{"id":"/operate/secrets-73","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-truthiness-rule","content":"every mutating verb of the CLI refuses with a `note:` that names the key to set"},"3915":{"id":"/operate/secrets-74","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-truthiness-rule","content":"That is deliberate: a subsystem nobody opted into must not be able to hold the stack's\ncredentials hostage."},"3916":{"id":"/operate/secrets-75","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#turning-the-flag-on","content":"To adopt it, set the key in the env file that belongs to the project you run - `.env` for\nthe stable stack, `dev-stack.env` for the dev one - and bring the stack up as usual:"},"3917":{"id":"/operate/secrets-76","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#turning-the-flag-on","content":"The runner appends the profile argv, so the three services come up with the rest of the\nproject."},"3918":{"id":"/operate/secrets-77","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#turning-the-flag-on","content":"A profile selects services; it does not delete what is already running, so flipping the\nkey back off needs a `down` (or deleting the three by name) before the containers actually\ngo away. Their named volumes survive that, and `reset` is the verb that destroys them."},"3919":{"id":"/operate/secrets-78","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-second-gate","content":"There is a **second gate** under the first: `INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START`, default `false`.\nIt decides only whether a workspace's boot fetch dials the vault at all."},"3920":{"id":"/operate/secrets-79","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-second-gate","content":"It is off by default so a workspace never waits on the vault, and it does nothing while\n`INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off. Both gates are read inside the boot body itself, so the rendered\nscript is one text for every workspace and the flag pair arrives on the agent environment."},"3921":{"id":"/operate/secrets-80","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-second-gate","content":"Both gates reach the templates through `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template`: the CLI forwards\n`--var infisical_enabled=true` and `--var infisical_pull_on_start=true` only for a truthy value in\n`.env`, so the shipped state pushes neither word and each template's own `default = false` stands."},"3922":{"id":"/operate/secrets-81","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-second-gate","content":"With the flag off the `coder_script` resource is absent outright - no script, no boot log line,\nno `INFISICAL_*` key on the agent environment - and the `.bashrc` hook is not written either,\nbecause that line is gated on `infisical_hook`, which both twins set from `var.infisical_enabled`."},"3923":{"id":"/operate/secrets-82","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl` is the body both twins render into\n`coder_script \"infisical_secrets\"` (`run_on_start`, 120s timeout)."},"3924":{"id":"/operate/secrets-83","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"One fact about its output decides everything else in this section: `~/.infisical/agent.env`\nis sourced by every shell the workspace starts, under `set -a`, from the line the startup\nscript appends to `~/.bashrc`."},"3925":{"id":"/operate/secrets-84","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-workspace-boot-fetch","content":"It is not a config file one process reads; it is an `export` line per key in the environment\nof every terminal, tmux pane and command from then on, and it outlives the start that wrote\nit."},"3926":{"id":"/operate/secrets-85","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#which-volume-the-file-rides","content":"Two kinds of volume meet in that path and they are not the same thing."},"3927":{"id":"/operate/secrets-86","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#which-volume-the-file-rides","content":"What rides per owner is state only: `~/.grok/notes`, `~/.grok/memory` and\n`~/.grok/sessions` on `grok-notes-<owner-id>`, `grok-memory-<owner-id>` and\n`grok-sessions-<owner-id>`, plus the `vscode-settings-<owner-id>` overlay at\n`~/.shared/vscode`."},"3928":{"id":"/operate/secrets-87","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#which-volume-the-file-rides","content":"Everything else under `~/.grok` is a real file on that workspace's own home volume,\n`config.toml` above all - it carries this workspace's own model lanes, its session-affinity\nkey and its Grok theme."},"3929":{"id":"/operate/secrets-88","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#which-volume-the-file-rides","content":"`~/.infisical` rides no per-owner mount at all, so `agent.env` sits on this workspace's own\nhome and only this workspace reads it."},"3930":{"id":"/operate/secrets-89","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#which-volume-the-file-rides","content":"That asymmetry is why the body filters rather than trusting a share boundary: a file a\nworkspace can read is a file anything running inside it can read."},"3931":{"id":"/operate/secrets-90","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-two-self-gates","content":"The body gates itself twice before it dials anything. First the **six configured names**:\n`INFISICAL_DOMAIN`, `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID`, `INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID`,\n`INFISICAL_ENVIRONMENT`, `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID` and\n`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET`."},"3932":{"id":"/operate/secrets-91","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-two-self-gates","content":"Any one of them empty prints which names are missing and where they come from, and exits 0."},"3933":{"id":"/operate/secrets-92","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-two-self-gates","content":"Then `INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START`, whose off path prints that the boot \"keeps the credentials it\nalready has\" and also exits 0. Every other failure path - no `infisical` binary in the image,\nno `HOME`, a refused Universal Auth login, an empty export, a refused publish - exits 0 as well."},"3934":{"id":"/operate/secrets-93","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-two-self-gates","content":"The golden image bakes the pinned CLI (`images/dev/Dockerfile`, `INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION` in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`), so the missing-binary path is what an image older than that pin\nhits. A vault outage has never once stopped a workspace."},"3935":{"id":"/operate/secrets-94","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"What gets published is whatever the workspace's own machine identity can read in the project,\nwhich is what the vault scopes - the twelve-key inventory is a host-side list\n(`infisical/managed-secrets.txt`) and is deliberately not repeated in the body. So the workspace is\nnot limited to those twelve names, and two filters run on the key name before a line is written:"},"3936":{"id":"/operate/secrets-95","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"Every key beginning `INFISICAL_` is dropped, so the vault's address and its Universal Auth pair -\nboth plausible keys in the very project being exported - are never republished as workspace\nenvironment. Those names reach the workspace only on the agent environment, which Coder renders\nper start and never writes to disk."},"3937":{"id":"/operate/secrets-96","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"Every name on the body's deny list is dropped, compared on the key upper-cased so `BASH_ENV`,\n`bash_env` and `Bash_Env` all fall in one arm. The list is what a shell, the dynamic loader, an\ninterpreter or git treats as code or as loader configuration rather than as data:"},"3938":{"id":"/operate/secrets-97","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"`BASH_ENV` and `ENV` (executed by every non-interactive bash), `PROMPT_COMMAND` (by every\ninteractive one)"},"3939":{"id":"/operate/secrets-98","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"`PS4` and `IFS` (how the sourcing shell parses itself), `PATH` and `HOME` (which binary\nand which config the next command reads)"},"3940":{"id":"/operate/secrets-99","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"the `LD_*` group (`LD_PRELOAD`, `LD_LIBRARY_PATH`, `LD_AUDIT`, `LD_DEBUG`)"},"3941":{"id":"/operate/secrets-100","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"the interpreter-option group (`NODE_OPTIONS`, `PYTHONPATH`, `PYTHONSTARTUP`, `PERL5OPT`,\n`PERL5LIB`, `RUBYOPT`)"},"3942":{"id":"/operate/secrets-101","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"the `GIT_*` group (`GIT_SSH_COMMAND`, `GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL`, `GIT_EXTERNAL_DIFF`, `GIT_DIR`)"},"3943":{"id":"/operate/secrets-102","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-gets-published","content":"the program-choice group (`PAGER`, `LESS`, `EDITOR`, `VISUAL`, `SSH_ASKPASS`,\n`SUDO_ASKPASS`)"},"3944":{"id":"/operate/secrets-103","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-pre-publish-refusal","content":"After the loop the staged file is read once more against one alternation built from that same\nvariable, and a match makes the body **refuse to publish**: it removes the staged file, prints\n`infisical: refused to publish <home>/.infisical/agent.env; the staged file names a\nshell-control variable. Nothing was written and no secret value is shown.` and exits 0."},"3945":{"id":"/operate/secrets-104","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-pre-publish-refusal","content":"The `agent.env` a previous boot wrote then stays exactly as it was, which is the point of\nrefusing rather than correcting."},"3946":{"id":"/operate/secrets-105","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-pre-publish-refusal","content":"The obvious alternative - appending `unset PATH HOME IFS` lines to the file - is the wrong\nshape: those names would be gone from every shell that sourced the file and from everything\nthose shells then launch. A refusal costs one refresh; that would cost the workspace."},"3947":{"id":"/operate/secrets-106","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#values-are-rewritten-not-copied","content":"Every value is rewritten rather than copied, and that is a security step, not a stylistic one -\nsee the last bullet of The credential boundary for the upstream\nquoting bug that makes it necessary. Two shapes still do not land, and the log says a different\nthing for each:"},"3948":{"id":"/operate/secrets-107","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#values-are-rewritten-not-copied","content":"**A value whose opening single quote does not close on its own line** - a PEM or a multi-line\ncertificate is the shape - is dropped whole. Its continuation lines are swallowed until one\ncarries a quote, because publishing them would invent keys out of secret bytes. One note per\nboot, naming neither key nor value: `infisical: skipped a secret whose value spans more than\none line; only single-line secrets are published.` The body carries no other shape, so a\ncredential of that shape has to reach a workspace by some path other than this fetch."},"3949":{"id":"/operate/secrets-108","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#values-are-rewritten-not-copied","content":"**A key the shell could not use as an identifier** - empty, starting with a digit, or holding a\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, which is also where blank and comment lines fall out - is\nskipped silently. Nothing is printed for it; the only line that says how many keys landed is the\nsuccess line, `ok: infisical: loaded N secret keys into <home>/.infisical/agent.env`, and it\ncounts what the publish loop actually wrote."},"3950":{"id":"/operate/secrets-109","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-trust-boundary","content":"**The security boundary**, stated so no operator has to guess at it: because `agent.env` is\nsourced by every shell a workspace starts, a vault key that the shell, the dynamic loader, an\ninterpreter or git would treat as code is not a secret, it is a command line chosen by whoever\ncan write a secret in the project."},"3951":{"id":"/operate/secrets-110","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-trust-boundary","content":"The two defences are the deny list above, matched on the normalised case, and the pre-publish\npass that refuses to write the file at all and leaves the previous `agent.env` in place."},"3952":{"id":"/operate/secrets-111","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#write-access-to-the-vault-project","content":"**The residual, honestly.** That list names what a shell or a loader *executes*. It is not a list\nof every environment variable that changes what a program does, and it cannot be one."},"3953":{"id":"/operate/secrets-112","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#write-access-to-the-vault-project","content":"Anyone with write access to the Infisical project still chooses which ordinary variables a\nworkspace boots with, and because the fetch's line is appended to `~/.bashrc` and runs under\n`set -a`, one of those names can overwrite whatever that file set earlier in it."},"3954":{"id":"/operate/secrets-113","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#write-access-to-the-vault-project","content":"Treat write access to the vault project as the trust boundary it is: it is shell access to\nevery workspace that boots with the fetch on. Read access is a much smaller grant than write,\nand the machine identity the workspaces authenticate as should hold only the former."},"3955":{"id":"/operate/secrets-114","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-files-own-mode","content":"The file itself is written mode 600 inside a mode 700 `~/.infisical`, staged beside its\ndestination and moved into place in one step rather than appended, so a reader never sees a\nhalf-written file."},"3956":{"id":"/operate/secrets-115","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-files-own-mode","content":"`docs/coder-templates.md` carries the template side of all of this: the eight `infisical_*`\nvariables, why the credential pair rides the agent environment rather than the rendered script\nbody, and the order the boot body runs in."},"3957":{"id":"/operate/secrets-116","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"| Service     | Image                           | Job                                                                                                                         | Published                                                                                                                                 | Volume |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------ |\n| `infisical` | `infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` | The vault. Serves the UI and the API on container port `8080`, runs its own cron and queue loop against Postgres and Redis. | `\"${INFISICAL_PORT:-8182}:8080\"` - published plainly, the way `:4000` and `:8181` are, so a workspace reaches it through the host-gateway | -      |","table":"table-0"},"3958":{"id":"/operate/secrets-117","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"| Service        | Image                | Job                                                                         | Published                  | Volume                                            |\n| -------------- | -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical-db` | `postgres:16-alpine` | Infisical's store. Infisical is PostgreSQL-only; the Mongo path is retired. | nothing - compose DNS only | `infisical-db-data` -> `/var/lib/postgresql/data` |","table":"table-0"},"3959":{"id":"/operate/secrets-118","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"| Service           | Image            | Job                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Published                  | Volume                                                                                                                                    |\n| ----------------- | ---------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical-redis` | `redis:7-alpine` | Job queue and cache. Runs `--save '' --appendonly no` under the `noeviction` policy: a `volatile-*` policy evicts queued jobs under memory pressure, and a dropped job is a sync that silently never runs. | nothing - compose DNS only | `infisical-redis-data` -> `/data` (empty while persistence is off; declared so a future change lands on a volume, not the writable layer) |","table":"table-0"},"3960":{"id":"/operate/secrets-119","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"All three carry `restart: unless-stopped`, the `secrets` profile, and the same three-line\n`json-file` logging block the rest of the stack uses, with `tag:` equal to the service\nname so Alloy ships each container to Loki under `container=<name>`."},"3961":{"id":"/operate/secrets-120","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"None of the three is a Prometheus target - Infisical publishes no `/metrics` endpoint the\nstack can rely on and neither alpine image ships one - so they are recorded as log-only in\nobservability.md."},"3962":{"id":"/operate/secrets-121","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"The `infisical` container takes `ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `AUTH_SECRET`, `SITE_URL`,\n`DB_CONNECTION_URI`, `REDIS_URL`, `HOST=0.0.0.0` and `PORT=8080` from the environment."},"3963":{"id":"/operate/secrets-122","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"`DB_CONNECTION_URI` and the db service's `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` both interpolate\n`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, which is the only thing keeping the role and its pointer in step\nacross a rotation."},"3964":{"id":"/operate/secrets-123","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-services","content":"The public name, when you turn one on, is `STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (default label `infisical`)\ncomposed by `scripts/lib/domain.sh` like every other name; the router sends that Host to\n`http://infisical:8080`. See public-access.md."},"3965":{"id":"/operate/secrets-124","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-operator-cli","content":"Everything an operator does goes through one CLI, from any cwd:"},"3966":{"id":"/operate/secrets-125","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb     | What it does                                                                                                                                                           | Writes  |\n| -------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `status` | What this box is configured to do: the flag, the profile argv, the port, the vault URL, the inventory size, and which required keys are still empty. Contacts nothing. | nothing |","table":"table-1"},"3967":{"id":"/operate/secrets-126","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb    | What it does                                                                                                                                                              | Writes  |\n| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `check` | `status` plus whether the CLI binary is installed, whether the required keys are all set, and whether the machine identity can actually exchange a token. Run this first. | nothing |","table":"table-1"},"3968":{"id":"/operate/secrets-127","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb          | What it does                                                                                                    | Writes     |\n| ------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------- |\n| `install-cli` | Fetch the pinned release (`INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION`) and install the binary. Never pipes a download into a shell. | the binary |","table":"table-1"},"3969":{"id":"/operate/secrets-128","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb        | What it does                                                                                                                          | Writes                    |\n| ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------- |\n| `bootstrap` | Create the super admin, the organization and the machine identity on a fresh instance, then record the identifiers into the env file. | the instance, then `.env` |","table":"table-1"},"3970":{"id":"/operate/secrets-129","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb   | What it does                                                                                                            | Writes    |\n| ------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | --------- |\n| `push` | Seed or migrate the managed keys from the env file into the vault, then read the vault back and compare a hash per key. | the vault |","table":"table-1"},"3971":{"id":"/operate/secrets-130","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb   | What it does                                                 | Writes |\n| ------ | ------------------------------------------------------------ | ------ |\n| `pull` | Reconcile the managed keys from the vault into the env file. | `.env` |","table":"table-1"},"3972":{"id":"/operate/secrets-131","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb         | What it does                                                          | Writes                 |\n| ------------ | --------------------------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |\n| `rotate KEY` | Mint a fresh value for one managed key, vault first, env file second. | the vault, then `.env` |","table":"table-1"},"3973":{"id":"/operate/secrets-132","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"| Verb         | What it does                                                  | Writes  |\n| ------------ | ------------------------------------------------------------- | ------- |\n| `run -- CMD` | Exec a command with the project's secrets in its environment. | nothing |","table":"table-1"},"3974":{"id":"/operate/secrets-133","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-verbs","content":"`--env-file PATH` points the verb at a dotenv file other than the `.env` compose itself\nreads. `--help` prints the usage block above."},"3975":{"id":"/operate/secrets-134","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#exit-codes","content":"Exit codes, which are how a script tells \"the vault said no\" from \"the vault is switched\noff\":"},"3976":{"id":"/operate/secrets-135","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#exit-codes","content":"**0** - the verb did its job. Always for `status` and `check`: a check that fails on a\nstack that never adopted Infisical is a check nobody runs. `run` exits 0 on the\nflag-off refusal too, having printed the `note:`; every other mutating verb exits 5."},"3977":{"id":"/operate/secrets-136","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#exit-codes","content":"**1** - a real failure. The CLI errored, a read-back hash disagreed, a `rotate` target\nis not a managed key. A `rotate` or `run` that names no key and no command lands here as\nwell, on an `error:` line."},"3978":{"id":"/operate/secrets-137","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#exit-codes","content":"**2** - an unknown subcommand, always raised before any work starts."},"3979":{"id":"/operate/secrets-138","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#exit-codes","content":"**5** - refused, because `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off or a prerequisite is empty. Printed\nas a `note:` that names the key to set."},"3980":{"id":"/operate/secrets-139","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#exit-codes","content":"There is deliberately no `--json` mode: a JSON answer about a credential is an answer that\nleaks it in a different font."},"3981":{"id":"/operate/secrets-140","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-runners-own-pull","content":"Nothing else in the repo shells out to this CLI."},"3982":{"id":"/operate/secrets-141","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-runners-own-pull","content":"The three runners that address the compose\nproject - `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack`, `//scripts:prod-stack` and `//scripts:update-stack` -\nsource `scripts/lib/infisical.sh` and call `infisical_pull` themselves, immediately before compose\ninterpolates the env file, so a key rotated in the vault lands on the bring-up in front of you\ninstead of waiting for a human to run the verb."},"3983":{"id":"/operate/secrets-142","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-runners-own-pull","content":"Each prints one `-> vault: refreshing the env file\nfrom Infisical` phase line when the flag is on, and each treats a failed pull as one `warning:`\nnaming the retry - `the env file was not refreshed from Infisical; <file> keeps the credentials it\nalready has - retry with bazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- pull` - and then continues."},"3984":{"id":"/operate/secrets-143","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-runners-own-pull","content":"The branch never runs while the flag is off, so a stack that never adopted the vault reads and\nprints exactly what it did before."},"3985":{"id":"/operate/secrets-144","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-runners-own-pull","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack -- --dry-run` plans that fetch\n(`note: dry run: would refresh <file> from Infisical`) and writes nothing; the two stack\nrunners have no dry-run mode, so their refresh happens only on a real `up`."},"3986":{"id":"/operate/secrets-145","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#adopting-the-vault","content":"The sequence, in this order. Each step names what it changes."},"3987":{"id":"/operate/secrets-146","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#prepare-the-env-file-and-the-flag","content":"Give the vault its own bootstrap credentials and its admin address in `.env`.\n`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, `INFISICAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY` and `INFISICAL_AUTH_SECRET` ship\nas `change-me-*` placeholders in `.env.example`, and `INFISICAL_ADMIN_EMAIL` ships\nempty while `INFISICAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` ships as a placeholder - `bootstrap` refuses\neither if it is still empty or still starts with `change-me`."},"3988":{"id":"/operate/secrets-147","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#prepare-the-env-file-and-the-flag","content":"These four cannot come from the vault, because the vault needs them to start."},"3989":{"id":"/operate/secrets-148","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#prepare-the-env-file-and-the-flag","content":"Turn the flag on: `INFISICAL_ENABLED=true` in the project's env file."},"3990":{"id":"/operate/secrets-149","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#install-the-cli-and-start-the-services","content":"Install the CLI binary on the host."},"3991":{"id":"/operate/secrets-150","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#install-the-cli-and-start-the-services","content":"This writes only the binary, at the path `infisical_cli_install_path` prints, and nothing\nelse changes. The release it fetches is the pin: `INFISICAL_CLI_VERSION` in `.env.example`\n(0.45.8 as shipped), from the goreleaser asset name the seam builds. Nothing here pulls a\nversion out of the network."},"3992":{"id":"/operate/secrets-151","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#install-the-cli-and-start-the-services","content":"Start the three services. `up` selects the profile now that the flag is on:"},"3993":{"id":"/operate/secrets-152","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#install-the-cli-and-start-the-services","content":"That creates `infisical`, `infisical-db` and `infisical-redis`, on the image tags the\n`image:` lines in `docker-compose.yml` pin (`infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` as shipped)."},"3994":{"id":"/operate/secrets-153","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#install-the-cli-and-start-the-services","content":"The vault answers on `http://127.0.0.1:8182` (`INFISICAL_PORT`) on the stack host, and on\nthe published LAN face. Expect the two alpine containers to read `running` rather than\n`healthy`: neither declares a healthcheck, because the only consumer waits on\n`service_started`."},"3995":{"id":"/operate/secrets-154","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#install-the-cli-and-start-the-services","content":"With the flag already on, this first `up` also tries the env-file refresh and gets nothing:\nthere is no `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` yet, so the seam prints `note: the pull was refused before it\nran; <file> keeps the values it has` and the runner follows with its `warning:` line. That\npair is noise until step 5 writes the identifiers - it is not a failed bring-up."},"3996":{"id":"/operate/secrets-155","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#bootstrap-the-instance","content":"Bootstrap the instance:"},"3997":{"id":"/operate/secrets-156","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#bootstrap-the-instance","content":"This creates the super admin, the organization and the machine identity **inside the\n`infisical` container**, and writes the returned identifiers into `.env`."},"3998":{"id":"/operate/secrets-157","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#bootstrap-the-instance","content":"It is idempotent in the safe direction: if `INFISICAL_PROJECT_ID` or\n`INFISICAL_MACHINE_IDENTITY_ID` is already set it prints a `note:` and refuses to run\nagain."},"3999":{"id":"/operate/secrets-158","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#bootstrap-the-instance","content":"It needs `python3` on PATH to parse the response, and the CLI from step 3. The\nmachine identity's own token is checked for presence and deliberately not stored: it stays\ninside Infisical and every later run exchanges the pair below for a fresh one."},"4000":{"id":"/operate/secrets-159","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#paste-the-universal-auth-pair","content":"Paste the machine identity's Universal Auth pair into `.env`. `bootstrap` prints the `note:`\nthat says so once it has written the identifiers, and nothing downstream guesses:\n`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_ID` and `INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` are two of\nthe six keys `check` reports as empty, and a `push` or `pull` with either blank is refused\nbefore it authenticates."},"4001":{"id":"/operate/secrets-160","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#paste-the-universal-auth-pair","content":"Create the credentials for that identity in the Infisical UI and put both values in the env\nfile; neither belongs in a commit."},"4002":{"id":"/operate/secrets-161","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#seed-the-vault-and-prove-the-round-trip","content":"Adopt what is already in `.env`:"},"4003":{"id":"/operate/secrets-162","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#seed-the-vault-and-prove-the-round-trip","content":"This seeds the twelve managed keys into the vault, skipping any key the env file does\nnot hold or holds empty - with a `warning:`, never an empty write. It then exports the\nproject again and compares a hash per key, so a set the server accepted and dropped is\nreported as a failure rather than a clean seed."},"4004":{"id":"/operate/secrets-163","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#seed-the-vault-and-prove-the-round-trip","content":"Prove the round trip:"},"4005":{"id":"/operate/secrets-164","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#seed-the-vault-and-prove-the-round-trip","content":"With the vault and `.env` agreeing this writes nothing new. It is the read path, so it\nis the one to run before you trust `push`."},"4006":{"id":"/operate/secrets-165","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#recreate-the-consumers","content":"Recreate the consumers so they pick up what the vault holds:"},"4007":{"id":"/operate/secrets-166","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#recreate-the-consumers","content":"Compose reads `.env` when it creates a container, and this `up` refreshes that file from\nthe vault first (see The operator CLI for the runners' own pull)."},"4008":{"id":"/operate/secrets-167","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#recreate-the-consumers","content":"Nothing is recreated until you ask for it, so the `pull` in step 8 alone changes no running\nservice - this step does."},"4009":{"id":"/operate/secrets-168","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#recreate-the-consumers","content":"Then, any time after, `check` is the single command that asks every precondition at once:"},"4010":{"id":"/operate/secrets-169","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"The inventory is data, not code: `infisical/managed-secrets.txt`, read through\n`infisical_managed_keys()`. Never re-hardcode the list."},"4011":{"id":"/operate/secrets-170","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"The count is asserted - `check` warns when the inventory is not exactly twelve, because a\nsilently-short list would leave a credential outside the vault while still reporting a clean\nsync."},"4012":{"id":"/operate/secrets-171","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"Every key must also exist in `.env.example`, since compose keeps interpolating that file."},"4013":{"id":"/operate/secrets-172","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                 | Consumed by                          | What breaks if it is wrong |\n| ------------------- | ------------------------------------ | -------------------------- |\n| `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` | `db` container's `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` |                            |","table":"table-2"},"4014":{"id":"/operate/secrets-173","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                  | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                             |\n| -------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` |             | `/metrics` carries this key as its bearer token too, so that target goes down with it. |","table":"table-2"},"4015":{"id":"/operate/secrets-174","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                    | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong            |\n| ---------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_SECRET` |             | The dashboard's own login is refused. |","table":"table-2"},"4016":{"id":"/operate/secrets-175","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                      | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                        |\n| ------------------------ | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HOMEPAGE_AUTH_PASSWORD` |             | The dashboard asks for a credential nobody holds. |","table":"table-2"},"4017":{"id":"/operate/secrets-176","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                         | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                         |\n| --------------------------- | ----------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PASSWORD` |             | The dashboard on `:9119` and its public name both refuse the login, on every door. |","table":"table-2"},"4018":{"id":"/operate/secrets-177","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                       | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                 |\n| ------------------------- | ----------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `HERMES_DASHBOARD_SECRET` |             | The dashboard session does not hold; the Chat tab will not stay signed in. |","table":"table-2"},"4019":{"id":"/operate/secrets-178","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                     | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                                 |\n| ----------------------- | ----------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `HERMES_API_SERVER_KEY` |             | The programmatic door the agents call with answers unauthorised; the Chat tab still works. |","table":"table-2"},"4020":{"id":"/operate/secrets-179","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                      | Consumed by             | What breaks if it is wrong                                                                                                                           |\n| ------------------------ | ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` | Grafana (observability) | The observability admin login fails. `GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER` is not managed, so an operator who changed only the password is locked out by a half-sync. |","table":"table-2"},"4021":{"id":"/operate/secrets-180","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key                       | Consumed by      | What breaks if it is wrong                             |\n| ------------------------- | ---------------- | ------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `STACK_SEARCH_SECRET_KEY` | `SEARXNG_SECRET` | `search` lane answers empty rather than with an error. |","table":"table-2"},"4022":{"id":"/operate/secrets-181","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key               | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong                                                                                                                                              |\n| ----------------- | ----------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `SPARK_1_API_KEY` |             | That box's deployments fail upstream auth and every alias riding them errors - while the other two boxes keep serving, which is what makes it read as a partial outage. |","table":"table-2"},"4023":{"id":"/operate/secrets-182","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key               | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong |\n| ----------------- | ----------- | -------------------------- |\n| `SPARK_2_API_KEY` |             | As above, for that box.    |","table":"table-2"},"4024":{"id":"/operate/secrets-183","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"| Key               | Consumed by | What breaks if it is wrong |\n| ----------------- | ----------- | -------------------------- |\n| `SPARK_3_API_KEY` |             | As above, for that box.    |","table":"table-2"},"4025":{"id":"/operate/secrets-184","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-twelve-managed-keys","content":"The first eight are the list `scripts/bootstrap.sh` used to carry twice; that duplication\nis what this inventory replaced."},"4026":{"id":"/operate/secrets-185","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#keys-the-vault-does-not-hold","content":"Two lists sit outside the inventory, and they are outside for different reasons."},"4027":{"id":"/operate/secrets-186","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-four-compose-interpolated-keys","content":"Four secret-shaped keys compose interpolates are among them, and each one is\noutside for a reason worth knowing before you add it:"},"4028":{"id":"/operate/secrets-187","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-four-compose-interpolated-keys","content":"`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD`, `INFISICAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY`, `INFISICAL_AUTH_SECRET` - the\nvault's own bootstrap credentials. By definition they cannot come from the vault: it\nneeds them to start. They live in `.env`, and you set them."},"4029":{"id":"/operate/secrets-188","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-four-compose-interpolated-keys","content":"`CODER_DEFAULT_TOKEN_LIFETIME` - a duration, not a secret."},"4030":{"id":"/operate/secrets-189","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-that-travel-by-cli","content":"Three more are credentials that never appear in a compose interpolation, so they\ntravel to their consumer through a CLI rather than through `${VAR}`:"},"4031":{"id":"/operate/secrets-190","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-that-travel-by-cli","content":"`INFISICAL_ADMIN_PASSWORD` - the login `bootstrap` uses to create the project and the\nmachine identity (`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:1216`), minted by\n`scripts/bootstrap.sh:119`. It is spent before the project exists, so there is no\nvault to read it from yet."},"4032":{"id":"/operate/secrets-191","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-that-travel-by-cli","content":"`INFISICAL_UNIVERSAL_AUTH_CLIENT_SECRET` - the machine identity's Universal Auth\nsecret, which is the credential every fetch authenticates with\n(`scripts/lib/infisical.sh:803`). Storing it in the project it unlocks would make the\nvault a precondition of reading the vault."},"4033":{"id":"/operate/secrets-192","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-that-travel-by-cli","content":"`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` - the optional Context7 MCP lane's upstream key, forwarded as\n`context7_api_key` by `scripts/push-template.sh:573-577` and seeded into\n`~/.grok/config.toml` by `//scripts:seed-dev-env`. Most operators never set it, and\n`pull` prints one `warning:` per inventory key the vault does not carry, so shipping\nit in the inventory would make the default state a warning on every fetch."},"4034":{"id":"/operate/secrets-193","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-three-that-travel-by-cli","content":"Anything compose interpolates that looks like a credential and appears nowhere on this\npage is either a bug in the inventory or a bug in the compose file, and `check` is the\ncommand that catches the first."},"4035":{"id":"/operate/secrets-194","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"The outage is bounded, because the only read the deploy path makes is a best-effort refresh\nwhose failure is one `warning:` line."},"4036":{"id":"/operate/secrets-195","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**`up` cannot be stopped by it.** Compose never contacts Infisical: it interpolates `.env`,\nwhich is a file on disk, and whether the three containers are part of the project is a\nprofile decision made from one key, not a network result. The runner's own pull runs before\nthat interpolation and a failed one costs a line, not the bring-up - `warning: the env file\nwas not refreshed from Infisical; <file> keeps the credentials it already has - retry with\nbazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- pull` - and compose recreates against what the file\nalready held."},"4037":{"id":"/operate/secrets-196","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"`//scripts:prod-stack` and `//scripts:update-stack` behave the same way; the latter plans\nthe fetch instead of running it under `--dry-run`."},"4038":{"id":"/operate/secrets-197","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**`pull` leaves `.env` alone and says so.** A failed export is an `error:` line naming\nthe file as untouched, and the verb exits 1. An export that comes back empty is a\n`warning:` and the same untouched file. A key the vault does not carry, or carries\nempty, is a `warning:` per key and the existing value in `.env` stays exactly as it was"},"4039":{"id":"/operate/secrets-198","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"a half-seeded project must not lock every service out of its own credential."},"4040":{"id":"/operate/secrets-199","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**A workspace still starts.** The boot fetch is best-effort: with the flag off, with\n`INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START` off, with no CLI in the image (the golden image bakes the\npinned release, so that is an image older than the pin), with a refused Universal Auth\nlogin, with an empty export, or with a refused publish, it exits 0 and the workspace\ncontinues its startup. `agent.env` keeps whatever the last start wrote, since the body\nstages beside the destination and moves in one step rather than truncating first. A vault\noutage has never once stopped a workspace."},"4041":{"id":"/operate/secrets-200","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#when-infisical-is-unreachable","content":"**`rotate` stops at the first half.** If the vault refuses the write, `.env` is left\nuntouched and the verb exits 1 - the stack keeps running on the old value. The reverse\ncase prints the recovery in the error line: if the vault took the new value and the env\nwrite failed, re-run `pull` to converge."},"4042":{"id":"/operate/secrets-201","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-case-that-corrupts-state","content":"So neither side can fail to *start* over a vault that is down. The workspace is the one that\nnever can: every path in the boot body, refusals included, exits 0 and the agent goes on with\nthe environment it already had."},"4043":{"id":"/operate/secrets-202","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-case-that-corrupts-state","content":"The stack is the one with state to corrupt, and the way it breaks is the opposite of an\noutage - the refresh succeeds by writing, so a vault that answers but holds a value nothing\nauthenticates with is what brings `coder`, `litellm`, `homepage`, `hermes`, `grafana` or\n`searxng` up unable to log in."},"4044":{"id":"/operate/secrets-203","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-case-that-corrupts-state","content":"That is a converge-and-recreate problem, not a start problem: fix the value in the vault, run\n`up` again, and read the `db` and `coder` logs if Coder takes longer than a recreate to come\nback."},"4045":{"id":"/operate/secrets-204","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-the-verb-writes","content":"The verb mints a fresh value, writes the **vault first**, and only touches `.env` once the\nvault accepted it - so there is no moment where `.env` holds a password the vault does not\nknow about. `rotate` names one managed key; naming anything outside the inventory is an\n`error:` and exit 1."},"4046":{"id":"/operate/secrets-205","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#why-the-predicate-reads-the-inventory-in-shell","content":"Whether a name counts as managed is decided by `infisical_is_managed_key` in\n`scripts/lib/infisical.sh`, which reads the inventory and compares the names inside the\nshell."},"4047":{"id":"/operate/secrets-206","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#why-the-predicate-reads-the-inventory-in-shell","content":"The first version piped the reader into `grep -qx` instead, and under the CLI's\n`set -euo pipefail` that lost: `grep -q` exits on its first match, the reader then died of\nSIGPIPE, the pipeline reported 141, and the verb called a genuinely managed key unmanaged\non 195 of 200 attempts."},"4048":{"id":"/operate/secrets-207","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#why-the-predicate-reads-the-inventory-in-shell","content":"Two suites now hold the line - one drives all twelve names through the predicate inside a\n`pipefail` shell, the other runs the verb against each of them - so the shape cannot come\nback unnoticed. A name outside the inventory is still an `error:` and exit 1, and the\ninventory itself stays the only list."},"4049":{"id":"/operate/secrets-208","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-next-up-is-what-applies-it","content":"Then the note the CLI prints is the real edge, and it is worth repeating here: compose\nreads `.env` only when a container is recreated, so the rotated password reaches Coder on\nthe next `up`. Nothing hot-reloads it."},"4050":{"id":"/operate/secrets-209","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-postgres-first-init-trap","content":"Under that sits one trap. The Postgres image honours `POSTGRES_PASSWORD` **only at first\nvolume init**, so changing it in `.env` and recreating `db` would leave the stored\npassword unchanged and lock Coder out of its own database. Two things cover it:"},"4051":{"id":"/operate/secrets-210","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-postgres-first-init-trap","content":"`scripts/lib/pg-apply-password.sh` reconciles the password at the `db` entrypoint, so\nthe stored credential is brought in line with `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` on every start of that\ncontainer."},"4052":{"id":"/operate/secrets-211","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-postgres-first-init-trap","content":"`restart_coder_if_pg_auth_failed`, sourced by both stack runners, bounces `coder` when\nits logs show `password authentication failed` - which is the failure mode a stale\npassword produces when the two halves land in the wrong order."},"4053":{"id":"/operate/secrets-212","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-postgres-first-init-trap","content":"Both run as part of `up`, so the operator's job is one `up` after the rotate. Read the\n`db` and `coder` logs if Coder takes longer than a recreate to come back:"},"4054":{"id":"/operate/secrets-213","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#rotating-infisical_db_password","content":"`INFISICAL_DB_PASSWORD` has the same first-init behaviour and no reconciler beside it - the\ncompose block says so at its own comment - so rotating it needs the `infisical-db-data`\nvolume reset, not a recreate."},"4055":{"id":"/operate/secrets-214","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#the-credential-boundary","content":"The rules that shape every write in this subsystem, inherited from\n`scripts/dashboard-password.sh`:"},"4056":{"id":"/operate/secrets-215","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#where-a-value-may-travel","content":"A value does not travel in argv except where upstream offers no other channel. The seam's\nheader names the exceptions: `--client-secret=` on the Universal Auth login and `--password=`\non `bootstrap`, because that CLI takes neither on stdin; and the `KEY=@<file>` form for a\nsingle write, which names a file rather than the value, as `--file=<tmp600>` does for a bulk\nset."},"4057":{"id":"/operate/secrets-216","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#where-a-value-may-travel","content":"Everything else that carries a managed value crosses the seam by redirect into a mode-600\nfile, never through `$( )`, a pipeline, or a variable the CLI fills."},"4058":{"id":"/operate/secrets-217","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-a-log-line-may-say","content":"A value never appears in a log line, including a failure line. Every ok, warning and\nerror names the **key**, never the value, and counts rather than listing."},"4059":{"id":"/operate/secrets-218","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-a-log-line-may-say","content":"The one function allowed to put a credential on a stream is `infisical_login`, whose whole\njob is handing the access token to its caller on stdout while its commentary goes to stderr;\nthe callers that do not need it send that stream to `/dev/null`. The boot body scrubs the\ntoken and the client secret out of the CLI's own error text before it prints what the export\nsaid."},"4060":{"id":"/operate/secrets-219","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#files-traps-and-verification","content":"A file that holds one is mode 600 and is removed on the failure path too, by the `EXIT`\ntrap the seam installs - export, push payload, verify read-back and rotate value all go\nthrough it. bash keeps one `EXIT` handler, so the seam captures whatever the caller had\ninstalled and runs it after its own temps are gone; what an entry script must never do is\ntake that slot back after sourcing the seam, which would strand a file of secrets on the\nfailure path."},"4061":{"id":"/operate/secrets-220","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#files-traps-and-verification","content":"A write is verified by hashing the value read back before anything claims `ok:`."},"4062":{"id":"/operate/secrets-221","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#files-traps-and-verification","content":"A key the vault does not carry is a warning, never a blanked line."},"4063":{"id":"/operate/secrets-222","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#never-source-the-clis-own-dotenv-output","content":"**A vault value is never sourced in the shape the CLI wrote it.** Measured against\n`infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` with CLI v0.45.8: `secrets export --format=dotenv` wraps\neach value in single quotes and leaves an embedded quote raw, so a secret carrying an\napostrophe closes its quote early and the rest of the line is shell - sourcing it either\naborts or runs the tail as a command."},"4064":{"id":"/operate/secrets-223","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#never-source-the-clis-own-dotenv-output","content":"`--format=dotenv-export` uses the same writer and fails the same way. The boot body\ntherefore takes every value apart and re-emits it under quoting that has no escape hatch,\nwhich is what makes `~/.bashrc`'s `set -a; . agent.env` safe to keep. Anything else that\nreads that file inherits the guarantee; nothing should feed the CLI's own dotenv output to\na shell."},"4065":{"id":"/operate/secrets-224","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#never-source-the-clis-own-dotenv-output","content":"There is no reason to open `.env` by hand while this subsystem is adopted, and no command\nhere prints a credential. If you are cat-ing the env file to debug a sync, you are reading\nthe wrong file: `status` and `check` answer the same question."},"4066":{"id":"/operate/secrets-225","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No dynamic secrets, no cross-project sharing.** Both are paid features of Infisical\nand neither is used: no `LICENSE_KEY` is read, generated, or referenced. The model is\nstatic shared secrets exported as dotenv."},"4067":{"id":"/operate/secrets-226","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No vault dependency in the deploy path.** Compose reads `.env`, and the one call the\nrunners make before it is best-effort: warn, continue, bring the stack up on the values the\nfile already held."},"4068":{"id":"/operate/secrets-227","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"The follow-up that would change that is a compose-secrets rewrite of every service block,\nand it would make every bring-up wait on a server nothing here waits on today."},"4069":{"id":"/operate/secrets-228","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No multi-line value reaches a workspace.** The boot fetch publishes single-line values only\nand drops the rest with one note; see\nThe workspace boot fetch."},"4070":{"id":"/operate/secrets-229","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No scrape, no alert.** The three containers are log-only, like `ollama`, `kasm` and\nthe rest. `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check` does not walk them; their state\nis `dev-stack -- status` and their logs are in Loki under their `container=` tag."},"4071":{"id":"/operate/secrets-230","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"**No second inventory.** Twelve keys, one file. A key added to compose without joining\n`infisical/managed-secrets.txt` stays outside the vault silently, which is why `check`\nasserts the count rather than trusting the file."},"4072":{"id":"/operate/secrets-231","page_id":"/operate/secrets","tags":["secrets","infisical","operate","credentials"],"type":"text","url":"/operate/secrets#what-is-deliberately-not-here","content":"Related: observability.md (the `GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD` login and\nthe log stream these containers ship), public-access.md (the\n`infisical-*` public name), troubleshooting.md."},"4073":{"id":"/project-conventions","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"page","content":"Project conventions","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions"},"4074":{"id":"/project-conventions-0","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions","content":"The shell, function, script, template and naming rules this repo holds itself to, and the gates that enforce them."},"4075":{"id":"/project-conventions-1","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Project conventions"},"4076":{"id":"/project-conventions-2","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"File header"},"4077":{"id":"/project-conventions-3","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Functions"},"4078":{"id":"/project-conventions-4","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Tests"},"4079":{"id":"/project-conventions-5","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#ingress","content":"Ingress"},"4080":{"id":"/project-conventions-6","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#the-one-exception","content":"The one exception"},"4081":{"id":"/project-conventions-7","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#the-bazel-target-form","content":"The Bazel target form"},"4082":{"id":"/project-conventions-8","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#scripts","content":"Scripts"},"4083":{"id":"/project-conventions-9","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"Style and language"},"4084":{"id":"/project-conventions-10","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#the-log-contract","content":"The log contract"},"4085":{"id":"/project-conventions-11","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#repo-root-tools-and-probes","content":"Repo root, tools, and probes"},"4086":{"id":"/project-conventions-12","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#templates","content":"Templates"},"4087":{"id":"/project-conventions-13","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#the-twin-payloads","content":"The twin payloads"},"4088":{"id":"/project-conventions-14","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"The single-copy payloads"},"4089":{"id":"/project-conventions-15","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#how-terraform-reads-them","content":"How Terraform reads them"},"4090":{"id":"/project-conventions-16","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#the-push-and-the-svg","content":"The push and the SVG"},"4091":{"id":"/project-conventions-17","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#dollar-escaping","content":"Dollar escaping"},"4092":{"id":"/project-conventions-18","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#names-and-flags","content":"Names and flags"},"4093":{"id":"/project-conventions-19","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#template-names","content":"Template names"},"4094":{"id":"/project-conventions-20","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#flags-on-the-clis-and-runners","content":"Flags on the CLIs and runners"},"4095":{"id":"/project-conventions-21","page_id":"/project-conventions","type":"heading","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"url":"/project-conventions#github-auth-and-the-runtime","content":"GitHub auth and the runtime"},"4096":{"id":"/project-conventions-22","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"**What's on this page**"},"4097":{"id":"/project-conventions-23","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"The file header and the function docstring block both gates require"},"4098":{"id":"/project-conventions-24","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Ingress rules: what runs from a shell and what is a Bazel target"},"4099":{"id":"/project-conventions-25","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"One language per file, the shared log contract, and the payload copies the templates share"},"4100":{"id":"/project-conventions-26","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Names, flags, and the style guide shell follows"},"4101":{"id":"/project-conventions-27","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"**What this enables**"},"4102":{"id":"/project-conventions-28","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Writing a script that passes `//:lint` and `//tests:doc_coverage` first try"},"4103":{"id":"/project-conventions-29","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Matching the house style instead of inventing a local one"},"4104":{"id":"/project-conventions-30","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Shell follows the Google Shell Style Guide."},"4105":{"id":"/project-conventions-31","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#project-conventions","content":"Branch rules live in branching.md; the Bazel harness lives in\ncontribute/building-with-bazel.md and\ncontribute/test-with-bazel.md."},"4106":{"id":"/project-conventions-32","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#file-header","content":"Every `scripts/**/*.sh` file starts with a `# ## ` header that names the file and says whether it is an entry point or a sourced library."},"4107":{"id":"/project-conventions-33","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"Every function carries this block immediately above the definition:"},"4108":{"id":"/project-conventions-34","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#functions","content":"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."},"4109":{"id":"/project-conventions-35","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Every function under `scripts/` is named by an asserting test under `tests/`: one suite per\nmodule, plus one test per entry-level function that drives the whole script (`tests/bats/\nentry-fns.bats` is where those live)."},"4110":{"id":"/project-conventions-36","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a name is missing, and `tests/doc_coverage.sh` when a\n`# @function` block is missing or incomplete."},"4111":{"id":"/project-conventions-37","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#tests","content":"Tests are hermetic: docker, ssh, curl, git and the other external tools resolve through\n`tests/bats/tool_stubs`."},"4112":{"id":"/project-conventions-38","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-one-exception","content":"Bazelisk is the only ingress, with one exception: `scripts/setup-host.sh`. That script is\nthe **stack host's** bootstrap — it installs Bazelisk, the shell/Starlark lint toolchain\n(shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier) and Terraform — so it has to run before Bazel exists, and\nstays the single documented `bash scripts/setup-host.sh`."},"4113":{"id":"/project-conventions-39","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-one-exception","content":"On a laptop you do not run it: the contributor container or\n`bazelisk run //scripts:test-in-image` brings the same pinned toolchain in from\n`images/dev/` instead\n(contribute/devcontainer.md)."},"4114":{"id":"/project-conventions-40","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-one-exception","content":"`--repair` is the other half of\nthat exemption: leftover Docker bind-mounts in Bazel's runfiles tree make\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` die creating `prod-stack.runfiles` (`Permission\ndenied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`) before any stack script can run."},"4115":{"id":"/project-conventions-41","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-one-exception","content":"On Linux it also writes a systemd-resolved drop-in (`1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8`) and\nruns `tailscale set --accept-dns=false` so host `git pull` still resolves\n`github.com` when MagicDNS is the only nameserver."},"4116":{"id":"/project-conventions-42","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-bazel-target-form","content":"Everything else is a Bazel target:"},"4117":{"id":"/project-conventions-43","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-bazel-target-form","content":"`make` only delegates. An operator CLI runs as `bazelisk run //scripts:<stem> -- <args>`\n(`<stem>` = the file name under `scripts/`); `make help` lists the shims."},"4118":{"id":"/project-conventions-44","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"No `eval`. Prefer `\"${var}\"`, `[[ ]]`, and `$(...)`."},"4119":{"id":"/project-conventions-45","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"One language per file where reasonable. A bash, python, or awk program that lives\ninside another file is its own tested file, imported with `source`, `cat` + `sh -c`\n/ `ssh`, `python3 file.py`, `awk -f`, or Terraform `file()` / `templatefile()`."},"4120":{"id":"/project-conventions-46","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"Keep:"},"4121":{"id":"/project-conventions-47","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"1–3 line awk"},"4122":{"id":"/project-conventions-48","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"usage heredocs"},"4123":{"id":"/project-conventions-49","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"Dockerfiles"},"4124":{"id":"/project-conventions-50","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"YAML data"},"4125":{"id":"/project-conventions-51","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"docs fences"},"4126":{"id":"/project-conventions-52","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"`python3 -m webbrowser`"},"4127":{"id":"/project-conventions-53","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#style-and-language","content":"Remote probes are POSIX and self-contained under\n`scripts/lib/probes/`; the host `cat`s them and tests run the file with `sh`."},"4128":{"id":"/project-conventions-54","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-log-contract","content":"Operator CLIs source `scripts/lib/common.sh` and do not redefine `log` / `die` /\n`skip`. Grouping:"},"4129":{"id":"/project-conventions-55","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-log-contract","content":"`section` prints `== name ==`"},"4130":{"id":"/project-conventions-56","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-log-contract","content":"`phase` prints `-> text`"},"4131":{"id":"/project-conventions-57","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-log-contract","content":"`ok` prints `ok: text`"},"4132":{"id":"/project-conventions-58","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-log-contract","content":"`summary` prints `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed`\nplus `  failed: …` and optional `next: cmd`"},"4133":{"id":"/project-conventions-59","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-log-contract","content":"`JSON_MODE` set sends prose to stderr. CLIs without `--json` unset `JSON_MODE` after source.\nKeep prefixes `warning:` / `note:` / `error:`."},"4134":{"id":"/project-conventions-60","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#repo-root-tools-and-probes","content":"A script never runs another repo script by relative path, and never assumes the cwd is the\ncheckout: it resolves the repo root with `resolve_checkout` in `scripts/lib/common.sh` and then\n`cd`s there (see the harness doc), because a Bazel sandbox may only hold a copy."},"4135":{"id":"/project-conventions-61","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#repo-root-tools-and-probes","content":"That helper tries `${REPO_ROOT:-}` / `${BUILD_WORKSPACE_DIRECTORY:-}` / `${GITHUB_WORKSPACE:-}` /\n`${PWD}` / `${SELF_DIR}/..` in order, and a candidate counts only when it holds the marker file\n(`docker-compose.yml` unless the caller names another) and its `pwd -P` is not under a\n`bazel-out/`, `bazel-bin/`, `execroot/`, or `*.runfiles` tree — an explicit `REPO_ROOT` is\nthe one candidate trusted as given, which is how the Bats runner names the copy under test."},"4136":{"id":"/project-conventions-62","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#repo-root-tools-and-probes","content":"Nothing proving out is one `error: unresolvable repo root: ...` line on stderr and exit\n`5`, never a guessed cwd."},"4137":{"id":"/project-conventions-63","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#repo-root-tools-and-probes","content":"External tools are probed with `command -v` and reported as \"not on PATH\", not assumed.\n`require_tool` skips locally and fails under `CI=true`."},"4138":{"id":"/project-conventions-64","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#repo-root-tools-and-probes","content":"A probe-style CLI takes its target as a flag (`--url`) rather than a hard-coded host, and reports\n\"nothing answered\" as a result rather than a crash, so one run works from the host and from inside\na workspace and still answers the question the operator asked. Its suite stubs the transport."},"4139":{"id":"/project-conventions-65","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-twin-payloads","content":"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload."},"4140":{"id":"/project-conventions-66","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and holds the single-copy\npayloads both templates consume:"},"4141":{"id":"/project-conventions-67","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`"},"4142":{"id":"/project-conventions-68","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`vscode-alt.svg`"},"4143":{"id":"/project-conventions-69","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`notes-setup.sh`"},"4144":{"id":"/project-conventions-70","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`tmux.conf`"},"4145":{"id":"/project-conventions-71","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`grok-build-terminal.sh`"},"4146":{"id":"/project-conventions-72","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`"},"4147":{"id":"/project-conventions-73","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`grok-build-delegation.SKILL.md`"},"4148":{"id":"/project-conventions-74","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-learn.SKILL.md`"},"4149":{"id":"/project-conventions-75","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-skillify.SKILL.md`"},"4150":{"id":"/project-conventions-76","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-create-skill.SKILL.md`"},"4151":{"id":"/project-conventions-77","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`stack-skills.sh.tftpl`"},"4152":{"id":"/project-conventions-78","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-bazel-first.SKILL.md`"},"4153":{"id":"/project-conventions-79","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-bazel-first.owned_files_check.py`"},"4154":{"id":"/project-conventions-80","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-bazel-first.glob_budget.py`"},"4155":{"id":"/project-conventions-81","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-strong-types.SKILL.md`"},"4156":{"id":"/project-conventions-82","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-strong-types.type_baseline.py`"},"4157":{"id":"/project-conventions-83","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-trunk-based-repo.SKILL.md`"},"4158":{"id":"/project-conventions-84","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-micro-plan.SKILL.md`"},"4159":{"id":"/project-conventions-85","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py`"},"4160":{"id":"/project-conventions-86","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl`"},"4161":{"id":"/project-conventions-87","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`coder-agents.md`"},"4162":{"id":"/project-conventions-88","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`mcp-cache-proxy.json`"},"4163":{"id":"/project-conventions-89","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-single-copy-payloads","content":"`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`"},"4164":{"id":"/project-conventions-90","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#how-terraform-reads-them","content":"Markdown and JSON are read with `file()` (chomp the markdown); shell helpers that Terraform must\nnot re-interpolate are also `file()` (so `$folder` stays `$folder`); `gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`\nis `templatefile()` because it interpolates `${github_auth_id}`."},"4165":{"id":"/project-conventions-91","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#how-terraform-reads-them","content":"`coder_script` bodies are sibling `.tftpl` files next to each `main.tf` (`code-server.sh.tftpl`,\nand on `devcontainer` also `dind-bootstrap.sh.tftpl`) rendered with `templatefile()`."},"4166":{"id":"/project-conventions-92","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#how-terraform-reads-them","content":"`gh-token-refresh.sh.tftpl`, `stack-skills.sh.tftpl` (the body that seeds the\n`ez-learn` / `ez-skillify` / `ez-create-skill` trio) and `stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl` (the\nbody that seeds the four-command repository family `ez-bazel-first` / `ez-strong-types` /\n`ez-trunk-based-repo` / `ez-micro-plan`, each python helper into a `scripts/` subdir\nbeside its `SKILL.md`)\nare the three that live in\n`templates/common/` instead, so both twins share one copy of them."},"4167":{"id":"/project-conventions-93","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-push-and-the-svg","content":"The SVG stays small enough that the resulting data URL fits Coder v2.36's 256-character\n`workspace_apps.icon` column."},"4168":{"id":"/project-conventions-94","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#the-push-and-the-svg","content":"The push\n(`scripts/push-template.sh` through `template_push_one` in `scripts/lib/template-push.sh`)\nstages every common payload beside each template's staged `main.tf` copy, which is the only place the\nserver-side Terraform can read them from, so editing the common copy changes both\ntemplates; a staged copy that lacks any one of them fails the push early by design."},"4169":{"id":"/project-conventions-95","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#dollar-escaping","content":"Dollar escaping differs across those two layers:"},"4170":{"id":"/project-conventions-96","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#dollar-escaping","content":"A `templatefile()` `.tftpl` treats `$$` as an escaped `$` everywhere\n(`$name` or `$${name}`), and leaves `$((` arithmetic alone."},"4171":{"id":"/project-conventions-97","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#dollar-escaping","content":"An HCL heredoc in `main.tf` (`<<-EOT`) only escapes `$${name}` to `${name}`; a lone `$`\nis already literal, so shell arithmetic is `$((...))` with one dollar."},"4172":{"id":"/project-conventions-98","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#dollar-escaping","content":"`$$((` is two dollars, and Coder runs `coder_script` bodies as `bash -c`, which\nexpands `$$` to the PID. Do not wrap `templatefile()` in `chomp()`."},"4173":{"id":"/project-conventions-99","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#template-names","content":"`devcontainer` — the dev-container template (directory `templates/devcontainer`, Coder\ntemplate name `devcontainer`), always backed by its own DinD sidecar. Its sibling is\n`workspace`: directory `templates/workspace`, template name `workspace`, the plain\ndev container with the optional `dind` parameter."},"4174":{"id":"/project-conventions-100","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#template-names","content":"`devcontainer.json` — a file the *application repository* carries, and the one thing the\n`devcontainer` template builds. `coder_devcontainer.repo` in\n`templates/devcontainer/main.tf` sets no `config_path`, so `@devcontainers/cli` on the\nDinD sidecar searches the clone for `.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` (or a\n`devcontainer.json` anywhere inside it)."},"4175":{"id":"/project-conventions-101","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#template-names","content":"This repo ships a `.devcontainer/` of its own —\nthe **contributor** one for a laptop and CI, built from `images/dev/Dockerfile` — and it\nis not that nested kind: `.devcontainer/contributor-env` alongside it makes\n`probe_repo` answer a miss, so a Coder workspace of this repo stays on the `workspace`\ntemplate instead of building its own container inside its own DinD sidecar."},"4176":{"id":"/project-conventions-102","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#template-names","content":"Other repositories keep carrying their own nested dev-container files, and those are still a\nprobe hit. See contribute/devcontainer.md."},"4177":{"id":"/project-conventions-103","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#flags-on-the-clis-and-runners","content":"`additional_repos` — the `--additional-repos` flag on the new-workspace CLI, and the\n`coder_parameter` of the same name that receives its value in both templates\n(`mutable = true`, default empty): extra git URLs for the workspace's clone folders,\ngiven as `url1,url2` or one URL per line. The flag exists on\n`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` only - the stack runners (`dev-stack`,\n`prod-stack`) have no `--additional-repos` (their flags are `-p`, `-f`, `-e`,\n`--tail`, `--follow`, `--json`, `-y`, `-h`)."},"4178":{"id":"/project-conventions-104","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#flags-on-the-clis-and-runners","content":"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name)."},"4179":{"id":"/project-conventions-105","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#flags-on-the-clis-and-runners","content":"The env file follows the runner: the dev project takes `dev-stack.env`\nbeside the compose file when one exists, and any other project gets none, so a run aimed at\nthe stable stack never picks up the dev host ports; `prod-stack` names no env file unless\n`-e` does, and compose then reads the `.env` beside the compose file itself."},"4180":{"id":"/project-conventions-106","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#flags-on-the-clis-and-runners","content":"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough)."},"4181":{"id":"/project-conventions-107","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#flags-on-the-clis-and-runners","content":"`bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."},"4182":{"id":"/project-conventions-108","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#github-auth-and-the-runtime","content":"GitHub auth is Coder External Auth, id `github` by default (Coder's injected\nGitHub App when coder.env is absent). A custom OAuth app in coder.env keeps\nits `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_ID` (commonly `primary-github`); push-template.sh\nforwards that id. There is no GitHub-token template variable and no PAT in\nany agent env map."},"4183":{"id":"/project-conventions-109","page_id":"/project-conventions","tags":["conventions","shell","style","contributing"],"type":"text","url":"/project-conventions#github-auth-and-the-runtime","content":"Runtime stays `docker compose`: Bazel runs the CLIs that drive Compose and never talks to a\ndaemon, a Spark box, or a model registry by itself. No Kubernetes layer."},"4184":{"id":"/public-access","page_id":"/public-access","type":"page","content":"Public subdomains","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access"},"4185":{"id":"/public-access-0","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access","content":"Serving the same seven services on public names over real TLS, additively, with the LAN shape left intact."},"4186":{"id":"/public-access-1","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Public subdomains"},"4187":{"id":"/public-access-2","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The path a public request takes"},"4188":{"id":"/public-access-3","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"The composed names"},"4189":{"id":"/public-access-4","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#getting-the-naming-right","content":"Getting the naming right"},"4190":{"id":"/public-access-5","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"The parts that compose a name"},"4191":{"id":"/public-access-6","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-three-states-of-stack_public_hosts","content":"The three states of `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS`"},"4192":{"id":"/public-access-7","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"What `bootstrap` changes when the names are set"},"4193":{"id":"/public-access-8","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#traefik-configuration","content":"Traefik configuration"},"4194":{"id":"/public-access-9","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-file-to-drop-in","content":"The file to drop in"},"4195":{"id":"/public-access-10","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#the-lines-that-have-to-match","content":"The lines that have to match"},"4196":{"id":"/public-access-11","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#timeouts-and-websockets","content":"Timeouts and WebSockets"},"4197":{"id":"/public-access-12","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#what-stays-lan-only","content":"What stays LAN-only"},"4198":{"id":"/public-access-13","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Checking the other machine"},"4199":{"id":"/public-access-14","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Authelia"},"4200":{"id":"/public-access-15","page_id":"/public-access","type":"heading","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Two follow-ups, both by hand"},"4201":{"id":"/public-access-16","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"**What's on this page**"},"4202":{"id":"/public-access-17","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The path a public request takes, from the browser to the container"},"4203":{"id":"/public-access-18","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The naming rule, and what `auto` composes from the parts you set"},"4204":{"id":"/public-access-19","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"What `bootstrap.sh` changes when the names are set"},"4205":{"id":"/public-access-20","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The Traefik file-provider config the other box needs, pasted as-is"},"4206":{"id":"/public-access-21","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Checking the other machine (including the Hermes WebSocket), Authelia, and the two follow-ups left by hand"},"4207":{"id":"/public-access-22","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"**What this enables**"},"4208":{"id":"/public-access-23","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Exposing the stack without breaking the LAN routes anyone at home uses"},"4209":{"id":"/public-access-24","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Telling a router problem apart from an Authelia problem when a name fails"},"4210":{"id":"/public-access-25","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Putting the seven routers on the Traefik box without guessing its timeouts"},"4211":{"id":"/public-access-26","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"The default shape is LAN-only."},"4212":{"id":"/public-access-27","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` additionally serves the same seven services on public names over\nreal TLS, each name composed from the parts you set once. It is purely additive: every LAN\nroute keeps working."},"4213":{"id":"/public-access-28","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#public-subdomains","content":"Grafana and Infisical are the two new services on this path: the observability backends\nbehind Grafana (Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, Mimir) stay unexposed, because\nSECURITY.md keeps\nmetrics and logs off the LAN, let alone the WAN."},"4214":{"id":"/public-access-29","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"TLS terminates at Traefik. The `router` sidecar holds no certificate."},"4215":{"id":"/public-access-30","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Cloudflare proxies the name (orange cloud) in front of Traefik. WebSockets are on\nthere by default, which is what the Hermes Chat tab needs."},"4216":{"id":"/public-access-31","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"The router's whole job is picking an upstream from the Host header, and an unmatched\nHost answers `421` rather than a guess, so a mistyped or rewritten Host fails loudly\ninstead of landing in the wrong service. A rewritten Host matches no server block\nhere, so it also arrives as `421`."},"4217":{"id":"/public-access-32","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Kasm is the only hop that speaks TLS to its origin. `proxy_ssl_verify` is off there\nbecause the certificate is the one Kasm's installer generates and reissues. Hermes,\nlike Homepage, Coder and LiteLLM, is plain HTTP at the origin (`http://hermes:9119`)."},"4218":{"id":"/public-access-33","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Nothing in this path replaces the LAN routes: `https://<lan-ip>/` still serves the\ndashboard on the self-signed cert, `https://<lan-ip>:3001` still serves Coder's\nown TLS listener, and `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` still serves Hermes."},"4219":{"id":"/public-access-34","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"A workspace **app** link (the **VS Code Web** button is the only app this stack still\nproxies) rides the coder row of that table. `coder_app.code_server` sets\n`subdomain = false`, so its URL keeps the `/@<owner>/<ws>.<agent>/apps/<slug>` path\nform and goes to `coder:3000` on its way to the editor on `localhost:13337` inside the\nworkspace. A 502 on that link is an app-lane failure and `litellm-gmktecbeast.` took\nno part in it."},"4220":{"id":"/public-access-35","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-path-a-public-request-takes","content":"Every `location` adds `X-Evo-Proxy` with the upstream name, and `always` is what\nmakes it survive a 502. Read it beside `X-Coder-Request-Id`, which Coder's own proxy\nsets on an answered request: neither header means the request stopped at Traefik or\nCloudflare, and `421` means the Host header matched no server block here."},"4221":{"id":"/public-access-36","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"These are what the shipped defaults compose. The operator sets the parts, not these\nstrings: the grammar is `<service>-<environment>-<suffix>.<base>`, always ONE label\nunder the zone."},"4222":{"id":"/public-access-37","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                      | Router upstream |\n| ------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `homepage:3000` |","table":"table-0"},"4223":{"id":"/public-access-38","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                            | Router upstream               |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `coder:3000` - plain listener |","table":"table-0"},"4224":{"id":"/public-access-39","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                                | Router upstream                         |\n| ----------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |\n| `coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `coder:3000` - the dev stack's own name |","table":"table-0"},"4225":{"id":"/public-access-40","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                           | Router upstream                              |\n| ------------------------------ | -------------------------------------------- |\n| `kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `https://kasm:4443` (`proxy_ssl_verify off`) |","table":"table-0"},"4226":{"id":"/public-access-41","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                              | Router upstream |\n| --------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `litellm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `litellm:4000`  |","table":"table-0"},"4227":{"id":"/public-access-42","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                             | Router upstream |\n| -------------------------------- | --------------- |\n| `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `hermes:9119`   |","table":"table-0"},"4228":{"id":"/public-access-43","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                              | Router upstream                       |\n| --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------- |\n| `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `grafana:3000` - the observability UI |","table":"table-0"},"4229":{"id":"/public-access-44","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"| Name                                | Router upstream                                                |\n| ----------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `infisical-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` | `infisical:8080` - the vault, up only with `INFISICAL_ENABLED` |","table":"table-0"},"4230":{"id":"/public-access-45","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"The dev row is what `bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack -- up` composes for the\n`evo-t1-dev` project: that command applies `STACK_ENV_LABEL=dev` when neither the shell\nnor the env file already sets a label, and only for that project, so\n`--project-name evo-t1-review` gets none."},"4231":{"id":"/public-access-46","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"A label goes on every name, so that stack's dashboard is `dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`\ntoo, and all seven need their own Traefik routers (see below)."},"4232":{"id":"/public-access-47","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` never invents a label."},"4233":{"id":"/public-access-48","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"Hermes is the same additive hop as the other four. Traefik forwards\n`http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with the original Host, and the router sends that\nHost to `http://hermes:9119`."},"4234":{"id":"/public-access-49","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-composed-names","content":"The LAN URL `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` stays. HTTP basic auth on the dashboard stays\ntoo; Authelia is the extra public gate. See hermes.md."},"4235":{"id":"/public-access-50","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"`scripts/lib/domain.sh` owns the grammar and every part is normalized before it is\njoined, so a value copied out of a browser bar cannot leak into an origin or a\n`server_name`:"},"4236":{"id":"/public-access-51","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"`STACK_DOMAIN_BASE` (default `overeazy.io`) is the zone the edge terminates TLS for;\n`STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX` (default `gmktecbeast`) is this box's label, shared by every\nname."},"4237":{"id":"/public-access-52","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"`STACK_CODER_NAME` / `STACK_KASM_NAME` / `STACK_LITELLM_NAME` / `STACK_HERMES_NAME` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` / `STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (defaults `coder` / `kasm` / `litellm` /\n`hermes` / `grafana` / `infisical`) are the service prefixes. `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME`\nnames the observability UI only: the metrics backends it fronts get no key of their\nown, and stay unexposed.\n`STACK_DASHBOARD_NAME` is empty by default, which puts the dashboard on the zone apex."},"4238":{"id":"/public-access-53","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"`STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (default `infisical`) is the seventh prefix and one of the two\nbehind a compose profile - `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` is the other, whose `grafana` service\nsits under `profiles: [\"observability\"]`."},"4239":{"id":"/public-access-54","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"The vault's three containers start only under `profiles: [\"secrets\"]`, which\n`INFISICAL_ENABLED` (off as shipped) selects, so a stack with the flag off has no vault\nbehind that name and answers `502`."},"4240":{"id":"/public-access-55","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"`domain.sh` reads no flag for either of them, so both names still compose and still\nreach `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS`, which is the safer half of that trade."},"4241":{"id":"/public-access-56","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"The router sends it to `http://infisical:8080` - the container port, not the published\none - and the mapping is `${INFISICAL_PORT:-8182}:8080`, published plainly rather than\nloopback-bound for the same host-gateway reason the search lane gives for `:8181`."},"4242":{"id":"/public-access-57","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"`STACK_ENV_LABEL` is empty for the stable stack and sits **after** the service prefix.\nTwo reasons: the one-label rule below rules the dotted form out anyway, and\n`classify_public_entry` in `scripts/lib/bootstrap.sh` routes an entry by its service\nprefix, so `coder-dev-box.example.com` is Coder while `dev-coder-box.example.com`\nfalls through to the dashboard."},"4243":{"id":"/public-access-58","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-parts-that-compose-a-name","content":"The shape is load-bearing: each name is **one label under the zone**, and each service\nprefix joins it with a **hyphen**. A wildcard certificate covers exactly one label, so\nthe dotted two-label form (`coder.gmktecbeast.`) has no certificate at the public edge:\nthe browser aborts with `ERR_SSL_VERSION_OR_CIPHER_MISMATCH` and Traefik never sees the\nrequest. Measured on this box - `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` completes the handshake\nwith the zone's `*.overeazy.io` cert, while the dotted form fails with a TLS alert 40\nand presents no certificate at all."},"4244":{"id":"/public-access-59","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-three-states-of-stack_public_hosts","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` has three states, and only the middle one composes:"},"4245":{"id":"/public-access-60","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-three-states-of-stack_public_hosts","content":"empty is LAN-only;"},"4246":{"id":"/public-access-61","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-three-states-of-stack_public_hosts","content":"`auto` composes the seven names from the parts above;"},"4247":{"id":"/public-access-62","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-three-states-of-stack_public_hosts","content":"a comma-separated list is used verbatim, which is how you pin a name by hand (leave\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE` empty, or that key keeps re-deriving)."},"4248":{"id":"/public-access-63","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-three-states-of-stack_public_hosts","content":"A part that cannot be a DNS label composes nothing: that service is left out of the list\nrather than sent as an empty entry, and a missing zone keeps the whole stack LAN-only."},"4249":{"id":"/public-access-64","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"},"4250":{"id":"/public-access-65","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"},"4251":{"id":"/public-access-66","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_URL`, the card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`. Compose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the\nHost and Origin the Chat tab checks. The same prefix rule fills `STACK_GRAFANA_URL`\nfrom a `grafana-*` or `grafana.*` entry, and compose copies that into\n`HOMEPAGE_VAR_GRAFANA_URL`, the observability card's `href`;"},"4252":{"id":"/public-access-67","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_INFISICAL_URL`, from a `infisical-*` or `infisical.*` entry, the vault card's\n`href`. Its badge reads down while `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off, which is the flag\nsaying so rather than a broken link;"},"4253":{"id":"/public-access-68","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an upgraded `CODER_ACCESS_URL` (the public coder name);"},"4254":{"id":"/public-access-69","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"an extended `HOMEPAGE_ALLOWED_HOSTS` - homepage validates the incoming Host against\nthat exact-match list, so an unlisted name fails its host check;"},"4255":{"id":"/public-access-70","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"a Coder certificate whose DNS SANs now carry the public names alongside the existing\nones. The LAN cert keeps working; in public mode the browser never sees it, since\nTraefik presents real ones;"},"4256":{"id":"/public-access-71","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, which records that the names are derived. Under `auto`\nthe parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output only, so a list\nedited by hand there is ignored and bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear. It clears itself on the LAN-only and explicit-list paths;"},"4257":{"id":"/public-access-72","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"the router's vhost table, whose token set is exactly the seven vhosts it answers\n(`__EVO_HOST_INFISICAL__` among them: a name that will not compose refuses the render\nrather than writing half a table): `proxy/router.conf.template` is rendered into\n`proxy/router.gen.conf` (gitignored) and `STACK_ROUTER_CONF=router.gen.conf` is\nwritten, because compose mounts `./proxy/${STACK_ROUTER_CONF:-router.conf}`."},"4258":{"id":"/public-access-73","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"The `warn_missing_router_hosts` check runs against that rendered file, so a name the\ntable does not answer is reported before the recreate rather than as a `421` after\nit."},"4259":{"id":"/public-access-74","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"Set the naming parts in `.env` and put `auto` in `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` (`.env.sample`\ndocuments every part and shows the explicit-list shape beside it), and in\n`dev-stack.env` too when a dev stack runs beside the stable one."},"4260":{"id":"/public-access-75","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-bootstrap-changes-when-the-names-are-set","content":"Then re-run `bootstrap` and recreate the stack you switched. The commands are in the\nREADME."},"4261":{"id":"/public-access-76","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-file-to-drop-in","content":"Nothing in this repo configures Traefik. This is the file the other box needs: drop it\nin the directory that box's `providers.file.directory` watches and Traefik picks it up\nwithout a restart."},"4262":{"id":"/public-access-77","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-file-to-drop-in","content":"The shape below is the one Traefik v2 and v3 both read; the entryPoint name, the\ncertificate and the middleware name have to match what that box already defines, so treat\nthose three as the values to align, not as new requirements."},"4263":{"id":"/public-access-78","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-lines-that-have-to-match","content":"`passHostHeader: true` is the line that makes this work at all. Without it Traefik\nsends the upstream URL's host, no `server_name` matches, and this box answers `421`."},"4264":{"id":"/public-access-79","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-lines-that-have-to-match","content":"**A published dev stack needs its own routers here.** The `dev-stack.env` behind\n`bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack` moves every published port by 10000 (the recipe is\nin the\nREADME),\nso that stack's `router` answers on `18080` (`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` default 8080) and\nits names carry the environment label\n(`coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`)."},"4265":{"id":"/public-access-80","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-lines-that-have-to-match","content":"A dev name is a different `Host` rule and a different backend port: add a router per\ndev name to the file above aimed at `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:18080`, and the DNS\nrecords for those names have to exist too, or the browser fails before this box is\never asked."},"4266":{"id":"/public-access-81","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-lines-that-have-to-match","content":"`authelia@file` is a placeholder for the Authelia middleware that box already\ndeclares. Name it exactly as that box names it - a middleware created by labels is\n`authelia@docker`, and a name Traefik cannot resolve fails the router rather than\nserving it unauthenticated. The six interactive names (dashboard, Coder, Kasm, Hermes,\nGrafana, Infisical) carry it; LiteLLM must not."},"4267":{"id":"/public-access-82","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#the-lines-that-have-to-match","content":"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."},"4268":{"id":"/public-access-83","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#timeouts-and-websockets","content":"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."},"4269":{"id":"/public-access-84","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#timeouts-and-websockets","content":"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder, hermes, grafana and infisical 300s, LiteLLM 600s,\nKasm 3600s - so Traefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all seven at\n`3600s` is the simple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway."},"4270":{"id":"/public-access-85","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#timeouts-and-websockets","content":"To cap each tier separately, declare one `loadBalancer` service per tier, each\npointing at the same `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080` with `passHostHeader: true` and\nits own transport (`evo-t1-coder` / `evo-t1-hermes` / `evo-t1-grafana` /\n`evo-t1-infisical` at `300s`, `evo-t1-litellm` at `600s`, `evo-t1-kasm` at `3600s`),\nand point each router at its own service."},"4271":{"id":"/public-access-86","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#timeouts-and-websockets","content":"If a session dies at about a minute with the page still loaded, check the\nclient-facing side too: an entryPoint's `transport.respondingTimeouts.readTimeout`\n(default 60s) and `idleTimeout` (default 180s) apply to the browser-to-Traefik hop."},"4272":{"id":"/public-access-87","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#what-stays-lan-only","content":"SearXNG is deliberately absent. It stays LAN-only on `:8181`, which is where the\nworkspaces reach it through `host.docker.internal`; putting it behind Traefik would\nexpose a search box that has no authentication of its own."},"4273":{"id":"/public-access-88","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"This repo cannot verify any of it; check it on the Traefik box."},"4274":{"id":"/public-access-89","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"A router per name pointing at `http://<this-box-lan-ip>:8080`, including\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`. That backend\nis `:8080` with the original Host, the same as the other names - not `:9119` and not\n`:3005`. A router aimed at a container port skips this\nbox's vhost. The file in Traefik configuration is that\nconfig; diff what the box runs against it."},"4275":{"id":"/public-access-90","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"The original Host header preserved - a rewritten one comes back as `421`."},"4276":{"id":"/public-access-91","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"Any authentication as the Traefik router's middleware. Authelia already fronts\n`hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` (the name 302s), the same interactive policy as\nthe homepage and Coder names, and `grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` belongs behind\nthat same policy: Grafana is an interactive login, not an API endpoint, and the name\nfronts the metrics bundle."},"4277":{"id":"/public-access-92","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"The sidecar forwards what it receives and authenticates nothing, so a name whose\nrouter skips the auth middleware reaches its container the same way the LAN does."},"4278":{"id":"/public-access-93","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"The app path is behind that policy like the rest of its name, so an expired session\nsurfaces there too."},"4279":{"id":"/public-access-94","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"DNS records for all seven names, each one label deep and Cloudflare-proxied (orange\ncloud). WebSockets are on there by default. A missing record shows up as a DNS\nerror, a rewritten Host as `421`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm and LiteLLM resolve\nthat way and complete the TLS handshake, checked from this box; the Hermes\nname is the same zone and the same proxy. The Grafana and Infisical records are new\nwith their names and have not been measured from this box, so check them there."},"4280":{"id":"/public-access-95","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#checking-the-other-machine","content":"WebSocket `Upgrade` forwarded by Traefik and Authelia on the Hermes router. The\nChat tab uses `/api/pty` and `/api/ws`, and Hermes sends a keepalive every 20\nseconds. A proxy that drops `Upgrade` loads the page and then fails the tab."},"4281":{"id":"/public-access-96","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"`https://gmktecbeast.overeazy.io/`, `coder-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`kasm-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`, `hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`,\n`grafana-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` and `infisical-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` 302 to\n`https://authelia.overeazy.io/`. Homepage, Coder, Kasm, Hermes, Grafana and Infisical\nare interactive sessions, and the Hermes policy is already in place."},"4282":{"id":"/public-access-97","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Covering a newly named host is an Authelia-side config choice; nothing here adds or\nrequires a policy, so the Grafana and Infisical policies are the two this box cannot\nassume exist."},"4283":{"id":"/public-access-98","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Hermes is not an API bypass: it stays behind that interactive policy, and after\nAuthelia it asks for its own HTTP basic auth. Both prompts on the public URL are\nexpected."},"4284":{"id":"/public-access-99","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Grafana and Infisical are the same shape: Authelia first, then each one's own login."},"4285":{"id":"/public-access-100","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Put LiteLLM behind it and every client breaks: LiteLLM authenticates from the\n`Authorization` header, and header-carried API traffic cannot pass an interactive\nsign-in. Either leave `litellm-gmktecbeast` outside any policy or add an explicit\nbypass - and then `LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` is the only credential in front of an\nendpoint that went from LAN-only to reachable WAN-wide (see\nSECURITY.md)."},"4286":{"id":"/public-access-101","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#authelia","content":"Workspace agents never reach Authelia either way. The template rewrites the agent URL\nto `http://host.docker.internal:3002`, the plain listener published through\n`CODER_AGENT_TUNNEL_PORT`, so agent traffic stays on the box."},"4287":{"id":"/public-access-102","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Workspaces created before public mode existed need a template re-push plus a\nrestart, to pick up the template's `coder_agent_url` variable (default\n`http://host.docker.internal:3002`). That variable is what keeps agents dialing the\nlocal plain listener whatever the access URL says."},"4288":{"id":"/public-access-103","page_id":"/public-access","tags":["public","tls","traefik","authelia","hermes","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/public-access#two-follow-ups-both-by-hand","content":"Kasm stores its connection endpoints in its own setup database (first-boot wizard or\nadmin UI), so the endpoint has to change there too or streams keep dialing the LAN\naddress."},"4289":{"id":"/spark-monitoring","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"page","content":"Spark monitoring","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring"},"4290":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-0","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring","content":"Enrolling the three DGX Sparks into the Prometheus stack: the two exporter containers a box needs, the one opt-in key, the plan/prove/install commands, how the boxes reach Prometheus through file_sd, and what this change leaves unproven."},"4291":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-1","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Spark monitoring"},"4292":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-2","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"What lands on a Spark"},"4293":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-3","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"The flag"},"4294":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-4","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#the-commands","content":"The commands"},"4295":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-5","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"Plan (the default, and the one that is safe anywhere)"},"4296":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-6","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"Prove (`--status`)"},"4297":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-7","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"Install (`--apply`)"},"4298":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-8","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#the-rest","content":"The rest"},"4299":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-9","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#--restart","content":"`--restart`"},"4300":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-10","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#--box-one-box-number","content":"`--box` (one box number)"},"4301":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-11","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#--json","content":"`--json`"},"4302":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-12","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"`--targets`"},"4303":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-13","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"How the boxes get into Prometheus"},"4304":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-14","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"What this change does not prove"},"4305":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-15","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#failure-modes","content":"Failure modes"},"4306":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-16","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#ssh-reachability","content":"ssh reachability"},"4307":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-17","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#a-port-already-in-use","content":"A port already in use"},"4308":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-18","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#an-exporter-running-but-not-answering","content":"An exporter running but not answering"},"4309":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-19","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","type":"heading","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"url":"/spark-monitoring#a-panel-stays-empty","content":"A panel stays empty"},"4310":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-20","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"**What's on this page**"},"4311":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-21","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The two containers that make a Spark a Prometheus target, and the fact that neither\nexists until you run `--apply`"},"4312":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-22","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The one key that decides whether the fleet is monitored at all, and what reads it"},"4313":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-23","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The three commands - plan, prove, install - plus one box, JSON, and the target document"},"4314":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-24","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"How the boxes get into Prometheus, and why an unenrolled fleet reads as zero Sparks"},"4315":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-25","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"What this change does not prove: the DCGM tag, the profiling counters, NVLink"},"4316":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-26","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The three failures worth recognising from the CLI's own words"},"4317":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-27","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"**What this enables**"},"4318":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-28","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Reading GPU utilisation, temperature, power and framebuffer pressure per Spark in\nGrafana instead of ssh-ing into a box and running `nvidia-smi`"},"4319":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-29","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Catching a box left on an old exporter tag before someone asks why its panels differ\nfrom its neighbours'"},"4320":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-30","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Enrolling or un-enrolling the fleet with one key, without editing a compose file"},"4321":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-31","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"Run every command here in a **shell on the stack host**."},"4322":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-32","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The probe reaches each box over ssh, and a Coder workspace container is not a shell that\nresolves the LAN names the `SPARKn_HOST` keys hold - the workspace-side run in the examples\nbelow shows exactly what that costs."},"4323":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-33","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#spark-monitoring","content":"The whole bundle this depends on sits behind the Compose profile\n`profiles: [\"observability\"]`, and observability.md is the page for\nthe six services that do the scraping."},"4324":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-34","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"Two containers per box, both created by this CLI and by nothing else:"},"4325":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-35","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"| Container             | Image                                      | Port (.env key, default)          | What it answers                                                                          |\n| --------------------- | ------------------------------------------ | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `spark-node-exporter` | `quay.io/prometheus/node-exporter:v1.12.1` | `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (9100) | CPU, memory, disk, filesystems, network, conntrack, file descriptors, `node_processes_*` |","table":"table-0"},"4326":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-36","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"| Container             | Image                                                      | Port (.env key, default)          | What it answers                                                                                                                            |\n| --------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `spark-dcgm-exporter` | `nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` | `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (9400) | The `DCGM_FI_*` GPU series - utilisation, memory copy, framebuffer used and free, temperatures, clocks, power, PCIe replays, remapped rows |","table":"table-0"},"4327":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-37","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"**Neither of them exists until you run `--apply`.** A box that has never been enrolled\nruns no exporter, so Prometheus scrapes nothing and the Spark panels stay empty."},"4328":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-38","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"This change installs nothing on its own: no deploy, no `up`, no `refresh-configs` creates\na container on a Spark. `--apply` is the only flag that touches a box, and it says so in\nits own header line before the first one (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:327`)."},"4329":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-39","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"Both containers are idempotent by name (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:79-80`), so a second\n`--apply` starts what is already there rather than creating a duplicate on a port the\nfirst one holds."},"4330":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-40","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"The DCGM exporter is not run on the image's own field list:"},"4331":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-41","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"the CLI ferries `observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv` to\n`/opt/evo-t1/dcgm/custom-counters.csv` on the box"},"4332":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-42","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"bind-mounts it read-only (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:97-98`,\n`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:239`), so the `DCGM_FI_*` series Prometheus sees are the ones\nthis repo reviews"},"4333":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-43","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-lands-on-a-spark","content":"Editing that CSV and re-running `--apply` reports `updated`, and the CLI then tells you to\n`--restart` so the running exporter rereads it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:696-697`)."},"4334":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-44","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`.env.example:629`. It is read by **this CLI**, at `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:212` - not\nby Compose. Nothing in `docker-compose.yml` consults it, because the exporters do not run\nin the stack's project at all; they run on the boxes. With the key off:"},"4335":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-45","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`--apply` skips every box instead of installing a container nothing scrapes\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:541`)"},"4336":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-46","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`--targets` prints the empty document `[]` (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:302`)"},"4337":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-47","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`--status` still probes, because knowing what a box runs never needs the flag."},"4338":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-48","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"The other keys, all in `.env.example`:"},"4339":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-49","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 607)"},"4340":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-50","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 612)"},"4341":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-51","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` (line 621, the pin above, and the knob you correct if the\nregistry refuses that tag)"},"4342":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-52","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"The host-side exporters this bundle adds live beside them - `OBS_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT`\n(583), `OBS_CADVISOR_PORT` (590), `OBS_BLACKBOX_PORT` (596), `OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT`\n(601) - and `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` (635) is empty by default; nothing requires it."},"4343":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-53","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"Those four host exporters publish on the **host's loopback only**, so a probe of them runs\nin a host shell, the same as Prometheus' own UI on `PROMETHEUS_PORT`."},"4344":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-54","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#the-flag","content":"The fleet itself comes from `SPARK_HOSTS` or the `SPARKn_HOST` keys and is resolved by\n`derive_spark_hosts` (`scripts/lib/spark.sh:67`); this CLI derives no hostname, and every\nbox line it prints names the host, because three Sparks of which one is quiet is otherwise\nindistinguishable from three Sparks of which one is down."},"4345":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-55","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"No flag, or `--dry-run`, is a plan: it opens no ssh session, starts no container, and\nprints what is configured plus the exact `docker run` lines `--apply` would ferry."},"4346":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-56","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"This is the run to make before pointing Prometheus at three boxes you have not looked at."},"4347":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-57","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"Captured output, with the CLI's placeholder host names replaced by `<host>`:"},"4348":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-58","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"That run was made from a Coder workspace, which is why the fleet fell back to the\nplaceholder trio."},"4349":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-59","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#plan-the-default-and-the-one-that-is-safe-anywhere","content":"From the host shell, with the `SPARKn_HOST` keys in the checkout's `.env`, the same\ncommand prints three real boxes and no host warning, and adds the `docker run` lines the\nplan promises (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:412-425`)."},"4350":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-60","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"This is the only mode that looks at a box. It ferries one read-only probe\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh`) base64-encoded through `probe_transport`\n(`scripts/lib/spark.sh:173`), which prints twelve `KEY=value` lines per box:"},"4351":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-61","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"docker availability"},"4352":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-62","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"both exporters' state, port and image"},"4353":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-63","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"the DCGM counter count"},"4354":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-64","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"the GPU count and models"},"4355":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-65","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"The probe never pulls, never writes, never installs, and exits 0 even when the box has\nnothing on it - the CLI decides what is a warning."},"4356":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-66","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"Captured output from this workspace, host names elided:"},"4357":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-67","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"A box that answers prints `ok:` lines instead of that warning - docker available with\nthe probe version, `node_exporter is running on :<port> (<image>)`, `DCGM exporter is\nrunning on :<port>, tag <image>`, and a `note:` with the counter count, the GPU count and\nthe models (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:612-644`)."},"4358":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-68","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#prove---status","content":"**Not verified from a live box**: no Spark was reachable from where these commands ran, so\nthe answering-`box` table above is quoted from those source lines, not from an observed\nrun. The unreachable form is what was actually measured."},"4359":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-69","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"The only mutator. Set `SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED=true` first or every box is skipped."},"4360":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-70","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"Per box it reports each container as `created`, `started` (an existing one), or\n`already running`, and the counter list as `same`, `updated` or `absent`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:662-700`)."},"4361":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-71","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"Exit status is 0 when every box answered or is simply not set up yet, and non-zero only\nwhen a mutator ran and a box failed - a Spark with nothing installed is a `warning:`, not\na failure, because that is the state a freshly imaged box is in."},"4362":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-72","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"`--apply` was **not run for this page**: it creates containers on real hardware, and no\nSpark answered ssh from where the other commands ran. Read the plan output above as the\ncontract for what it does."},"4363":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-73","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"The `--` separator is not decoration. Measured:"},"4364":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-74","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#install---apply","content":"with exit 2 - Bazel claims the flag as its own build option and the script never starts.\nEvery flag this CLI takes goes after `--`."},"4365":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-75","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--restart","content":"`docker restart` on both containers per box (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:332`). This is what\nyou run after editing `observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv`, since a running exporter\nkeeps the field list it started with."},"4366":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-76","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--restart","content":"A box with no such container is a `warning:` naming `--apply`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:725`)."},"4367":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-77","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--box-one-box-number","content":"Restricts any mode to one box number (`1`, `2`, `3`). A number outside the resolved fleet\nis refused with the fleet size and the resolved list."},"4368":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-78","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--box-one-box-number","content":"Measured, plan mode with `--box 1`:"},"4369":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-79","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--json","content":"One JSON object on **stdout** and nothing else; every `ok:` / `warning:` / `summary:` line\nroutes to stderr while `--json` is set, so `| jq` sees a document."},"4370":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-80","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--json","content":"Verified: stdout was one line, 1397 bytes, parseable, carrying:"},"4371":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-81","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--json","content":"`mode`, `host_source`, `monitor_enabled`, both images, both ports, `ssh_port`"},"4372":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-82","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--json","content":"a `boxes` array with one object per box (`probed`, `docker`, `node_exporter`, `dcgm`,\n`dcgm_counter_count`, `gpu_count`, `gpu_names`, `probe_version`, `verdict`, `detail`)"},"4373":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-83","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--json","content":"a `summary` object"},"4374":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-84","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"The `file_sd` document, to stdout, writing nothing. With the flag off, verified output:"},"4375":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-85","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"With it on, four groups per box:"},"4376":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-86","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"the node_exporter on `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT`"},"4377":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-87","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"the DCGM exporter on `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT`"},"4378":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-88","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"the two blackbox probe URLs on `SPARK_HTTP_PORT` (`/health` and `/v1/models`)"},"4379":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-89","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"Every group carries the three labels the rule files and the dashboards group by - `box`,\n`exporter`, and `hostname` - so the GPU rows, the host rows and the probe rows of one box\nall land on the same identity."},"4380":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-90","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#--targets","content":"`--help` prints the usage block from the script's own header. The modes are mutually\nexclusive: `--status cannot be combined with --apply; pick one mode`."},"4381":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-91","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"`--targets` prints the document; it never writes it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:340`)."},"4382":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-92","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"The file lives at `observability/spark-targets/sparks.json` on the **stack host**, and that\npath is gitignored (`.gitignore:76`) precisely because it names your boxes - three\naddresses are not something this repo commits."},"4383":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-93","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"`//scripts:spark-monitor` is what generates it on the host, from the three ports above."},"4384":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-94","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"Prometheus reads that one file with `file_sd_configs` at `refresh_interval: 60s` on all\nthree Spark jobs (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:149`, `:171`, `:195`), so a\nchanged file is picked up within a minute without a restart."},"4385":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-95","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"Because four kinds of target share the file, each job keeps only its own with a relabel\n`action: keep` on the `exporter` label - without those filters the two probe URLs would be\nscraped as metric endpoints and every box would carry two permanently failing targets."},"4386":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-96","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"The two blackbox jobs take the address from the same file and pass it as `__param_target`,\nso the target label carries the box rather than the prober."},"4387":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-97","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"The prober itself is scraped as a service of its own\n(`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:219`), which is what makes\n`up{job=\"blackbox\"}` exist for `BlackboxProbeGap` to fire on."},"4388":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-98","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"Spark alerts stamp `tier=spark` and `spark=<hostname>`."},"4389":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-99","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"The consequence of the empty document above: **an unenrolled fleet reads as zero Sparks.**"},"4390":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-100","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"No group in `sparks.json` means no target, which means no `up` series, which means an\nempty panel rather than a red one - the symptom looks like \"nothing is wrong\" from\nGrafana."},"4391":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-101","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#how-the-boxes-get-into-prometheus","content":"Check it with `--targets` before you blame the dashboard."},"4392":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-102","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"Say these plainly, because each one costs someone an afternoon if they meet it\nunexpected:"},"4393":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-103","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**The DCGM tag has never been pulled.** `nvcr.io` answers 404 to an anonymous token, so\nthe pin `nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` was not verifiable\nupstream the way the other three were. The first `--apply` against a box is what proves\nit, and a wrong tag surfaces there as the registry's own error: `--apply` prints\ndocker's reason rather than swallowing it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`,\n`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`), so you read what the registry said. Correct the tag in\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` and re-run - it is a key, not a code change."},"4394":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-104","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**`--status` prints the tag a box is really running**, taken from the container\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh:111-114`), and says so when it is not the pin this\nCLI would install (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:644`). That `note:` line is how a stale pin\ngets caught: the box that was enrolled last spring keeps answering on the old tag and\nnobody looks."},"4395":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-105","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**Profiling counters return nothing without `SYS_ADMIN`.** Every `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` field\nneeds the exporter started with `--cap-add SYS_ADMIN` (plus\n`NVreg_RestrictProfilingToAdminUsers=0` on the box), and this CLI does not pass that cap\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:239`). A box answers those fields with no rows at all rather\nthan an error - `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` being absent from a scrape is the expected result, not\na broken exporter (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:98-99`)."},"4396":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-106","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#what-this-change-does-not-prove","content":"**A box without NVLink logs nothing for NVLink fields.** The per-link\n`DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_*_L<n>` counters are commented out of the shipped field list for\nthat reason: on a GPU that exposes no NVLink each one is a per-field error in the\nexporter log on every scrape (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:72-81`). The\naggregate `DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_BANDWIDTH_TOTAL` ships and reads zero."},"4397":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-107","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#ssh-reachability","content":"**ssh will not reach a box.** In `--status` this is a `warning:` naming the ssh error, and\nthe exit status stays 0 (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:566-576`): a box that is off, or one\nthis shell has no credentials for, is a not-yet state."},"4398":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-108","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#ssh-reachability","content":"Under `--apply` or `--restart` the same box is a `fail_item` and the run exits non-zero,\nbecause a fleet where two of three boxes were enrolled is precisely the drift this CLI\nexists to remove."},"4399":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-109","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#ssh-reachability","content":"Check `SPARK_SSH_USER` (default: your login name, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:101`) and\n`--port`, and run it from the host shell - a workspace cannot resolve the LAN names."},"4400":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-110","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#ssh-reachability","content":"If the box drops packets rather than refusing, you see\n`ssh was cut off after <n>s (timeout(1)\nguard)`; on a host without `timeout(1)` the CLI warns up front that the round trips run\nuncapped."},"4401":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-111","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-port-already-in-use","content":"**A port is already taken on a box.**"},"4402":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-112","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-port-already-in-use","content":"`--apply` reports it per container as `<name> is not running (<state>): <what docker said>`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`) - the text after the colon\nis docker's, so `port is already allocated` names the process holding 9100 or 9400."},"4403":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-113","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-port-already-in-use","content":"The containers are idempotent by name, so this is a *different* process on the port, not\nthis CLI double-booking itself."},"4404":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-114","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-port-already-in-use","content":"Move the port with `SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` / `SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` - the target\ndocument and the scrape follow the key - and re-run `--apply`."},"4405":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-115","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#an-exporter-running-but-not-answering","content":"**An exporter container exists and is not answering.** Two shapes, and they read\ndifferently:"},"4406":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-116","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#an-exporter-running-but-not-answering","content":"A container that `docker ps` does not list (exited, restarting) comes back\n`absent`, so the CLI says `node_exporter is absent -- Prometheus scrapes <host>:9100 and\ngets nothing` and points at `--apply --box <n>`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:616-624`)."},"4407":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-117","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#an-exporter-running-but-not-answering","content":"A container that *is* listed but does not serve still counts as running for\nnode_exporter, and shows up one line later on the DCGM side: the probe counts counters\nfrom a live local scrape of `:<port>/metrics` and only falls back to the field list baked\ninto the image when that scrape returns nothing\n(`scripts/lib/probes/spark-monitor.sh:196-206`), so a `note:` whose counter count matches\nthe image's default list rather than this repo's file means the exporter is up and not\nserving your field list."},"4408":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-118","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#an-exporter-running-but-not-answering","content":"`--restart` is the first thing to try; `--apply` re-ferries the CSV if the file changed."},"4409":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-119","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-panel-stays-empty","content":"When a Spark panel stays empty, work outward in this order:"},"4410":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-120","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-panel-stays-empty","content":"`--status` (is the container there and serving)"},"4411":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-121","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-panel-stays-empty","content":"`--targets` (is the box in the document at all)"},"4412":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-122","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-panel-stays-empty","content":"the scrape on the host through `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check`"},"4413":{"id":"/spark-monitoring-123","page_id":"/spark-monitoring","tags":["observability","prometheus","spark","dcgm","operate"],"type":"text","url":"/spark-monitoring#a-panel-stays-empty","content":"Symptoms that outgrow those three commands belong in troubleshooting.md."},"4414":{"id":"/troubleshooting","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"page","content":"Troubleshooting","breadcrumbs":["Docs"],"tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting"},"4415":{"id":"/troubleshooting-0","page_id":"/troubleshooting","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"type":"text","url":"/troubleshooting","content":"Symptom-first list of what breaks on this box: the error string, the cause, the recipe, and what the fix costs."},"4416":{"id":"/troubleshooting-1","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#troubleshooting","content":"Troubleshooting"},"4417":{"id":"/troubleshooting-2","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#stack-bring-up","content":"Stack bring-up"},"4418":{"id":"/troubleshooting-3","page_id":"/troubleshooting","type":"heading","tags":["troubleshooting","operate","errors"],"url":"/troubleshooting#not-a-directory-are-you-trying-to-mount-a-directory-onto-a-file---missing-compose-bind-mount-source","content":"`not a directory: Are you trying to mount a 