sk cannot be\ndeclared inside a template. The CLI therefore creates it at run time and, when it cannot reach the\nCoder API -- the usual case outside a logged-in host shell -- prints the exact command and continues\nwith exit 0."],[2653,"**`--writable` matters more than it looks.** The official image sets\n`HERMES_WRITE_SAFE_ROOT=/opt/data`, so a file-tool write anywhere else is refused\noutright and no approval card even appears."],[2708,"**`agent-swarm`** is one of the nine Spark aliases, and one of four - with\n**`agent-explore`**, **`agent-review`** and **`agent-swarm-fast`** - that the LiteLLM\nsession pin does not cover: same model, same three boxes, same baked `xhigh`, but each\nrequest is placed on the least-busy healthy box."],[2642,"**`check` is the first thing to run when an answer looks impossible.** It reports:"],[329,"**`coder_env` is the only provably-earlier file write.** The agent's\n`writeSecretFiles` (`agent.go`) runs before any start script, `MkdirAll 0700` then\n`WriteFile 0600`. It takes a string value, so it can carry a payload, and it writes\ninto the agent's secret directory rather than anywhere you name."],[330,"**`CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` is the per-script scratch the agent already hands you.**\n`agent/agentscripts/agentscripts.go` appends `CODER_SCRIPT_DATA_DIR` and\n`CODER_SCRIPT_BIN_DIR` to every script's environment, which is a writable place to\nassemble something across scripts."],[2083,"**`Ctrl+V` on a secure origin is not a paste.** VS Code binds terminal paste to\n`Ctrl+Shift+V` on Linux and `Cmd+V` on macOS, so `Ctrl+V` reaches the pane as\nthe `\\x16` byte (readline's quoted-insert, vim's visual-block). The seed\nre-binds nothing there on purpose: a TUI in the pane owns that chord."],[5261,"**`litellm` alone is `down` with a 401**: the scrape sends the master key as a bearer\ntoken that it reads from a file, `/etc/prometheus-secrets/litellm_token`\n(`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml`), which Compose writes from\n`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` when it creates the container."],[2069,"**`Prefix m` is the wheel, not a copy route.** One press hands the pointer to\ntmux so the wheel walks pane history, the next hands it back."],[4038,"**`pull` leaves `.env` alone and says so.** A failed export is an `error:` line naming\nthe file as untouched, and the verb exits 1. An export that comes back empty is a\n`warning:` and the same untouched file. A key the vault does not carry, or carries\nempty, is a `warning:` per key and the existing value in `.env` stays exactly as it was"],[4041,"**`rotate` stops at the first half.** If the vault refuses the write, `.env` is left\nuntouched and the verb exits 1 - the stack keeps running on the old value. The reverse\ncase prints the recovery in the error line: if the vault took the new value and the env\nwrite failed, re-run `pull` to converge."],[1710,"**`run` can call a helper, and a helper that `exit`s is safe.** `run` forks, so\n`run --separate-stderr usage_error '…'` followed by `[ \"${status}\" -eq 2 ]` works even\nthough `usage_error` ends in `exit 2`. Pass a real command (`bash \"${SCRIPT}\" …`) when\nthe script is what is under test."],[1709,"**`run` keeps stdout in `$output`.** With `run --separate-stderr` there is no `$stdout`:\n`$output` is stdout and `$stderr` is stderr. An assertion written against `$stdout`\ncompares against an empty string and fails, which is how a whole suite of real\nassertions can look like a broken harness."],[2098,"**`terminal.limited-color` during an agent turn.** A tool command launched by a\nrunning Grok session inherits `NO_COLOR=1`, `FORCE_COLOR=0`, `CLICOLOR=0` and\n`CARGO_TERM_COLOR=never` from that session, so a `grok doctor` run from inside\nan agent turn reads color as off. The `grok` process itself and the tmux server\ncarry none of those variables, so run it from a plain terminal for the real\ncapability: the line reads `color truecolor` and `themes all`."],[2103,"**`terminal.ssh-wrap`** is a recommendation about the computer you type `ssh`\non, since the wrapper has to sit in front of that client: run\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there, or `grok wrap ssh <host>` per connection. It\nforwards copies to the local clipboard and restores terminal modes when the\nlink drops. `/settings` -> **Show contextual hints** -> **SSH wrap** silences\nthe tip."],[2104,"**`unavailable` tmux and runtime probes.** `tmux.version`,\n`tmux.client-features` and the `runtime.*` lines come back `unavailable`\nwhenever Doctor has no interactive client of its own, which covers a piped run\nand a run from an agent tool call. Read them by hand (`tmux -V`,\n`tmux display -p '#{client_termfeatures}'`) or from `/doctor` in a live\nsession."],[4036,"**`up` cannot be stopped by it.** Compose never contacts Infisical: it interpolates `.env`,\nwhich is a file on disk, and whether the three containers are part of the project is a\nprofile decision made from one key, not a network result. The runner's own pull runs before\nthat interpolation and a failed one costs a line, not the bring-up - `warning: the env file\nwas not refreshed from Infisical; <file> keeps the credentials it already has - retry with\nbazelisk run //scripts:infisical -- pull` - and compose recreates against what the file\nalready held."],[2099,"**`voice.no-input-device`.** Expected in a workspace. The container has no\ncapture device at all (`/dev/snd` and `/proc/asound` are absent) and no\nrecorder on PATH."],[3976,"**0** - the verb did its job. Always for `status` and `check`: a check that fails on a\nstack that never adopted Infisical is a check nobody runs. `run` exits 0 on the\nflag-off refusal too, having printed the `note:`; every other mutating verb exits 5."],[3977,"**1** - a real failure. The CLI errored, a read-back hash disagreed, a `rotate` target\nis not a managed key. A `rotate` or `run` that names no key and no command lands here as\nwell, on an `error:` line."],[3978,"**2** - an unknown subcommand, always raised before any work starts."],[3979,"**5** - refused, because `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off or a prerequisite is empty. Printed\nas a `note:` that names the key to set."],[316,"**A `#!` line does not help.** The body still becomes\n`execve(env, [env, bash, -c, body])`; the interpreter line is content inside the\nargument."],[1513,"**A Bats file is cheap; a script is not.** `tests-for tests/bats/common.bats` returns five targets.\n`tests-for scripts/lib/common.sh` returns 60 of the 67 test targets on this checkout, because every\nsuite declares `//scripts:doc_sources`, which is every `scripts/**.sh` (66 targets reach that\ngroup). That is the honest graph answer: editing a broadly sourced script genuinely re-runs nearly\nthe whole suite set, and knowing it before you start is what decides whether to run the derived\nlist or the aggregate."],[4396,"**A box without NVLink logs nothing for NVLink fields.** The per-link\n`DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_*_L<n>` counters are commented out of the shipped field list for\nthat reason: on a GPU that exposes no NVLink each one is a per-field error in the\nexporter log on every scrape (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:72-81`). The\naggregate `DCGM_FI_DEV_NVLINK_BANDWIDTH_TOTAL` ships and reads zero."],[91,"**A bug fix ships with its proof.** The test that fails before the change and passes after it,\nwith the reproduction's failing output quoted in the pull request. \"Cannot reproduce\" is a\nfinding to post on the issue, and the issue stays open."],[5411,"**A checked-in `config/flags.yaml`** once there are enough switches that a reviewer cannot\nhold them, or when one tree runs in several environments with different settings. Copy\n`config/flags.yaml.example` from this repository. Keep the schema - `default`, `owner`,\n`description`, `expires`, `environments`, optional `stacks` overrides - and keep the real\nprocess environment winning, so CI and an operator can still force a value."],[972,"**A defined-but-empty key crash-loops the server.** Any `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\ncounts as a configured provider even when its value is empty, and an empty key\nmakes `coder server` exit at boot with `external auth provider \"\" doesn't have a\nvalid id` - which the compose restart policy turns into a crash loop."],[2125,"**A fourth command rides that same body: `/ez-micro-plan`.** It converts nothing - it is\nthe parent's dispatch protocol for a fan-out of subagents: a repo-local plan at\n`.grok/plans/<slug>/plan.md`, the change split into micro tasks, layered context briefs\nbeside it, and a check that refuses a plan too narrow to fill the session's subagent\nslots."],[57,"**A hotfix branches from `main` and lands on `main` first** — every environment deploys\n*from* `main` (or from a tag cut on `main`), so there is no `staging` or `production` branch\nto patch and then remember to merge back."],[3949,"**A key the shell could not use as an identifier** - empty, starting with a digit, or holding a\ncharacter outside `[A-Za-z0-9_]`, which is also where blank and comment lines fall out - is\nskipped silently. Nothing is printed for it; the only line that says how many keys landed is the\nsuccess line, `ok: infisical: loaded N secret keys into <home>/.infisical/agent.env`, and it\ncounts what the publish loop actually wrote."],[2129,"**A leftover `/learn` seed retires itself.** This skill was seeded under the name\n`learn` until it moved, and a copy left standing keeps offering that old name beside\n`/ez-learn` for the life of the volume; there is no bundled `learn` for it to hide,\nbecause no published source names one."],[72,"**A local commit is free, a push is not.** Nothing leaves the machine until you push, so err\ntoward committing, including a `WIP:` checkpoint before a step that might break something\ngreen."],[4401,"**A port is already taken on a box.**"],[4264,"**A published dev stack needs its own routers here.** The `dev-stack.env` behind\n`bazelisk run //scripts:dev-stack` moves every published port by 10000 (the recipe is\nin the\nREADME),\nso that stack's `router` answers on `18080` (`STACK_PROXY_HTTP_PORT` default 8080) and\nits names carry the environment label\n(`coder-dev-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io`)."],[813,"**A token that authenticates still has to be granted.** A credential that was never\nGRANTED the repository fails clone and push, and reads prove nothing about write: a private\nrepository hidden from the credential answers 403 or 404 on both lanes, and a public one\nanswers a read probe with 200 for any authenticated caller, so a read-only credential\npasses a read test while every write to that repo is denied."],[3948,"**A value whose opening single quote does not close on its own line** - a PEM or a multi-line\ncertificate is the shape - is dropped whole. Its continuation lines are swallowed until one\ncarries a quote, because publishing them would invent keys out of secret bytes. One note per\nboot, naming neither key nor value: `infisical: skipped a secret whose value spans more than\none line; only single-line secrets are published.` The body carries no other shape, so a\ncredential of that shape has to reach a workspace by some path other than this fetch."],[4063,"**A vault value is never sourced in the shape the CLI wrote it.** Measured against\n`infisical/infisical:v0.167.10` with CLI v0.45.8: `secrets export --format=dotenv` wraps\neach value in single quotes and leaves an embedded quote raw, so a secret carrying an\napostrophe closes its quote early and the rest of the line is shell - sourcing it either\naborts or runs the tail as a command."],[4040,"**A workspace still starts.** The boot fetch is best-effort: with the flag off, with\n`INFISICAL_PULL_ON_START` off, with no CLI in the image (the golden image bakes the\npinned release, so that is an image older than the pin), with a refused Universal Auth\nlogin, with an empty export, or with a refused publish, it exits 0 and the workspace\ncontinues its startup. `agent.env` keeps whatever the last start wrote, since the body\nstages beside the destination and moves in one step rather than truncating first. A vault\noutage has never once stopped a workspace."],[1389,"**Abbreviations.** A term listed in `docs/includes/abbreviations.md` as `*[TERM]: definition`\nis wrapped wherever it appears in prose. Hover or focus shows the definition; click or Enter\nopens it in a dialog. Text inside a link, a heading, inline code, or an existing term is left\nalone. Keep each definition tooltip-sized."],[5257,"**Absent from the list entirely**: the job is not in\n`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml`. Add it there and let the collector read it\nagain: that file arrives through a bind mount, which follows the *path*, so the edit is\nalready inside the container and only the running process is behind it."],[1110,"**Action cache.** An action's outputs are keyed on its inputs and command line, so an\nunchanged suite replays its cached PASS instead of re-running Bats."],[3367,"**Add an auto-reboot watchdog.** A box sitting in the initramfs with dropbear\nlistening is the recoverable state; rebooting on a timer destroys the\nrecovery window and can turn one bad boot into a loop. `-I` bounds the\nsession, and the listener stays."],[1817,"**After install:** the UI is on `https://<host>:4443`. Both ports serve TLS with the\nself-signed certificate Kasm's own installer generates, and plain `http://` reaches\nneither port."],[3821,"**Alertmanager's UI is published on loopback only** (`OBS_ALERTMANAGER_PORT`, 9093),\nfor the same reason Coder's `:2112` metrics port is: that UI can silence a firing\nalert, so an unauthenticated silencer on the LAN is a way to make a real fault\ninvisible. Read it from a shell on the stack host."],[3584,"**Alloy, not Loki, reads the disk.** Loki has no Docker credential and no host mount.\nThe only container that sees the host's container log directory is Alloy, and it sees\nit read-only. That directory is `${STACK_DOCKER_LOG_DIR:-/var/lib/docker/containers}`,\nmounted at the same path inside the collector, so a host whose daemon keeps its logs\nsomewhere else (rootless Docker does) needs that key set or the collector tails\nnothing."],[3596,"**An alert a person builds in Grafana goes to the same Alertmanager.** The provisioned\ncontact point is `type: alertmanager` at `http://alertmanager:9093`\n(`observability/grafana/provisioning/alerting/stack.yaml:28`), not a webhook, so it\nspeaks `/api/v2/alerts` and Alertmanager's grouping, inhibition and silences apply to\nGrafana-originated alerts exactly as they do to Prometheus'. One place holds the\nsilences, whichever engine fired."],[2073,"**An app inside the pane drives the machine clipboard over OSC 52; the browser\nbuild drops it.** `set -g set-clipboard on` with `set -g allow-passthrough on`\nstay in the seed for that one route: vim's `+` register, and anything else that\nemits the escape, reach the machine clipboard the way a native terminal hands them\nto it."],[2033,"**An attached client is not the same thing as a viewer.** code-server's pty\nhost keeps the pty open after the browser side of a tab goes away, so the tmux\nclient stays attached, keeps its session's `session_attached` at 1, and\nreceives nothing at all."],[3820,"**An empty `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL` is the shipped state, and it pages nobody.**\nThe rules still fire and stay readable on Prometheus's `/alerts` page and on Grafana's\nAlerting page; only the delivery step is silent. Set the key to a webhook URL when you\nwant a person told, and never put a URL that carries a credential in `.env`."],[1514,"**An empty result means run everything.** Nothing changed against HEAD, or the change touches no\nfile the graph tracks, and `scope` says so and points at `//:test-fast`."],[4405,"**An exporter container exists and is not answering.** Two shapes, and they read\ndifferently:"],[2650,"**An interactive session needs a real tty** (the TUI draws on a pty compose allocates\nfrom your terminal), so `chat` and `shell` refuse from a pipe or a cron line - that is\nwhat `ask` is for."],[85,"**Announce a long fix.** For anything past an obvious edit, a short comment saying what is\nabout to change lets a maintainer redirect the work early and keeps two people off the same\nfile."],[2648,"**Approvals are the same gate, inline.** A dangerous command stops in your tty with\n`[o]nce | [s]ession | [a]lways | [d]eny`, deny by default."],[465,"**Autostop is disabled by default.**"],[2754,"**Both keyless switches stay off, and that is the part that keeps the promise.**\n`agent/web_search_registry.py` resolves a backend in five steps, and its last\none is an anonymous free-tier ring - `exa`, `parallel`, `firecrawl`,\n`keenable` - reached with no key at all."],[62,"**Branch first, then edit.** The work branch is cut as the first action of the change, off the\ntip of `main`."],[504,"**Build repo's dev container** (`use_devcontainer`, default `true`) is immutable:\nbuilding a dev container or not is a different topology. With Yes the editor lives in\nthe dev container; with No, in the plain container."],[558,"**Clones a non-empty Git repository field** into `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`\n(in `devcontainer`, onto the `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>` bind)."],[65,"**Commit each unit as it lands.** A unit is what a reviewer reads as one step:"],[5410,"**Compose `profiles:`** when what you are hiding is a whole service."],[2878,"**Contribute** covers the gates a change runs through"],[976,"**Create `coder.env` only for a *custom* GitHub OAuth app**, and fill EVERY key -\n`ID` names the provider and `TYPE` says what kind it is. Restart coder to apply\nthe file."],[981,"**Device-flow login is not the App install.** Private repos need the Coder GitHub\nApp installed from\ngithub.com/apps/coder/installations/new\n(the workspace \"Install GitHub App\" button), then a restart so `GH_TOKEN` remints."],[1391,"**Diagrams.** A ` ```mermaid ` fence renders through `Mermaid`, follows the light/dark switch,\nand shows the chart text beside a parse error rather than leaving a blank box."],[404,"**Docker-in-docker** (`dind`, Yes/No radio, immutable)."],[312,"**Each body is its own `execve`, so each gets its own ceiling.** Splitting a body into\ntwo `coder_script` resources relocates the wall; it does not raise it. On this server\n(v2.36) the start scripts fan out concurrently in an errgroup and `ready` is set only\nafter all of them return, so splitting does not reduce a peak either.\n`coder_script_order` needs Coder `>= v2.39`."],[3365,"**Edit `/etc/crypttab` or the LUKS headers.** The volumes are read to decide\nwhether the boot can open them. Key slots, passphrases and headers are never\nwritten, so this CLI cannot be the thing that destroys a key."],[414,"**Editor colour theme** (`vscode_color_theme`, free-form, mutable, default\n`Default Dark Modern`). Written as `workbench.colorTheme` in that workspace's\ncode-server User settings, where it wins over the shared editor-settings overlay."],[3366,"**Enroll a TPM.** `systemd-cryptenroll` is a different design with a\ndifferent failure mode - a firmware update that changes the PCR policy seals\nthe box with nobody in the loop - and it belongs in its own change."],[1392,"**Fenced code.** Every fence needs a language, checked by the same contract test, because the\nhighlighter and the copy button both key off it."],[1816,"**First boot:** `https://<host>:3000` - run the install wizard once."],[2542,"**From the phone.** The same file runs inside the Hermes container, whose image has\nno bazelisk, straight off the identical-path checkout mount:"],[406,"**Git repositories (additional)** (`additional_repos`, textarea, mutable, default\nempty)."],[405,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`, input, mutable). The Terraform default is empty\n(`workspace`) or the coder/coder demo (`devcontainer`); after a push that forwards\n`.env` `REPO_URL` (this stack's own repo in the sample) the form prefills that URL.\nAnything you type is cloned into the workspace on the next start."],[503,"**Git repository** (`repo_url`) is mutable: switching repository is a re-clone plus a\ndevcontainer build, not a new topology the way `workspace`'s DinD toggle is."],[3595,"**Grafana lists the rules because the datasource says to.** The Prometheus datasource is\nregistered `alerting: true` (`observability/grafana/provisioning/datasources/stack.yaml:26`);\nwithout that flag the rules answer only on Prometheus' own `/alerts` page and never\nappear under Alerting -> Rules. Delivery still belongs to Alertmanager."],[415,"**Grok approval mode** (`grok_permission_mode`, dropdown, mutable, default\n`always-approve`). How much that workspace's Grok sessions stop and ask before they\nact, rendered as `[ui] permission_mode` in that workspace's `~/.grok/config.toml`.\nThe dropdown offers exactly four values:"],[2039,"**Grok Build** keeps its own session, keyed off the repository: the helper walks up\nfrom the terminal's folder with `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` (or uses the\nterminal's `$PWD` outside a clone), names the session `grok-build-<folder>-<hash>`\nafter that root, and runs `grok --cwd <repo root>` inside it. A terminal opened\nanywhere inside the clone rejoins the one session the repository shares, and starts\nGrok Build at the repository root."],[412,"**Grok config extras** (`grok_config_extra`, textarea, mutable, default empty).\nWhatever you type is appended verbatim, under a comment header, to that workspace's\nrendered `~/.grok/config.toml`. That file is this workspace's own in either profile\nmode, so the extras reach it alone."],[411,"**Grok default model** (`grok_default_model`, dropdown, immutable, default `agent`)."],[407,"**Grok profile** (`grok_profile_mode`, dropdown, immutable, default `shared`):"],[413,"**Grok theme** (`grok_theme`, free-form, mutable, default `grokbuild`). Rendered as\n`[ui] theme` in that workspace's `~/.grok/config.toml`; the literal `auto` renders the\nCLI's `groknight`/`grokday` pair instead of a fixed theme. Each workspace keeps its own."],[1109,"**Hermetic sandbox.** `test --spawn_strategy=local,sandboxed` and `build\n--spawn_strategy=sandboxed`: an action sees its declared inputs and its runfiles tree, not\nthe whole checkout. That is why a suite declares its stubs and payload files as `data` and\nwhy a script resolves its repo root with `resolve_checkout` (`scripts/lib/common.sh`) instead of\ntrusting the cwd."],[2195,"**How a session learns its own ceiling.** `[subagents] max_concurrent` is the per-session\nnumber, and `sampling_limit` is left unset because it defaults to that same number - one\nvariable, so the two caps cannot drift apart. The parent reads it once per session and\ndispatches to it:"],[5379,"**How is anything deployed here?** Look, do not assume:"],[2246,"**How it is dispatched, and what the check prints.** `plan_check.py` is the gate, and it\nis the same file in both skill homes:"],[2278,"**How one is selected.** Four layers name an agent definition, and they are not the same\nknob:"],[983,"**How the token reaches a workspace.** It arrives as `GH_TOKEN` and `GITHUB_TOKEN`\nin the agent environment, and that pair is a snapshot: Coder mints it when the\nworkspace starts and never refreshes it, so it expires about an hour later while\nthe workspace keeps running."],[2230,"**How the work is cut.** `/ez-micro-plan` is the parent's protocol for splitting one\nmulti-file change across that fleet, and it writes its own document, separate from Grok\nBuild's `/plan`: `/plan` lives at `~/.grok/sessions/<cwd>/<session-id>/plan.md`, is the\nPlan Mode preview, and dies with the session, while this one is repo-local at\n`.grok/plans/<slug>/plan.md` and is what the parent and every child read from."],[2078,"**Into the pane: `Ctrl+Shift+V` or right-click, on a secure context.** Both\narrive bracketed (`ESC[200~ ... ESC[201~`) in a bash terminal and in a tmux\npane alike, and a three-line paste arrives whole without a warning dialog."],[2640,"**It enters the running container rather than starting a second one.** Hermes keeps\nits sessions and memory in a SQLite store under `/opt/data`, and two containers on one\ndata directory is the documented way to corrupt it - the WAL sidecars and the lock\nonly mean something inside one container."],[2693,"**It reports, it does not gate.** Exit 0 on a clean bill (warnings allowed), 1 when a\ncheck failed, 2 on a bad invocation, 5 when it cannot find a checkout.\n`hermes-shell -- check` stays the thing that decides whether to launch a session."],[2620,"**LAN:** `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` - plain HTTP, exactly like LiteLLM on `:4000`.\nThere is no self-signed cert for this port and no redirect to HTTPS. This URL stays\nwhen the public name is on. Tailscale reaches it the same way, on that port."],[2876,"**Learn** covers how the pieces work"],[975,"**Leave `coder.env` absent for the usual case.** Coder CE injects its own GitHub\nApp (provider id `github`, device flow) and both templates declare that id by\ndefault, so `bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` works with no extra file.\nWorkspace owners connect GitHub under Settings -> Tokens / external auth."],[87,"**Let the merge close it.**"],[1111,"**Local disk and repository cache.** `.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` at\n`~/.cache/bazel-disk` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache/bazel-repo`. A contributor\ncontainer and a Coder workspace reuse those directories across runs."],[3822,"**Make silences in Alertmanager, not in Grafana.** Alertmanager owns grouping,\nsilences and the repeat interval, so a silence created anywhere else does not stop the\nroute - and the two views then disagree about why nobody was paged."],[2396,"**MCP servers: the default render declares three - `github`, `context7` and the\ngated `searxng` server, which is the search lane itself.** Each is a boolean gate in\nthe template, so one env flag parks exactly one block and a template re-push applies\nit fleet-wide: `GITHUB_MCP=false`, `CONTEXT7_MCP=false`, `SEARCH_MCP=false`."],[3583,"**Mimir is written to, not scraped from.** Prometheus `remote_write`s to\n`http://mimir:8080/api/v1/push`. A query against the `prometheus` datasource answers\nfrom the local TSDB; the `mimir` datasource answers through\n`http://mimir:8080/prometheus`. Those two disagreeing is the diagnostic, not a bug."],[327,"**Move the prose out.** Rationale belongs in this file, not in `startup.sh.tftpl`.\nComments in the template are bytes in everybody's argument; here they cost nothing."],[86,"**Name the issue in the branch, close it in one place.** A bug fix branches as\n`fix/<n>-<slug>`, and the closing keyword goes in the pull request body only. GitHub\nauto-links `#<n>` in a commit subject too, so repeating `Fixes #<n>` down the branch\nmultiplies one link and hides which commit answers the report."],[4327,"**Neither of them exists until you run `--apply`.** A box that has never been enrolled\nruns no exporter, so Prometheus scrapes nothing and the Spark panels stay empty."],[988,"**Never a static PAT in `.env`.** `.env.sample` used to carry a `GITHUB_TOKEN=`\nline holding a long-lived fine-grained PAT, which `push-template.sh` forwarded to\nboth templates as a template variable, into every workspace's environment."],[3743,"**No destination is configured, so nothing pages anyone as shipped.** The rules, the\nroutes and the receivers all exist; only the URL is missing. `ALERTMANAGER_WEBHOOK_URL`\nis empty in `.env.example`, so on a box nobody edited every alert is visible - on\nPrometheus' own alerts page and under Grafana's Alerting pages - and is delivered\nnowhere."],[4066,"**No dynamic secrets, no cross-project sharing.** Both are paid features of Infisical\nand neither is used: no `LICENSE_KEY` is read, generated, or referenced. The model is\nstatic shared secrets exported as dotenv."],[3745,"**No healthcheck or dashboard card for the four new services.** None of\n`node_exporter`, `cadvisor`, `blackbox_exporter` or `alertmanager` declares a\n`healthcheck`, and the dashboard's **Observability** group still carries five cards\n(dashboard.md), so their liveness is the `status` row plus the container\nlog."],[4069,"**No multi-line value reaches a workspace.** The boot fetch publishes single-line values only\nand drops the rest with one note; see\nThe workspace boot fetch."],[3746,"**No object storage.** Loki, Mimir and Tempo keep blocks on named volumes on this\nbox's disk. The follow-up is a bucket and the storage config in each of those three\nfiles; nothing about the read path changes when it lands."],[2109,"**No published source names an upstream `/learn`, and the bundled skill set never\nlands here**: the `xai-org/grok-build` built-in command table, all 240 pages of\ndocs.x.ai, every first-party changelog under `x.ai/cli/changelogs/`, and the public\n`xai-org/plugin-marketplace` catalog carry zero occurrences of it."],[4070,"**No scrape, no alert.** The three containers are log-only, like `ollama`, `kasm` and\nthe rest. `bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check` does not walk them; their state\nis `dev-stack -- status` and their logs are in Loki under their `container=` tag."],[4071,"**No second inventory.** Twelve keys, one file. A key added to compose without joining\n`infisical/managed-secrets.txt` stays outside the vault silently, which is why `check`\nasserts the count rather than trusting the file."],[3747,"**No trace client.** Tempo has an OTLP door and a datasource, and no caller. Pointing\nthe first service at `tempo:4317` is a change in that service, not in this bundle."],[4067,"**No vault dependency in the deploy path.** Compose reads `.env`, and the one call the\nrunners make before it is best-effort: warn, continue, bring the stack up on the values the\nfile already held."],[1337,"**Not a credential store.** GitHub auth stays Coder External Auth; no static token and no\nPAT appears in any file this page describes."],[4750,"**Not a fast lane.** The hop is armed by the alias alone, so a session on `agent`,\n`agent-xhigh` or `agent-swarm` compacts on Spark by design. The groups allowed to hop are\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_LANES`, default `agent-xhigh-fast,agent-swarm-fast`."],[1336,"**Not a place for contributor-only behavior in the image.** The image is shared with Coder\nworkspaces and CI, so anything that only a person at a keyboard wants (the Bazel volume\nseam, the warm-up, the git hooks, the socket ACL) lives in\n`.devcontainer/library-scripts/configure.sh`, which only this container runs. An `ENV` or\n`RUN` line added to `images/dev/Dockerfile` for the same purpose changes what a workspace\ngets, which is the mistake this split exists to prevent."],[1335,"**Not a second toolchain.** Nothing in the contributor path installs pins onto a laptop\nor a runner — that is what the image is for."],[1334,"**Not a template change.** `templates/workspace/startup.sh.tftpl` and\n`templates/devcontainer/startup.sh.tftpl` are untouched by any of this; the contributor\nseed reads them as payloads, it does not rewrite the twins."],[1333,"**Not the stack host.** The compose host (gmktec) is bootstrapped with\n`bash scripts/setup-host.sh`, and it cannot be this container: it owns the daemon that\nruns Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and Hermes. See\n../project-conventions.md for the ingress rule that keeps\nthat script the single documented `bash scripts/…`."],[4754,"**Not the summary call.** The CLI appends its instruction as the last turn, and the hook\nreads it there: one of the two asks the CLI sends, from a `user` or `system` role, in a\nturn no longer than the instruction itself. It does not read the system prompt, which\nrides every turn of the session, so a rulebook that quotes the sentence cannot move an\nordinary turn."],[4358,"**Not verified from a live box**: no Spark was reachable from where these commands ran, so\nthe answering-`box` table above is quoted from those source lines, not from an observed\nrun. The unreachable form is what was actually measured."],[2375,"**Nothing reads it back.** `~/.grok/sessions` stays the only session state the CLI,\nthe rulebook, and Coder's session management know about; no config key and no startup\nlogic points at the archive, and the hooks deny list\n(`templates/common/hooks.deny-patterns.txt`) refuses any agent command that names the\npath, the volume, or a globbed form of either. The point is a copy an operator can\ninspect later, from the host, after the workspace that made it is gone."],[5409,"**One env var, read at one seam**, documented in the dotenv sample with its off default. This\ncarries most needs: `FEATURE_AGENT_TUNNEL_V2=0`."],[2877,"**Operate** covers running the box day to day"],[2639,"**Options** (before the subcommand): `-p/--project-name` (`evo-t1-prod`; the dev\nsandbox is `evo-t1-dev`), `-f/--file`, `-e/--env-file`, `-m/--model <alias>` for one\nsession, `-w/--writable <dir>`, `--one-shot`, `--dry-run`, `-h/--help`."],[2061,"**Out of a pane: drag, and it is already on the clipboard.** tmux asks the outer\nterminal for mouse reporting only while `mouse` is on: measured on tmux 3.4, an\nattach with `mouse on` writes `ESC[?1000h`, `ESC[?1002h` and `ESC[?1006h` to the\nclient, and an attach with it off writes none of them."],[1708,"**Prepend; never replace PATH.** `use_stubs` and `with_path` put the stub directory *in\nfront of* the host PATH. `PATH=\"${BIN}\"` takes `bash`, `grep`, `cut`, `sort` and `wc` away\nfrom the script under test as well, and a script that pipes its answer through\n`grep | wc | tr` then fails for a reason that has nothing to do with the contract the\nsuite means to check."],[5260,"**Present and `down`**: the app is not answering on the name Prometheus dials. The\nscrapes are compose-DNS (`coder:2112`, `litellm:4000`), not the host mappings - a\n`curl 127.0.0.1:2112` that works from the host proves only that the port mapping is\nfine."],[4395,"**Profiling counters return nothing without `SYS_ADMIN`.** Every `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` field\nneeds the exporter started with `--cap-add SYS_ADMIN` (plus\n`NVreg_RestrictProfilingToAdminUsers=0` on the box), and this CLI does not pass that cap\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:239`). A box answers those fields with no rows at all rather\nthan an error - `DCGM_FI_PROF_*` being absent from a scrape is the expected result, not\na broken exporter (`observability/dcgm/custom-counters.csv:98-99`)."],[3564,"**Prometheus is the only scraper.** Its scrape config targets the apps that publish\n`/metrics` - `coder:2112` and `litellm:4000` - plus:"],[2621,"**Public name, opt-in:** `hermes-<suffix>.<base>`, so\n`https://hermes-gmktecbeast.overeazy.io` under the shipped defaults, when\n`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` composes it or an explicit list names it. Bootstrap\nclassifies `hermes-*` and `hermes.*` and writes `STACK_HERMES_URL`."],[74,"**Push once, when the pull request opens**, and pre-flight it:"],[2668,"**Push** is what makes a phone viable, because pull means opening a dashboard every\ntime. It rides upstream's ntfy adapter and is configured in the assistant's own\n`/opt/data/.env`, not in compose:"],[1047,"**Read the guard beside the client threshold: that pairing picks the tier.** Both fast\naliases declare a `context_window` of 262144 and the shipped `[session]` block sets\n`auto_compact_threshold_percent` to 70, so a session on either one reaches its\nauto-compaction at about 183,500 tokens - over the guard, so the Arc was never going to read\nit and the call goes to the Spark tier instead."],[80,"**Read the report before you plan.** `gh issue view <n> --comments` is the requirements and\nthe reproduction in one place, and it is the wording the change is measured against. An agent\nreads it through `env -u GH_TOKEN -u GITHUB_TOKEN gh ...` for the reason in\ntroubleshooting.md."],[3364,"**Reboot anything.** The reboot is the operator's decision, made after a\ngreen `check`."],[810,"**Refresh, and reconnect.** `coder external-auth access-token` is the live path when the\nstore goes stale: it re-mints on demand and answers with a live token, or with the\nre-authentication URL and exit 1; with no connected account the refresh script prints its\none note (pointing at **GitHub reauth**, and at **Install GitHub App** when the injected\nApp is in use) and exits 0, and mints nothing."],[2063,"**Right-click and middle-click paste that clipboard.** The seed sets\n`terminal.integrated.rightClickBehavior: \"copyPaste\"` - copy while there is a\nselection, paste when there is none - and pins\n`terminal.integrated.middleClickBehavior: \"paste\"`, because the config registry in\nthe shipped 4.145.0 gives that setting the default `\"default\"`, which pastes only\non the Linux build. tmux has no middle-click paste of its own to answer first:\n`MouseDown2Pane` is unbound in the root table."],[2699,"**Run it before a `--force` re-seed and after a merge.** The drift check names the\nfile that differs and prints the `hermes-bootstrap --data <mount> --force` line that\nwould overwrite it, which is the pair an operator wants side by side."],[81,"**Search before you file.** `gh issue list --state open --search '<keywords> in:title,body'`,\nthen the same with `--state closed`."],[4397,"**ssh will not reach a box.** In `--status` this is a `warning:` naming the ssh error, and\nthe exit status stays 0 (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:566-576`): a box that is off, or one\nthis shell has no credentials for, is a not-yet state."],[70,"**Stage by path.** `git add <path> <path>`, and read the index back with\n`git diff --cached --name-only`."],[328,"**Stage the payload as a file, not an argument.** The obvious resource does not\nexist: the OSS provider's `ResourcesMap` (`provider/provider.go`) registers\n`coder_agent`, `coder_agent_instance`, `coder_ai_task`, `coder_app`,\n`coder_metadata`, `coder_script`, `coder_env`, `coder_devcontainer` and\n`coder_external_agent`, and nothing else. `coder_file` was requested in\n`coder/coder#3055` and closed `not_planned` on 2022-09-23; a maintainer was still\nasking for it in 2025. Do not plan around it."],[561,"**Starts the out-of-band session archive mirror** (boot stage 9). It chowns\n`/var/lib/grok-session-archive` to the home owner, folds the container hostname into a\nsafe leaf name under `workspaces/`, then leaves a backgrounded loop that `rsync`s\n`~/.grok/sessions/` into that leaf every 180 seconds, without `--delete`, for the life\nof the container."],[2638,"**Subcommands:** `chat` (the default), `ask TEXT` for one question and exit, `shell`\nfor a shell with the container's mounts and socket, `check` for the readiness report,\n`commands` for the assistant's own help. Every word after the subcommand is handed to\nthe assistant verbatim, so a flag this script has never heard of still works."],[1390,"**Tables.** A content table gets its head pinned under the navbar while the table is on\nscreen, and a mirrored scroll bar at the bottom of the viewport when its columns are wider\nthan the page. Both come from `docs-site/components/table-chrome.tsx`; a table that already\ncarries a class is left as authored."],[2660,"**The API server** is the headless door: an iOS Shortcut or a Tasker profile can\nPOST a prompt and read the answer with no browser and no dashboard login."],[3573,"**The apps with no `/metrics` are observed by logs alone, on purpose.** These publish\nno Prometheus endpoint, so scraping them would leave a target answering 404 forever\nand a tally no one trusts:"],[2415,"**The binary is missing from the golden image.** A warning: the running\n`evo-t1-dev` predates the npm bake. Remedy: rebuild the image and recreate the\nworkspace, or run the `npm i -g --prefix /opt/npm-global` command the check prints\nto install the pinned package into the running box (pins in\n`images/dev/tool-versions.env`)."],[2413,"**The block is missing from the rendered config.** That is a failure, not a\nwarning: the default render declares the lane, so something took it away - a push\npassed its gate `false`, the workspace runs an older template, or the file lost\nits stack marker and nothing re-renders it."],[2416,"**The credential is missing.** Also a warning, and per lane it means something\ndifferent. `github` with `$GH_TOKEN` empty is a real gap: connect GitHub External\nAuth (the **GitHub reauth** app) and start a new session. An empty\n`CONTEXT7_API_KEY` is not a gap - see the key's paragraph above, the lane answers\nkeyless at the public rate limit."],[4393,"**The DCGM tag has never been pulled.** `nvcr.io` answers 404 to an anonymous token, so\nthe pin `nvcr.io/nvidia/k8s/dcgm-exporter:4.4.2-4.7.0-ubuntu22.04` was not verifiable\nupstream the way the other three were. The first `--apply` against a box is what proves\nit, and a wrong tag surfaces there as the registry's own error: `--apply` prints\ndocker's reason rather than swallowing it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`,\n`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`), so you read what the registry said. Correct the tag in\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` and re-run - it is a key, not a code change."],[880,"**The env files.** `docker-compose.yml` builds its variable references in two steps:"],[1032,"**The fast-compaction hop has five keys of its own.** On `agent-xhigh-fast` and\n`agent-swarm-fast` only, the proxy callback `litellm/compaction_router.py` recognises\nthe CLI's conversation-summary call and re-serves it from somewhere cheaper than the\nSpark box it arrived on, in one of two tiers:"],[2687,"**The fourteen checks:**"],[3593,"**The gap alerts select on the job name.** `HostNodeExporterGap` and `HostCadvisorGap`\nfire when `job=\"node_exporter\"` or `job=\"cadvisor\"` carries no series, which is why the\njob name is the Compose service name (`observability/prometheus/prometheus.yml:142` and\n`:151`). Renaming either service without renaming its job makes two alerts permanently\nquiet, and no test will tell you."],[4751,"**The kill switch.** `EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION` ships on, and `0`, `false`, `off` or `no`\nstops the hop on every lane at once."],[5412,"**The platform's parameters** (Terraform variables, Helm values, Ansible vars, Coder template\nvariables) when the flag selects infrastructure."],[3952,"**The residual, honestly.** That list names what a shell or a loader *executes*. It is not a list\nof every environment variable that changes what a program does, and it cannot be one."],[2220,"**The review pass is a panel.** The independent check that closes a fleet run is a wave of\nskeptics dispatched in one turn, each riding `agent-review` through the `grok_review_lane`\npin above, each owning no file, and each re-running the producers' own `done:` commands\nrather than reading their reports. The default panel is three angles:"],[3950,"**The security boundary**, stated so no operator has to guess at it: because `agent.env` is\nsourced by every shell a workspace starts, a vault key that the shell, the dynamic loader, an\ninterpreter or git would treat as code is not a secret, it is a command line chosen by whoever\ncan write a secret in the project."],[3594,"**The Spark tier is quiet until the boxes are enrolled.** Those two jobs read their\ntargets from `observability/spark-targets/sparks.json` through `file_sd_configs`, a file\nthe stack host generates and `.gitignore:76` keeps out of git. No file means no target\ngroups, which reads as zero Sparks rather than as a broken probe. The boxes' own\nnode_exporter and DCGM exporter are installed on the boxes by `scripts/spark-monitor.sh`,\nnot by this Compose file."],[2123,"**The stack ships its own three repository-conversion commands as `/ez-bazel-first`,\n`/ez-strong-types` and `/ez-trunk-based-repo`**, and these stand in for nothing upstream:\nthey convert whatever repository the workspace happens to hold, which is why they carry no\npath that only exists in this stack."],[2116,"**The stack ships its own three skill commands as `/ez-learn`, `/ez-skillify` and\n`/ez-create-skill`**, so they are stack payloads like the delegation skill, not something\nto install, and the `ez-` prefix keeps them clear of the upstream names they sit\nbeside: `/create-skill` is one of the CLI's bundled platform skills and\n`/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember`, `/memory` are its built-in memory commands, while no\npublished source names a `/learn` or a `/skillify` at all."],[2541,"**The state words** come from `state_word` in the script and are what the\n`coding-agents` Hermes skill teaches: `running` (output within 30 s), `quiet` (an\nagent loaded and silent for 30 s - finished, or parked on a question, which is what\n\"it's stuck\" usually is), `waiting` (a client attached at a plain shell - a person\nis already in that pane), `idle-shell`, `detached-shell`, and `unknown`. A state\nword is a pointer at the pane, never a verdict: `tail` before you say done."],[4763,"**The tier names a group nobody declared.** Neither target is validated by LiteLLM, so the\nhook checks both against the `model_name:` entries in `litellm/config.yaml` and refuses a\nname that is not among them, with one `compaction router: target ... is no model group in\nconfig.yaml` warning per call. A typo there therefore looks like a hop that never fires\nrather than a summary that hard-fails. `grep -E '^  - model_name: ' litellm/config.yaml` is\nthe list of legal answers for both keys."],[885,"**The workspace config.** `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, `~/.tmux.conf` and the `~/.coder`\nand `~/.agents` seeds are materialised inside each workspace when it first boots\nand are never revisited after that."],[2694,"**Three findings it exists to catch**, each one measured on a live box:"],[4269,"**Timeouts.** `proxy/router.conf` on this box already holds per-service read\ntimeouts - dashboard 60s, coder, hermes, grafana and infisical 300s, LiteLLM 600s,\nKasm 3600s - so Traefik must not be the shorter link. One transport for all seven at\n`3600s` is the simple choice because Kasm needs that floor anyway."],[2064,"**tmux keeps no buffer, so there is nothing to paste out of one.** `Prefix-[`\nstill enters vi copy-mode and it is a viewer: the cursor keys, `PPage`/`NPage`,\n`Ctrl-u`/`Ctrl-d` and `k`/`j` walk history, `q` leaves, and `Escape` clears a\nselection."],[2094,"**tmux options.** `templates/common/tmux.conf` seeds the four options Doctor's\nown fixes write, `set -g set-clipboard on`, `set -g allow-passthrough on`,\n`set -g extended-keys on` and the `*:RGB` `terminal-features` entry, plus the\n`xterm*:extkeys` entry Doctor leaves to the operator."],[4756,"**Too big for the Arc, and the Spark tier was blanked.** The estimate covers the whole\nrequest, the tool schemas included, and charges a base64 payload and a non-ASCII reply at\ntheir own denser rates rather than the prose rate; anything above\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_INPUT_LIMIT` (default 24000) goes to\n`EVO_T1_FAST_COMPACTION_SPARK_TARGET` rather than to the Arc: the\nArc weights answer inside a 32k window and Ollama would clamp such an input rather than\nfail it."],[3585,"**Traces arrive over OTLP only, and one client already speaks it.** Anything that wants\ntraces in this stack sends OTLP gRPC to `tempo:4317`. Grafana is the one shipped\ncaller: `GF_TRACING_ENABLED` points its own exporter at `tempo:4317`, so the trace\nviewer has the product's own spans before any workspace emits one."],[515,"**Treat the repository as untrusted code.** Everything it builds runs on a privileged\ndaemon on this box, so a Dockerfile in that repo is root-equivalent on the stack host.\nThe create form's own description says so, and only repositories you trust belong in\nthe field."],[92,"**Triage belongs to a person.** Closing, reopening, reassigning, labelling and milestoning are\nthe maintainer's calls, and an agent makes them only when the operator asked for that in words."],[2540,"**Two details that make the answer correct.** Each workspace creates a\n`coder-<id>-dind-sidecar` carrying the same labels; it runs a Docker daemon and no\nshell, so it is dropped by name. And a tmux server belongs to one uid and listens on\n`/tmp/tmux-<uid>/default`, so the sockets are enumerated per uid - `docker exec`\nlands as the image's user, and asking *that* user for its default server reports\n\"no sessions\" for an agent the operator can see on screen."],[2202,"**Two numbers, one rule.** The same grep opens its four lines of context with\n`# fleet-width: 15` - the fleet's own ceiling, rendered into the same table from\n`var.grok_swarm_width` (`templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:427`, fed at\n`templates/workspace/main.tf:558`) so an explicit width never erases it - while the number\nbelow it is `grok_subagent_max_concurrent`, the variable's own shipped 15. The pair is\nwhat the width gets read against:"],[2084,"**Two scroll-back stores, two different budgets.** `terminal.integrated.scrollback`\nin the settings seed is the xterm.js buffer, which is what a plain `bash`\nprofile scrolls through; tmux's `history-limit` is what `Prefix-[` walks\ninside a pane. They read 2000 and 100000 lines here, and the two numbers\ndiffer on purpose because the two stores are billed differently."],[1474,"**Use `--json` when you want to act on the answer.** Prose (`== section ==`, `ok:`, `note:`,\n`summary:`) rides stderr and stdout carries only labels, one per line, which is the shape\n`bazelisk test` consumes:"],[4268,"**WebSockets need nothing here.** Traefik forwards `Upgrade` and `Connection`\nas-is; there is no `protocol: websocket` to set and no middleware to add. The Hermes\nChat tab's `/api/pty` and `/api/ws` open on that alone. They fail only when\nsomething in between drops the upgrade, and Cloudflare has WebSockets on by\ndefault, so the usual suspect is an Authelia middleware configured to buffer."],[5377,"**What does each non-trunk branch hold that the trunk does not?**"],[5392,"**What flags already exist?** `grep -rnE 'FEATURE_|_ENABLED|^\\s*profiles:'` over the compose\nfiles, dotenv samples, Terraform variables and scripts."],[5393,"**What is the gate you must not break?** `bazelisk test //:test-fast`, `npm test`,\n`make test`, `./manage.sh <check>`, `pytest`, `go test ./...`. Run it before you start so a\nlater red is attributable."],[2651,"**What it can see:** the checkout at `$STACK_HOST_PATH`, the deploy workspace at\n`${STACK_DEPLOY_PATH:-/opt/stack-deploy}`, `/opt/stack-skills`, the data volume, and\nthe docker socket."],[2652,"**What it cannot:** `/home` is not mounted, so no dotfiles, SSH keys or shell\nhistory - by design. The image carries git and the Docker CLI but not bazelisk, so\ninside the container the same operations go through\n`docker compose -p evo-t1-prod -f \"$STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml\"`."],[2684,"**What it reads:** the config the container actually loads, the environment it was\ncreated with, the mounts it was given, and the two seed files beside the checkout."],[2209,"**What the lane choice buys.** `grok_subagents_lane` is the knob for the writers and it\ndecides placement, not size: the in-flight width comes from\n`grok_subagent_max_concurrent` (the variable's own shipped 15) on every lane, and only a\nworkspace that sets it to `null` on purpose takes the width from `grok_swarm_width` instead\n(its validation accepts any whole number 1..32 plus that explicit null)."],[34,"**What this enables**"],[230,"**What this enables**"],[874,"**What this enables**"],[1079,"**What this enables**"],[1186,"**What this enables**"],[1368,"**What this enables**"],[1440,"**What this enables**"],[1575,"**What this enables**"],[1666,"**What this enables**"],[1753,"**What this enables**"],[1845,"**What this enables**"],[2012,"**What this enables**"],[2603,"**What this enables**"],[2848,"**What this enables**"],[2893,"**What this enables**"],[2992,"**What this enables**"],[3318,"**What this enables**"],[3397,"**What this enables**"],[3543,"**What this enables**"],[3763,"**What this enables**"],[3803,"**What this enables**"],[3897,"**What this enables**"],[4101,"**What this enables**"],[4207,"**What this enables**"],[4317,"**What this enables**"],[4593,"**What this enables**"],[5343,"**What this enables**"],[2674,"**What to ask from a phone** is the same list you would run at the desk, and the\n`coding-agents` skill is what teaches the assistant to answer it:"],[27,"**What's on this page**"],[223,"**What's on this page**"],[867,"**What's on this page**"],[1073,"**What's on this page**"],[1176,"**What's on this page**"],[1362,"**What's on this page**"],[1432,"**What's on this page**"],[1568,"**What's on this page**"],[1659,"**What's on this page**"],[1747,"**What's on this page**"],[1840,"**What's on this page**"],[2002,"**What's on this page**"],[2595,"**What's on this page**"],[2844,"**What's on this page**"],[2889,"**What's on this page**"],[2983,"**What's on this page**"],[3312,"**What's on this page**"],[3389,"**What's on this page**"],[3535,"**What's on this page**"],[3760,"**What's on this page**"],[3798,"**What's on this page**"],[3887,"**What's on this page**"],[4096,"**What's on this page**"],[4201,"**What's on this page**"],[4310,"**What's on this page**"],[4585,"**What's on this page**"],[5335,"**What's on this page**"],[5376,"**Which branches exist, how old are they, who owns them?**"],[2537,"**Why the daemon and not the Coder API.** A workspace that can host a session is a\n*running* one, and a running workspace is a labelled container: both templates stamp\n`coder.workspace_id` and `coder.workspace_name`, the same labels\n`scripts/github-auth.sh` enumerates."],[806,"**Why the env pair goes stale.** Coder injects `GH_TOKEN`/`GITHUB_TOKEN` into the agent\nenvironment when the workspace starts."],[403,"**Workspace image** (`workspace_image`, dropdown, immutable). `--var image=` seeds its\ndefault and is also injected as its own option, so a custom tag still reaches the\ncontainer while the form stays honest about what it is building."],[2680,"*is the box healthy* -> `//scripts:hermes-shell -- check`, and the `host-diagnostics` skill"],[2678,"*start one on repo X* -> `launch --dir /home/coder/<repo> <workspace>`"],[2677,"*tell it yes* -> `send <workspace> <session> \"yes\"`"],[2679,"*wake me when it finishes* -> `watch <workspace> <session> 1800`, then `hermes send`\nto the ntfy topic above"],[2675,"*what are my agents doing* -> `//scripts:coder-sessions -- list` / `show`"],[2676,"*what did it get stuck on* -> `tail <workspace> <session> 40`, then quote the pane"],[3456,"`__PROJECT_DESCRIPTION__`"],[3458,"`__PROJECT_LICENSE_HOLDER__`"],[3455,"`__PROJECT_MODULE__`"],[3453,"`__PROJECT_NAME__`"],[3457,"`__PROJECT_OWNER__`"],[3454,"`__PROJECT_SLUG__`"],[3459,"`__PROJECT_YEAR__`"],[468,"`--activity-bump` goes to `0` with it: a bump only extends a deadline, and with no\ndeadline there is nothing to extend."],[777,"`--additional-repos` is the opt-in escape hatch for the service-and-the-library-it-uses\ncase. It takes a comma-separated list (`\"url1,url2\"`), one URL per line works too, and\nthe spaces around each entry are trimmed."],[2280,"`--agent-profile` overrides both and loads no file, so that session runs the default agent\nunless the client names one (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/15-agent-mode.md:69`)."],[1510,"`--all` also lists the aggregate suites that reach everything (`//:test`, `//:test-fast`,\n`//:test-all` and `//tests:bats`), which is useful when you want the full set named explicitly\nrather than the leaf suites you can invoke."],[4402,"`--apply` reports it per container as `<name> is not running (<state>): <what docker said>`\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:675`, `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:685`) - the text after the colon\nis docker's, so `port is already allocated` names the process holding 9100 or 9400."],[4335,"`--apply` skips every box instead of installing a container nothing scrapes\n(`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:541`)"],[4362,"`--apply` was **not run for this page**: it creates containers on real hardware, and no\nSpark answered ssh from where the other commands ran. Read the plan output above as the\ncontract for what it does."],[4300,"`--box` (one box number)"],[4180,"`--check` — the read-only mode `scripts/setup-host.sh` and `scripts/bootstrap.sh` both\ntake. `setup-host --check` names the tools that are missing, leftover runfiles that\nwill make `bazelisk run` fail with Permission denied, and whether `github.com`\nresolves, and installs nothing; `setup-host --repair` is the recovery for those\nleftovers and, on Linux, for MagicDNS-only host DNS (uses sudo when chmod or\n`/etc` is not enough)."],[4630,"`--check` prints which nameservers are in play and whether `github.com` resolves."],[4628,"`--check` still writes nothing."],[535,"`--devcontainer` forces the devcontainer template and `--template <name>` overrides the\ntemplate choice outright."],[784,"`--dry-run` prints the list under `also:` before it creates anything, so the decision\nis readable first."],[3974,"`--env-file PATH` points the verb at a dotenv file other than the `.env` compose itself\nreads. `--help` prints the usage block above."],[472,"`--failure-ttl` (Failure cleanup), `--dormancy-threshold` and\n`--dormancy-auto-deletion` are all marked \"This licensed feature's default is 0h\n(off)\" in `templates edit --help` on v2.36.6, and `--allow-user-autostart` works\nthe same way."],[4064,"`--format=dotenv-export` uses the same writer and fails the same way. The boot body\ntherefore takes every value apart and re-emits it under quoting that has no escape hatch,\nwhich is what makes `~/.bashrc`'s `set -a; . agent.env` safe to keep. Anything else that\nreads that file inherits the guarantee; nothing should feed the CLI's own dotenv output to\na shell."],[2439,"`--help` prints the same two commands with every flag and every `CODER_AGENTS_*`\noverride, which is the list to reach for from a shell."],[4380,"`--help` prints the usage block from the script's own header. The modes are mutually\nexclusive: `--status cannot be combined with --apply; pick one mode`."],[2303,"`--home DIR` points it at another profile home instead of `$HOME`, and `--json` prints\nthe whole record instead of the one `ok: N files, M symbols -> ...` line. The helper is\n`templates/common/ez-code-index.code_index.py`, seeded into a `scripts/` subdir of the same\nskill directory the way the other helper-carrying skills ship theirs. The `code-index.md`\nheader records the `sha` it indexed, so compare that line against `git rev-parse HEAD` before\ntrusting a `path:line` the map hands you; a stale map is a fresh `rg`."],[945,"`--home` takes a host directory shaped like a workspace `HOME`, which is also how\nyou try a change on a scratch tree before pointing a live stack at it. See\ntroubleshooting.md for the docker-vantage rules."],[3360,"`--iface ens3` with `--ip dhcp` produces the same pinned-device shape with\nDHCP kept. A name the kernel never assigned is a blocker, not a typo you\ndiscover after the reboot: `check` compares it against `/sys/class/net`."],[4301,"`--json`"],[2393,"`--json` adds `engines_parked`. It checks the service hop; `grok mcp doctor searxng`\n  inside the workspace checks the MCP hop."],[2260,"`--json` prints the same findings as an object with `plan`, `slots`, `tasks`, `lanes`,\n`ready`, `errors` and `warnings` for a script to read"],[3725,"`--no-docker` skips the four unpublished backends when you are not on a shell that can\naddress the daemon."],[2261,"`--no-files` skips the on-disk existence check for the briefs"],[4673,"`--no-tests` skips all four recipe probes for a quick read-only\npass, and that is what `//scripts:bootstrap` runs."],[4819,"`--noproxy '*'` matters: with a proxy set in the environment curl hands the name to the\nproxy instead of resolving it, and `000` or a 5xx then describes the proxy."],[4178,"`--project-name` - every stack-runner subcommand (`up`, `down`, `status`, `logs`, `reset`),\non both `dev-stack` and `prod-stack`. `dev-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-dev`, the dev sandbox;\n`prod-stack` defaults to `evo-t1-prod`, the stable stack it brings up, and it addresses a\nstable stack brought up by hand with `--project-name evo-t1-coder-stack` (the checkout\nfolder's name)."],[2726,"`--provider arc-chat` sends `\"model\":\"chat\"`"],[2728,"`--provider arc-coder-fast` sends `\"model\":\"coder-fast\"`"],[2727,"`--provider arc-coder` sends `\"model\":\"coder\"`"],[2729,"`--provider litellm` sends `\"model\":\"agent\"` - that row's `default_model` is\n`agent`, which is also what the `model:` block holds"],[1509,"`--ref <ref>` for what this branch adds over a base"],[4114,"`--repair` is the other half of\nthat exemption: leftover Docker bind-mounts in Bazel's runfiles tree make\n`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` die creating `prod-stack.runfiles` (`Permission\ndenied` on `homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`) before any stack script can run."],[1089,"`--repair` is the recovery when `bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack` dies creating\nthe runfiles tree (`FileAccessException ... Permission denied` on\n`homepage/assets/icons/evo-t1.svg`). Bazel never reaches the stack script, so the\nrepair has to live in this shell entry point."],[4299,"`--restart`"],[4408,"`--restart` is the first thing to try; `--apply` re-ferries the CSV if the file changed."],[2259,"`--slots N` overrides the plan's number"],[5158,"`--source=chain` (the default) probes every credential that could answer for that\nrepository; `--source=env`, `--source=gh` or `--source=mint` answers one slot and says\nbeside the answer that git and gh consult the env pair first."],[1507,"`--staged` for the index"],[4410,"`--status` (is the container there and serving)"],[3834,"`--status` prints the tag a box actually runs"],[4337,"`--status` still probes, because knowing what a box runs never needs the flag."],[2262,"`--strict` makes warnings fail"],[4302,"`--targets`"],[4411,"`--targets` (is the box in the document at all)"],[3835,"`--targets` prints the `sparks.json` that Prometheus reads through `file_sd_configs`; the\nredirect that writes it on the host, the refresh interval, and what an absent file mean\nare in `observability/spark-targets/README.md`."],[4381,"`--targets` prints the document; it never writes it (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:340`)."],[4336,"`--targets` prints the empty document `[]` (`scripts/spark-monitor.sh:302`)"],[1508,"`--untracked` to count files git does not track yet (a file you just created is exactly the case\nworth covering)"],[2443,"`--wait` (or `--wait=SECONDS`) polls for that first owner instead of skip-0. The\nwindow is counted from the database's first \"no owner\" answer, so a slow buildinfo\nprobe or a slow first query cannot spend it. `SKIP_CODER_AGENTS=1` skips bootstrap\nand stack-up seeding. `CODER_AGENTS_WAIT_SECS`\nis what stack `up` forwards as `--wait=`."],[2730,"`-m/--model <alias>` overrides it: `--model agent-swarm` sends `\"model\":\"agent-swarm\"`,\nand `--provider litellm --model chat` sends `\"model\":\"chat\"`."],[4423,"`./.env: line 188: qwen2.5-coder:14b: command not found` from `bazelisk run //scripts:pull-models` -> the model list was being parsed as shell"],[1224,"`.bazelrc` points `--disk_cache` and `--repository_cache` at `~/.cache`, and Bazel's\noutput base lives under `~/.cache/bazel` as well. Inside a container `$HOME` is the\nimage's own filesystem, so all three die with the container, and a recreation - which\nis what every edit to this config or the Dockerfile causes - repays the whole cost:"],[1328,"`.devcontainer/contributor-env` is a sentinel, not a config file."],[1203,"`.devcontainer/devcontainer.json` is what that reads:"],[1253,"`.devcontainer/library-scripts/*.sh` is first-party shell: it carries\n`# @function` blocks, is linted by `//:lint` (shellcheck `-x --severity=warning`, shfmt\n`-s -i 2 -ci`), and its behaviour is pinned by\n`bazelisk test //tests:bats_contributor_devcontainer_test`."],[3416,"`.editorconfig`"],[4334,"`.env.example:629`. It is read by **this CLI**, at `scripts/spark-monitor.sh:212` - not\nby Compose. Nothing in `docker-compose.yml` consults it, because the exporters do not run\nin the stack's project at all; they run on the boxes. With the key off:"],[971,"`.env.sample` deliberately carries no GitHub credential of any kind, so the env\npass has nothing to reconcile here: the twin declares no `CODER_EXTERNAL_AUTH_0_*`\nkey and `update-configs` will never add one. Those keys belong in `coder.env`\nbeside `docker-compose.yml` (gitignored), which is not a file this tool touches."],[140,"`.env` keys it reads"],[3467,"`.git` (which would hand the new repository the template's history and origin remote)"],[5390,"`.github/workflows/`"],[1315,"`.github/workflows/ci.yml` builds `images/dev` with `docker buildx build` and the GitHub\nActions layer cache (`type=gha`), then `docker run`s that image and runs the checks inside\nit:"],[2171,"`.gitignore` carries the matching half on the git side: the whole family is\nignored except the names `allow` lists."],[2304,"`.grok/workflows/fleet-verify.js` is the same three phases as a saved workflow rather than a\nprocedure an agent follows by hand: **Map** puts two read-only explorers on `agent-explore`,\n**Implement** puts one writer on `agent-swarm`, and **Verify** puts an independent skeptic on\n`agent-review` that re-runs the producer's own command and returns a structured verdict of\n`kept`, `broken` or `mixed` with the evidence behind each finding. Its three model ids are\naliases in `litellm/config.yaml`, so `grok inspect` is the check after an alias edit - a name\nthe catalog no longer carries fails the run rather than degrading it."],[3466,"`.self-check/` (the scaffold's conformance suite, which goes stale the moment a real project\ndiverges from the template it was checked against)"],[3465,"`.template-lanes/` (the overlay source this script reads)"],[4520,"`.terraform.lock.hcl is missing` -> a note, not a failure"],[350,"`.terraform/` is excluded (pushing it would put the plan's provider binaries into the\nversion's Filestore row)"],[4911,"`(D)elete it` is the right answer for an orphan. Do not pick `(R)ecover`: every start\nre-renders `config.toml` from `grok-config.toml.tftpl`, so recovery would restore bytes\nthe stack is going to replace regardless."],[2279,"`[agent] name` in `~/.grok/config.toml` picks the definition for an interactive `grok`\nrun; a missing name falls through to `GROK_AGENT`, which takes an absolute path or a bare\nagent name (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/05-configuration.md:124-132`, `:810`)."],[2159,"`[features]` carries `telemetry = false`, `web_fetch = true` and\n`active_agent_messages = true`, the last of them giving the parent\n`send_subagent_message`: a child already running can be handed a new instruction and\nkeep its context, which is cheaper than killing it and dispatching a replacement. All\nthree keys are static in `templates/workspace/grok-config.toml.tftpl:368-373` and none\nis rendered from a template variable."],[2135,"`[models] default` points at the `grok_default_model` alias - `agent` unless the\ntemplate variable or the create form's **Grok default model** dropdown names\nanother. `[models] default_reasoning_effort = \"xhigh\"`. The Spark lane answers\n`low` / `medium` / `xhigh`; `high` is never advertised."],[2465,"`[models] session_summary` is not that call either; the key serves titles and the\nsession recap."],[2144,"`[models]` also carries `extra_headers` with\n`x-litellm-session-id: ws-<workspace-id>` - the key LiteLLM uses to pin a\nworkspace's traffic to one healthy Spark box. Each workspace renders its own\nkey: `config.toml` is that workspace's own file."],[2165,"`[permission]` holds the guardrails the stack owns. The approval mode is a stack\ndefault now rather than a per-user call: the render writes\n`[ui] permission_mode = \"always-approve\"` unless the create form's **Grok approval\nmode** field (`grok_permission_mode`, whose accepted values are `default`, `ask`,\n`auto`, `always-approve`) names another, and the contributor seed takes the same\nchoice from `GROK_PERMISSION_MODE`."],[5146,"`[shadowed: the credential above outranks this slot, ...]` is the most misread line in\nthe report: the credential under it is healthy and git will not use it while the env\npair stands."],[2154,"`[subagents.models]` pins every subagent type a session can get to a lane of its\nown, and there are four variables behind those pins, not one: `grok_subagents_lane`\n(default `agent-swarm`) for the writers, `grok_explore_lane` (default `agent-explore`) for the\nmappers, `grok_plan_lane` (default `agent`) for the planner, and `grok_review_lane`\n(default `agent-review`) for the skeptics."],[2282,"`[subagents.models]` pins the lane for that type, and it is what the workspace renders -\nsee The fan-out seam."],[2145,"`[subagents]` sets how wide a fan-out goes and `[subagents.models]` which lane each\ntype rides, both rendered from template variables: `grok_subagents_lane` (default\n`agent-swarm`) for the writers, three more for the reader, planner and skeptic roles named\nbelow, and `grok_subagent_max_concurrent` (default 15, the variable's own shipped\nnumber; `null` is kept as the opt-in that hands the width to `grok_swarm_width`, the\nfleet's own ceiling), and `grok_subagent_max_depth` (default 2,\nvalidated 1..3) sets how deep the tree of children may go."],[4659,"`[tensorfold] done chatcmpl-... prompt=58 ... effort=xhigh tokens=16 finish=length`\nfollowed by `warning: a reply reached max_tokens while still thinking, so its content\nis empty`: that is the LiteLLM proxy's health check, not a user."],[4662,"`[tensorfold] ended chatcmpl-... reason=client-left prompt=... after=Ns`: whoever asked\nhung up. A tiny prompt abandoned at around 81 s was a health check the proxy\nabandoned on its own probe budget; a huge prompt abandoned at around 400 s is a\nclient with its own timeout, and it re-prefills from zero if it retries."],[1488,"`*.bzl`"],[5385,"`*.tf`"],[1317,"`//:lint`"],[1123,"`//:stack_data` is the explicit 