,\n`STACK_ENV_LABEL` and the per-service `STACK_*_NAME` prefixes, all documented in\n`.env.sample`."],[4246,"`auto` composes the seven names from the parts above;"],[3016,"`background_health_checks: true` and `enable_health_check_routing: true` do the\nre-probing and then make the result decide routing, so recovery does not wait for\nuser traffic to rediscover it."],[1618,"`bare-container` (a `list`, `set`, or `map` with no element type, which is `any`\nwearing a different hat)"],[3938,"`BASH_ENV` and `ENV` (executed by every non-interactive bash), `PROMPT_COMMAND` (by every\ninteractive one)"],[4525,"`bash: -c: line 32: syntax error near unexpected token '('` after \"Running code-server in the background\" -> HCL `$$((` in the editor wait loop"],[2176,"`Bash(git add -f *)` / `--force`, past `.gitignore`"],[2175,"`Bash(git commit --no-verify *)` / `-n`, past the hermetic pre-commit hook"],[2167,"`Bash(rm -rf *)` blocks the destructive\nform, and the dotenv family is split by what a file actually holds:"],[2178,"`Bash(rm -rf *)` is a prefix glob, so it catches a benign per-path scratch cleanup\nalong with the destruction it is there to prevent. Agents run into that constantly:\nacross 271 workspace sessions, 86 recorded at least one denied tool call and 56\nattempted an `rm -rf`, and 55 sessions did both."],[1704,"`bats_source_repo_root` — the checkout whose **bytes** are what the stack ships, which\nunder the coverage mirror is the one the mirror was built from (`EVO_COV_SOURCE_ROOT`)\nwhile `REPO_ROOT` is the mirror. A gate that counts the size of a shipped payload reads\nit there; the production functions it loads still come from `REPO_ROOT`, so measuring\nthe shipping tree costs no coverage mark."],[1703,"`BATS_TEST_TMPDIR` — per-test scratch, removed after each test. `make_scratch` prints a\nfresh directory under it; `fake_root`-style helpers build a throwaway checkout there."],[1467,"`bazel-map` is a thin shell over `bazelisk query`. It reads nothing and writes nothing; each verb\nis one query, run from the real checkout, with Bazel's stderr kept so a miss is reported instead of\nswallowed."],[1237,"`bazelisk run\n//docs-site:install`"],[50,"`bazelisk run //:validate` runs the fast pair plus `docker compose config -q` and the\n`.env.sample`/`.env.example` comparison; the pre-PR gate is `//:test-all`, which\n`//:validate` and the pre-push hook both reach."],[4602,"`bazelisk run //scripts:...` also runs from the checkout."],[4249,"`bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` derives:"],[52,"`bazelisk run //scripts:branch-hygiene` is the check for that sentence and for the two\nbelow:"],[115,"`bazelisk run //scripts:branch-hygiene` still audits the topology, because the layers are\nbranches: a merged-but-undeleted layer, or one past the branch lifetime, is still its\nfinding, and an environment-named layer is still the error Environments\nalready refuses."],[1198,"`bazelisk run //scripts:build-dev-image` refuses to build when a pin has no matching\n`ARG`, or when an `ARG` default drifted from the pin. Add both sides of a new pin in the\nsame change."],[824,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- -h` - full usage: the verdict kinds, both argument\norders, both modes and the fix contract."],[821,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- auth <container> [-]` - STACK HOST ONLY (it\ndrives `docker exec`). Installs a pasted PAT on stdin into that one workspace, then\nre-installs the env-proof helper that `gh auth setup-git` would otherwise overwrite.\nLegitimate only once `check` has already answered REFUSED-GRANT, NOT VISIBLE or REJECTED:\nthe repo rule is that a workspace's credential comes from the External Auth provider, so\nno verdict short of those justifies a pasted token."],[816,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- check [container] [repo-url] [--source=<slot>]` -\nread-only. Inside a workspace or the contributor devcontainer it runs SELF mode with no\ndocker and prints `self:` lines for this environment's own chain; on the stack host it\nenumerates the labeled workspace containers, or probes a named container alone.\n`--source=chain|env|gh|mint` names one slot (chain is the default), and flags may go\nbefore or after the positionals."],[819,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix [container] [repo-url]` - THE ONE COMMAND,\nand the answer to every `push would FAIL` line. Inside a workspace it repairs that\nworkspace; on the host a named container repairs that one workspace, and no container is\nrefused there because it names the blast radius. It mints a live token through Coder\nexternal-auth, writes the gh auth store, points git's `github.com` and `gist.github.com`\ncredential helper at that store through a form that clears the env pair first, then\nre-runs the write probe and prints the verdict again, so it proves itself. Idempotent."],[986,"`bazelisk run //scripts:github-auth -- fix <repo-url>` repairs a\nworkspace that cannot push."],[2734,"`bazelisk run //scripts:hermes-shell -- -m <alias>` from the host, which sets\n`HERMES_MODEL` in the container"],[4791,"`bazelisk run //scripts:mcp-check` covers both hops plus the config side in one\npass: the declared lanes, whether each `command=` binary is there, whether\n`~/.grok/config.toml` is still stack-managed, whether the lane's credential is\nset, whether the search lane returns results for a plain lookup rather than just\nan HTTP 200, and each server's handshake."],[516,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace -- <git-url> [name]` answers the two questions\nabove for you: which template, and whether to build the dev container."],[5062,"`bazelisk run //scripts:new-workspace` decides that for you, per repo:"],[5267,"`bazelisk run //scripts:observability-check`\n  prints that target's `lastError`, so it names the 401 without a `docker logs` read."],[48,"`bazelisk run //scripts:pre-commit` on demand"],[4702,"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- logs --tail 200 litellm | grep -i 'cycle_duration_exceeded'`\nis the grep worth typing; the loop's own lines need the proxy started with\n`--detailed_debug`."],[4611,"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` is the command that pair recovers with; the\n`make prod-stack-up` (and `make stack-up`) shims run `--repair` before calling that same\ntarget. Bare `bash scripts/setup-host.sh` repairs leftovers and then installs any missing\nhost tools. `bash scripts/setup-host.sh --check` lists leftover paths without writing."],[4232,"`bazelisk run //scripts:prod-stack -- up` never invents a label."],[5034,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` prints it because no lockfile is committed\nunder `templates/`, and Coder pushes the directory without one."],[288,"`bazelisk run //scripts:push-template` runs `scripts/push-template.sh`."],[4786,"`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` (or `scripts/search-check.sh` from a shell that\ncan reach the host gateway) dials the service directly. It reads `$SEARXNG_URL`\nbefore its `http://127.0.0.1:8181` default, and every workspace's agent env carries\nthat key, so a workspace-side probe needs no `--url`."],[2759,"`bazelisk run //scripts:search-check` from a host shell counts results per\nengine, which is how an empty answer stays distinguishable from no answer"],[114,"`bazelisk run //scripts:stack-prs` is the CLI for the chain: it records the layers in order,\nbottom to top, and performs the bottom-up merges through `merge-async` and nothing else."],[3710,"`bazelisk run //scripts:storage-clean` is the interactive janitor for the persistent\nlayer if a volume outgrows what you meant it to hold."],[593,"`bazelisk run //scripts:subagent-check` is the same comparison for a shell, and the same\n  two numbers answer it: a profile whose marker the operator removed gets neither the line\n  nor the note, because both are products of this render."],[2207,"`bazelisk run //scripts:subagent-check` reads both and names which of the three readings you\nhave, and counts the above one as a `note:` beside the ok items, since past the fleet's\nstreams the extra spawns queue in LiteLLM rather than failing."],[3985,"`bazelisk run //scripts:update-stack -- --dry-run` plans that fetch\n(`note: dry run: would refresh <file> from Infisical`) and writes nothing; the two stack\nrunners have no dry-run mode, so their refresh happens only on a real `up`."],[3697,"`bazelisk run //scripts:validate` covers the coarser layer: it parses every dashboard JSON\nthrough `scripts/lib/parse_json.py` (`scripts/validate.sh:104`), the gate that already\ncovers the three rule files."],[43,"`bazelisk test //:lint --test_tag_filters=manual`"],[42,"`bazelisk test //:test-fast`"],[1236,"`bazelisk test //:test-fast`"],[1050,"`bazelisk test //tests:bats_litellm_config_test`\npins the config and the compose passthrough, and `docs/troubleshooting.md` names the one log\nline that shows the hop firing."],[1516,"`bazelisk test` replays a cached PASS for an action whose inputs did not change, so the derived list\nproves your change against whatever actually rebuilds, and one\n`bazelisk test //:test-fast --nocache_test_results` remains the pass you owe before the PR, per\nbuilding-with-bazel.md."],[4181,"`bootstrap --check` prints the same\nprobes the full run makes (tools, compose plugin, placeholder secrets by name, hosts,\ncerts, GPU, images) and writes nothing. An unrecognised argument in either script is\nan error, never a silent fall-through to the mutating run."],[1007,"`bootstrap.sh` derives `STACK_DASHBOARD_URL` / `STACK_CODER_URL` /\n`STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `STACK_GRAFANA_URL` /\n`STACK_INFISICAL_URL` from the prefix on each entry (`coder-` / `kasm-` / `litellm-` /\n`hermes-` / `grafana-` / `infisical-`, dotted form still\nrecognised) and upgrades `CODER_ACCESS_URL` to the coder name, so Coder builds\nworkspace links on a secure context with a real certificate."],[2772,"`bootstrap` refreshes the key only after checking the path holds this\n`docker-compose.yml`: compose mounts whatever the key names, so a