s/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when\nthe Spark lane is dark), `agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, and\n`chat` as the Coder Agents chat model configs, through `CODER_AGENTS_PROVIDER_URL`\n(default `http://litellm:4000/v1`). `agent-swarm` is registerable but is never picked\nfor an auxiliary lane pin: the control-plane chat agent is one conversation, and one\nconversation wants the pin."],[3090,"`scripts/coder-agents.sh` registers `agent` (the default, with `chat` standing in when the\nSpark lane is dark), `agent-low`/`agent-medium`/`agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, and `chat`\nas Coder Agents chat model configs, for the same reason: an agent turn without callable\ntools cannot browse or edit, so registering `coder` or `coder-fast` would only add a\nvisibly broken model to the pickers."],[4235,"`scripts/lib/domain.sh` owns the grammar and every part is normalized before it is\njoined, so a value copied out of a browser bar cannot leak into an origin or a\n`server_name`:"],[4051,"`scripts/lib/pg-apply-password.sh` reconciles the password at the `db` entrypoint, so\nthe stored credential is brought in line with `CODER_PG_PASSWORD` on every start of that\ncontainer."],[1615,"`scripts/lib/tf_types.awk` is run by `//:validate` over both template twins and\nreports three findings:"],[1258,"`scripts/seed-dev-env.sh` materializes, into a laptop or CI home, the same stack-owned\nfiles a Coder workspace writes at startup — from the same template payloads, with no\nTerraform and no docker."],[1088,"`scripts/setup-host.sh` is the only script run straight from a shell, and it is the\n**stack host's** bootstrap: the box that runs Coder, LiteLLM, Ollama, Kasm, Homepage and\nHermes, where Compose and Bazel have to live on the host itself. It installs what Bazel\nneeds (Bazelisk, shellcheck, shfmt, buildifier, Terraform), so it necessarily runs before\nBazel exists — that is its whole exemption, not a convenience:"],[2400,"`SEARXNG_CACHE_TTL_MS` (15 minutes, which switches on the bounded 128-entry response\ncache that ships disabled, so a repeated query stops re-dialling backends that already\nrefused this instance)"],[2403,"`SEARXNG_DEFAULT_LANGUAGE` (`all`, so an image bump cannot quietly start geo-filtering\nevery agent search)"],[2402,"`SEARXNG_DEFAULT_SAFESEARCH` (mirrors `search.safe_search` in `searxng/settings.yml`)"],[2401,"`SEARXNG_MAX_RESULTS` (a ceiling that keeps one search inside the MCP output cap - on the\npinned 1.0.77 CLI that cap rides `GROK_MAX_MCP_OUTPUT_BYTES`, because `grok inspect`\nstill answers `[mcp] — unrecognized config key` and never applies it)"],[2399,"`SEARXNG_TIMEOUT_MS` (20s, above the instance's per-engine budget and equal to the\nprobe's `--timeout`)"],[1868,"`searxng/settings.yml`"],[3575,"`searxng`"],[2383,"`searxng` (SearXNG metasearch, host port 8181) is the search backend the stack runs\nfor itself: keyless engines only, no key, no account, no request logging. A\nworkspace reaches it through the host gateway, so the workspace networks stay\nuntouched."],[4129,"`section` prints `== name ==`"],[3410,"`SECURITY.md`"],[2536,"`send`, `launch` and `stop` change things; the rest read. `watch` blocks: it returns\n0 as soon as a session stops working and 4 when its budget runs out with the session\nstill `running`, which is what makes \"tell me when it's done\" a single call an\nassistant can follow with a push. Global options\n(`--json`, `--container`, `--timeout`, `--dry-run`) go before the subcommand, and\n`--json` makes `list`, `show` and `watch` printable by a phone client."],[4733,"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is 0, not 2, on each of the nine Spark lanes\n(`agent`, `agent-low`, `agent-medium`, `agent-xhigh`, `agent-swarm`, `agent-explore`,\n`agent-review`, `agent-xhigh-fast`, `agent-swarm-fast`): one attempt for a dead stream,\nso a hung request is bounded at 1200 s instead of up to 3465 s."],[4742,"`ServiceUnavailableErrorRetries` is the only one of the two that can be zeroed; zeroing\n`DefaultRetries` breaks the `NoRoom` contract above."],[3106,"`session_affinity` sits above the picker, in `router_settings.optional_pre_call_checks`,\nand it arms the router's `DeploymentAffinityCheck`. Checked on LiteLLM 1.102.1."],[1626,"`set -euo pipefail` is the floor, so an unset parameter is a failure rather than\nan empty string."],[571,"`set-clipboard on` and `allow-passthrough on` stay for the one copy route that is\n  not tmux's own - an app inside a pane (vim's `+` register, anything that emits\n  OSC 52) drives the machine clipboard through them, which is what a native terminal\n  hands that app - and a client that blocks OSC 52 (iTerm2 by default) gets nothing at\n  all, because there is no tmux buffer left to fall back to."],[752,"`settings.json.tftpl` is copied once, the first time `~/.shared/vscode/settings.json`\nis missing, onto the per-owner volume `vscode-settings-<owner-id>`, where it stays as\nthe overlay below. Startup chowns that volume (and the three `~/.grok` state volumes)\nto the home owner before the write: an empty Docker named volume is `root:root` 755, and the agent runs\nas `coder`."],[2351,"`shared` (the default here): the three state directories ride their owner's\nvolumes, so the notes, the memory and the sessions are the same files in every\nworkspace that account owns, and they survive a rebuild. `config.toml` is not part\nof that share - each workspace renders its own."],[994,"`shared` (the default): every workspace of a user mounts three of that user's\nvolumes nested inside `/home/coder` - `grok-notes-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/notes`,\n`grok-memory-<owner-id>` at `~/.grok/memory`, `grok-sessions-<owner-id>` at\n`~/.grok/sessions` - so the notes, the memory and the sessions are one set of state\nthat account's workspaces share, and they survive a rebuild."],[408,"`shared` puts that workspace's notes, memory and sessions on one per-owner volume\neach"],[3214,"`SINGLE_DEPLOYMENT_TRAFFIC_FAILURE_THRESHOLD` is 1000, so the \"all\nrequests failed\" arm never fires at this fleet's volume."],[388,"`SKIP_TEMPLATE_PUSH=1 bazelisk run //scripts:bootstrap` skips the step."],[2813,"`SOUL.md` is the identity - voice and stance, a short persona. It is not a fork of\nAGENTS.md: the repo's shell conventions stay in that file, where the\nwriters and the linters read them."],[3664,"`Spark $host`"],[4341,"`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE` (line 621, the pin above, and the knob you correct if the\nregistry refuses that tag)"],[4340,"`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 612)"],[1019,"`SPARK_HTTP_PORT` (default 8888) is the port every box of the recipe answers\non - TensorFold's `/v1` and `/health`, for the `agent` lane and the cards."],[3831,"`SPARK_MONITOR_ENABLED` (false as shipped) is the opt-in that decides whether `--targets`\nprints the three boxes or an empty array, and the DCGM image comes from\n`SPARK_DCGM_EXPORTER_IMAGE`."],[1023,"`SPARK_n_API_KEY` is the bearer token for each endpoint. TensorFold is\nvLLM-lineage, so treat it like vLLM/SGLang: any non-empty string is accepted\nunless the server was started with `--api-key`, in which case set the REAL key\nper box."],[4339,"`SPARK_NODE_EXPORTER_PORT` (line 607)"],[4617,"`spark-configure` / `spark-verify` writing `sparks/` output into a tree Bazel\nrecreates"],[3809,"`spark.rules.yml` the eight Spark alerts"],[3818,"`SparkDown` needs the probe to fail as well as the scrape, so a box that is genuinely up\nand simply not reporting host metrics pages nothing, and `SparkGpuSeriesSilent` asks only\nthe DCGM family."],[3819,"`SparkHostMetricsSilent` is the one that reads a box whose health probe succeeds while\n`spark:box_up` has never been 1 - the usual cause being a hand-edited file where the\nstamped `exporter` label lost its exact spelling, which drops that box's group out of\nevery job's keep filter."],[1026,"`SPARKn_HEALTH_URL` (kept commented in the twin) feed the dashboard's per-box\nSpark cards - one widget per box, so no card assumes one \"usual\" box. Compose\nbuilds each from `SPARKn_HOST` plus `SPARK_HTTP_PORT`; nothing but the\ndashboard reads them."],[1017,"`SPARKn_HOST` is where each box is named - once. docker-compose.yml composes\nevery Spark URL from it plus the port below, so a box that gets a new\nDHCP name or a static IP is edited in this one place and the LiteLLM\ndeployments, the dashboard's per-box cards, and the host list\n`//scripts:spark-verify` / `//scripts:spark-configure` probe all move\ntogether."],[1020,"`SPARKn_OPENAI_URL` is the OpenAI-compatible `/v1` endpoint. It is an\noverride, kept commented in the twin: set it when that box answers somewhere\nthe host plus port cannot express (https behind a reverse proxy, two boxes on\none host)."],[4237,"`STACK_CODER_NAME` / `STACK_KASM_NAME` / `STACK_LITELLM_NAME` / `STACK_HERMES_NAME` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` / `STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (defaults `coder` / `kasm` / `litellm` /\n`hermes` / `grafana` / `infisical`) are the service prefixes. `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME`\nnames the observability UI only: the metrics backends it fronts get no key of their\nown, and stay unexposed.\n`STACK_DASHBOARD_NAME` is empty by default, which puts the dashboard on the zone apex."],[4251,"`STACK_CODER_URL` / `STACK_KASM_URL` / `STACK_LITELLM_URL` / `STACK_HERMES_URL` /\n`STACK_GRAFANA_URL`, the card `href`s. A `hermes-*` or `hermes.*` entry is what fills\n`STACK_HERMES_URL`. Compose copies that value into `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL`, the\nHost and Origin the Chat tab checks. The same prefix rule fills `STACK_GRAFANA_URL`\nfrom a `grafana-*` or `grafana.*` entry, and compose copies that into\n`HOMEPAGE_VAR_GRAFANA_URL`, the observability card's `href`;"],[4250,"`STACK_DASHBOARD_URL`, which compose feeds to `HOMEPAGE_EXTERNAL_URL` so NextAuth\nbuilds its redirects and Secure cookie from the public origin;"],[2781,"`STACK_DEPLOY_PATH` (default `/opt/stack-deploy`) is the **deploy workspace**: the one\ndirectory meant for clones, builds, and the compose project of a third-party project\nthe assistant brings up."],[4236,"`STACK_DOMAIN_BASE` (default `overeazy.io`) is the zone the edge terminates TLS for;\n`STACK_SUBDOMAIN_SUFFIX` (default `gmktecbeast`) is this box's label, shared by every\nname."],[4242,"`STACK_ENV_LABEL` is empty for the stable stack and sits **after** the service prefix.\nTwo reasons: the one-label rule below rules the dotted form out anyway, and\n`classify_public_entry` in `scripts/lib/bootstrap.sh` routes an entry by its service\nprefix, so `coder-dev-box.example.com` is Coder while `dev-coder-box.example.com`\nfalls through to the dashboard."],[2770,"`STACK_HOST_PATH` is the checkout's `pwd -P`, or, from a subdirectory or a linked\nworktree, its git top-level. Compose bind-mounts that host path at\nthe same path inside the container, so `docker compose -f $STACK_HOST_PATH/docker-compose.yml`\ntalking through the socket names bind sources the daemon can find."],[2696,"`STACK_HOST_PATH` naming a directory with no checkout (compose mounts an empty\ndirectory over the repo and every container-level check still reads healthy)"],[4238,"`STACK_INFISICAL_NAME` (default `infisical`) is the seventh prefix and one of the two\nbehind a compose profile - `STACK_GRAFANA_NAME` is the other, whose `grafana` service\nsits under `profiles: [\"observability\"]`."],[4252,"`STACK_INFISICAL_URL`, from a `infisical-*` or `infisical.*` entry, the vault card's\n`href`. Its badge reads down while `INFISICAL_ENABLED` is off, which is the flag\nsaying so rather than a broken link;"],[4256,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS_MODE=auto`, which records that the names are derived. Under `auto`\nthe parts are the source of truth and `STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is output only, so a list\nedited by hand there is ignored and bootstrap prints a `note:` naming the key to\nclear. It clears itself on the LAN-only and explicit-list paths;"],[5240,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` does not create the vhost. It is what bootstrap writes\ninto `STACK_HERMES_URL` / `HERMES_DASHBOARD_PUBLIC_URL` for the Chat tab.\n`bootstrap` now also warns when a name in that list is missing from the\nconf it mounts."],[4244,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` has three states, and only the middle one composes:"],[1000,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS` is the one switch, and it has three states:"],[4212,"`STACK_PUBLIC_HOSTS=auto` additionally serves the same seven services on public names over\nreal TLS, each name composed from the parts you set once. It is purely additive: every LAN\nroute keeps working."],[272,"`stack-advisors.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_advisors\"` body that seeds those seven\npayloads: each skill into `~/.coder/skills/<name>/` and its `cp` twin into\n`~/.agents/skills/<name>/`, with `documenter.md` written under `~/.grok/agents/` beside the\nskill that needs it so a start has the command and its role or has neither. Absent-only and\nsilent on a healthy workspace, the way the two skill bodies above are."],[274,"`stack-hooks.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_hooks\"` body that seeds that family, from\nsix `chomp(file())` entries, on its own body for the usual ceiling reason."],[4160,"`stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl`"],[270,"`stack-repo-skills.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_repo_skills\"` body that seeds\nthat family, from eight `chomp(file())` entries: one `SKILL.md` per command plus the four\npython helpers. It is a second body rather than eight\nmore heredocs in the first one for the reason this whole file keeps repeating: each\n`coder_script.script` is its own `execve` argument with its own ceiling, and a family\nthat ships helpers grows faster than one that ships a `SKILL.md` each."],[4151,"`stack-skills.sh.tftpl`"],[260,"`stack-skills.sh.tftpl` is the `coder_script \"stack_skills\"` body that seeds that trio,\nrendered with `templatefile()` from the three `chomp(file())` entries `main.tf` passes it.\nIt is absent-only and silent on a healthy workspace, because its log is what an operator\nreads when a skill is missing."],[549,"`startup_script_behavior = \"blocking\"`, so a workspace only reports *ready* once the\ntoolchain is genuinely staged - no opening an editor mid-install."],[4824,"`startup_script` is blocking by design, so a failure there surfaces as a **failed**\nbuild with the reason in the workspace startup log. The container is not marked\nhealthy until the toolchain is staged."],[4904,"`startup_script` is blocking, so it finished before the agent reported ready and\nbefore code-server started. Check the workspace agent logs first."],[317,"`startup_size_guard` in `scripts/push-template.sh` enforces this before the CLI runs,\nbeside the icon and preset-description guards. It sums each body's composed bytes and\nrefuses the push past `STARTUP_SIZE_BOUND` (122880, which leaves 8 KiB, about two\ndocumentation-sized commits, under the kernel's own limit). Its reader is\n`scripts/lib/startup_size.awk`; `//tests:bats_startup_script_size_test` re-derives the\nsame totals with an independent parser and pins the two together, so a guard that starts\nmeasuring something else goes red in review."],[506,"`startup.sh.tftpl` clones the repository onto that bind in **both** toggle modes."],[3781,"`storage-clean` is the janitor for the persistent layer and asks before it deletes."],[1706,"`stub_bin <tool…>` copies each stub out of `STUB_BIN_DIR` into `${BATS_TEST_TMPDIR}/bin`\nand `chmod 755`s it; `use_stubs` = `stub_bin` + a fresh `STUB_LOG` + the PATH shadow.\nThe copy-plus-chmod is load-bearing: the Bazel sandbox and some tar extractions strip the\nexec bit, and a per-test copy keeps one test's argv log out of the next test."],[1711,"`stub_log <tool>`, `stub_log_last`, `stub_log_count` read back what the stub recorded from\n`$STUB_LOG/<tool>.log`; assert on the argv, not on prose."],[763,"`subdomain = false` everywhere: there is no `CODER_WILDCARD_ACCESS_URL` on this box, so\nthe editor is proxied on the path-based access URL."],[4132,"`summary` prints `summary: N ok, W warnings, F failed`\nplus `  failed: …` and optional `next: cmd`"],[2386,"`supports_backend_search = false` does not route around it, because the client-side\n  fallback still hands the model alias a `web_search`-typed tool, which is the exact\n  shape that endpoint rejects. The fallback lane fails too: it is the `coder` alias,\n  Ollama `qwen2.5-coder:32b`, and that model is not pulled on the host."],[1376,"`tags` are indexed, so a reader who searches for the word you filed the page under finds it.\n`pageSchema` strips unknown keys, which is why `tags` is declared explicitly in\n`docs-site/lib/content.ts` — a new field needs the same treatment or it silently never\nreaches the index."],[3667,"`Temp headroom`"],[300,"`templates push` carries no description (`push` has no `--description` flag), so\n`templates edit --description` is the write. That text is the routing hint the Coder\nAgents chat reads when it picks a template to provision."],[5391,"`templates/*/main.tf`"],[4140,"`templates/common/` exists once above the twins instead and holds the single-copy\npayloads both templates consume:"],[242,"`templates/common/` exists once instead of twice, and both templates consume it.\n`template_push_one` stages every file in this list beside the staged `main.tf`; a\nmissing one fails the push before the CLI runs."],[1481,"`templates/common/` holds the files both consume as one shared copy"],[2287,"`templates/common/<name>.SKILL.md` for `feature-implementation`, `skeptical-review`,\n`security-audit` and `bug-hunt`, each seeded twice - `~/.coder/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for\nthe Coder Agents chat and `~/.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md` for the Grok Build CLI, the\nsecond always a `cp` of the first:"],[2225,"`templates/common/ez-micro-plan.plan_check.py:227` accepts a `review:` key\nnaming the whole panel, comma or space separated, and holds each reviewer to owning nothing\nwhile needing every task that ships a file."],[3923,"`templates/common/infisical-secrets.sh.tftpl` is the body both twins render into\n`coder_script \"infisical_secrets\"` (`run_on_start`, 120s timeout)."],[661,"`templates/common/terminal-launchers.sh.tftpl` is the case that earned it: a\n  launcher that never landed is a code-server tab that dies with `exit code: 127`, and\n  the body closed at `summary: 2 ok, 0 warnings, 0 failed` while writing nothing. The\n  bodies that seed the two skill homes under `~/.coder` and `~/.agents` still `mkdir -p`\n  directly - neither is a bind-mount parent, so nothing arrives there owned by root."],[4139,"`templates/workspace/` (the plain dev container) and `templates/devcontainer/` (the\nDinD-backed one) keep independent `.tftpl` payload copies (`startup.sh.tftpl`,\n`settings.json.tftpl`, `grok-config.toml.tftpl`). The twins must change together — today\nonly `settings.json.tftpl` is byte-identical across the two directories, so edit both\ncopies of that payload."],[1480,"`templates/workspace/` and `templates/devcontainer/` keep independent payload copies\nthat must move\ntogether"],[2689,"`terminal-cwd`, `write-root`, `web-lane`, `keyless-egress`, `mcp-servers`,\n`seed-drift`, `mount-identity`, `image-pin`, `model-key`, `dashboard`, `memories`"],[2790,"`terminal.backend: local` keeps the dangerous-command checker active. The `docker`\nbackend skips that checker, because it treats the container as the safety boundary -\nand here the container is mounted onto the host, so that boundary is not real."],[2695,"`terminal.cwd` still carrying the `${STACK_HOST_PATH}` token (Hermes validates that\nvalue with `expanduser()` alone, so the token reads as a missing directory while\n`hermes config get` prints it looking healthy)"],[765,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `\"tmux\"`. The automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build/tasks commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[2018,"`terminal.integrated.defaultProfile.linux` is `tmux`; the automation profile stays\n`/bin/bash`, so build and task commands never wait on tmux. Plain `bash` and **Grok\nBuild** stay available as extra profiles."],[4871,"`terminal.limited-color`: a tool command spawned by a running Grok session\ninherits `NO_COLOR=1`. Rerun `grok doctor` from the terminal itself and the\nreport reads `color truecolor` with `themes all`."],[4873,"`terminal.ssh-wrap` asks to be run on the workstation you type `ssh` on:\n`grok doctor fix ssh-wrap` there. Nothing a workspace installs satisfies it."],[299,"`terraform init` on the server still talks to `registry.terraform.io` (and\n`registry.coder.com` for `devcontainer`'s `devcontainers_cli` module), even\nwhen a previous provider version is cached. The `coder` compose service\nforwards those lookups to `1.1.1.1` / `8.8.8.8` so a host whose only\nnameserver is Tailscale MagicDNS does not SERVFAIL the push. Recreate\n`coder` after pulling that compose change (`prod-stack up`) before pushing."],[1675,"`tests/bats_runner.sh` — what Bazel executes: it locates bats-core and this checkout in\nrunfiles, exports `REPO_ROOT`, and execs bats."],[1692,"`tests/bats_runner.sh` points `REPO_ROOT` at\nthat mirror when `EVO_COV_MIRROR` is set, so the suites execute the instrumented\ncopies through whichever access pattern they already use — run the CLI, source the\nlib, or `load_shell_function`. Then `tests/coverage/aggregate.py` names every\nfunction whose body never ran."],[1671,"`tests/bats/*.bats` — one suite per module. `tests/bats.bzl` turns each file into one\n`bats_<stem>_test` sh_test (dashes become underscores: `bats_spark_configure_test` for\n`spark-configure.bats`) and adds it to the `bats` suite that `//:test-fast` runs."],[3678,"`tests/bats/observability-dashboards.bats` asserts seventeen properties over every file in\nit:"],[1672,"`tests/bats/test_helper.bash` — the shared helpers, pulled in with `load 'test_helper'`."],[1674,"`tests/bats/tool_stubs/` — the fake external tools (`docker`, `ssh`, `curl`, `git`,\n`coder`, `psql`, `timeout`, …); `tests/bats/fixtures/` holds a fixture that has to answer\nlike the real client, such as the `psql-token` copies that echo `returning id`."],[1132,"`tests/bazel_deps.py` re-derives all of them by scanning the shell and Bats sources and\nreports the drift both ways: a script that starts reading a payload its target does not\ndeclare, and a target that still ships a payload nothing opens."],[1448,"`tests/BUILD.bazel` names what each Bats suite opens"],[4108,"`tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails the build when a header or a block is missing."],[3176,"`tests/python/test_compaction_router.py` proves which group each\nrequest shape lands on; the operator's own log line is what will prove what it costs."],[1676,"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a function under `scripts/` is not named under\n`tests/`; `tests/doc_coverage.sh` fails when a `# @function` block (or the file header) is\nmissing or incomplete. So every new function needs, in the same change as the function:"],[4110,"`tests/shell_inventory.sh` fails when a name is missing, and `tests/doc_coverage.sh` when a\n`# @function` block is missing or incomplete."],[1673,"`tests/test_helpers.sh` — the assertions, shared with the bash gates."],[4553,"`the env file was not refreshed from Infisical` -> the vault did not answer the bring-up refresh"],[4481,"`The terminal process \"/bin/bash '-lc', 'exec \"$HOME/.grok/bin/grok-build-terminal\"'\" terminated with exit code: 1` -> something closed the tmux session under the tab"],[4482,"`The terminal process \"/bin/bash '-lc', 'exec \"$HOME/.grok/bin/vscode-terminal-tmux\"'\" terminated with exit code: 127` -> the launcher was never written"],[2284,"`thorough-researcher`, `senior-implementer` and `skeptical-reviewer` ship as\n`templates/common/<name>.PERSONA.toml` and land at `~/.grok/personas/<name>.toml`, where the\nfile stem is the persona name (`~/.grok/docs/user-guide/16-subagents.md:93`). Discovery runs\nthe project's `.grok/personas/`, then this user directory, then the bundled catalog, and an\ninline `[subagents.personas]` table outranks all three (`:89-93`)."],[4741,"`TimeoutErrorRetries: 0` would not help: the hang never becomes a `litellm.Timeout`."],[1374,"`title` is the sidebar label, the search result title, and the string your `# H1` has to\nmatch character for character."],[4267,"`tls: {}` means \"serve whatever certificate matches the SNI\", which is the zone's\n`*.overeazy.io` pair. Add `tls: { certResolver: <name> }` if that box issues through\nan ACME resolver instead. SNI has to be the public name itself - the wildcard covers\nexactly one label, which is why the hyphenated one-label form is the only one that\nhandshakes."],[2182,"`tmux kill-server` / `kill-session` / `kill-window` / `kill-pane` / `restart-server`"],[2023,"`tmux ls` lists the names and `tmux list-clients` says who is looking at each."],[4144,"`tmux.conf`"],[249,"`tmux.conf` is the fidelity config the startup script writes. `grok-build-terminal.sh`\nand `vscode-terminal-tmux.sh` are the two terminal launchers, and they ride\n`coder_script \"terminal_launchers\"` with the shared body\n`templates/common/terminal-launchers.sh.tftpl` rather than the startup argument, which\nis the one execve that runs out of room: nothing at boot runs either script, the\ncode-server terminal profile is their only consumer. Helpers use `$folder` (not\n`$${folder}`) because `file()` contents are not re-interpolated."],[3363,"`UMASK=0077` keeps the image itself out of unprivileged hands, and the initramfs\nhost keys stay separate from `/etc/ssh` on purpose - they are generated per boot\nimage, and sharing them would leak which key the sealed machine answers to."],[4524,"`Unexpanded count argument on block \"coder_app.github_app_install\"` -> apps were gated on start_count"],[4521,"`unexpected non-JSON response \"text/html; charset=utf-8\"` -> Authelia answered the CLI"],[1495,"`unlisted` is how you find out you have not done that, rather than finding out that the gate never\nnoticed."],[3769,"`update-configs` is what a `git pull` cannot do for you, because the files that go stale are\nnot tracked — see config-updates.md."],[3606,"`update-stack.sh` in `stack_compose()`"],[530,"`use_devcontainer=false` whenever `devcontainer` was chosen but no dev container was\nfound (forced with `--devcontainer` onto a repo that has none, or a probe that could\nnot check the repo - the cases where leaving the toggle at its default would build\nnothing anyway)"],[5383,"`values.yaml`"],[4870,"`voice.no-input-device`: the container has no microphone and no recorder on\nPATH. The probe checks for a recorder binary, so installing `alsa-utils` makes\nDoctor answer `available` with nothing to record from; the image ships no\nrecorder and the finding stays. The PulseAudio route that would make capture\nreal: the voice bullet in grok-build-and-agents.md."],[4142,"`vscode-alt.svg`"],[244,"`vscode-alt.svg` is the editor icon (read with `filebase64()`). It stays small\nenough that the resulting `data:image/svg+xml;base64,...` URL fits Coder\nv2.36's `workspace_apps.icon` column (`varchar(256)`)."],[540,"`vscode-settings-<owner-id>` at `~/.shared/vscode`"],[4146,"`vscode-terminal-tmux.sh`"],[5092,"`vscode-terminal-tmux`\nreattaches a folder's session only while nothing is attached to it, and opens a\nnumbered sibling (`vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`, `-3`, ...) beside live ones, so an\nextra tab is a new pane rather than a mirror. Every session target in that\nhelper carries tmux's `=` exact-match prefix, because a bare name is also\nanswered by any session starting with it - once `foo` is closed, `foo-2` would\notherwise answer for it and put a second client on a live pane."],[767,"`vscode-terminal-tmux` keeps `vscode-<folder>-<hash>` as its base session name. It\nre-joins one of that folder's sessions while no client is attached to it, oldest\nfirst (the closed-tab rejoin: an unattached session is reused even while sibling\nterminals are live); it takes the next numbered sibling, `vscode-<folder>-<hash>-2`,\n`-3`, ... only when every session of the folder already has a client."],[4980,"`warning: <container> could not be read (docker exec returned <rc>) - its profile says nothing`\nis the exec itself failing for a reason that is not a missing file and not the timeout:\nany status outside 0, 1, 2, 124 and 137 lands here."],[4979,"`warning: <container> did not answer within <n>s - the sweep cannot read its profile`\nis the cap biting: the exec had not returned when `PROBE_TIMEOUT` cut it off, which is\n`timeout(1)`'s 124, or 137 once the kill landed. The container is up and hung."],[5197,"`warning: the\n  machine identity could not exchange a token; check the client id and secret, and that\n  the vault answers` when the vault is there but will not authenticate the identity\n  (`scripts/infisical.sh:361`)"],[5191,"`warning: the Infisical export\n  produced an empty file; <file> is untouched` when it answered with nothing"],[2756,"`web_extract` is deliberately unconfigured. SearXNG aggregates engine results\nand does not fetch pages, so the seed leaves `web.backend` and\n`web.extract_backend` empty and page extraction fails with \"no extract provider\nconfigured\"."],[2752,"`web_search` goes to the stack's SearXNG container: the seeded config sets\n`web.search_backend: searxng`, and compose gives the container\n`SEARXNG_URL=${STACK_HERMES_SEARCH_URL:-http://searxng:8181}`."],[2809,"`web-research` (what is worth looking up, how to read an empty answer from the\nlane above, and what that lane refuses to do)"],[1707,"`with_path <dir…>` prepends directories to PATH when a test needs a hand-written fake\ninstead of a shared stub."],[2426,"`workbench.colorTheme` re-asserted from the render last, so a theme put in the\nshared overlay loses to this workspace's own theme (the **Editor colour theme** field,\n`vscode_color_theme`, default `Default Dark Modern`)"],[2435,"`workspace_external_agent`, the entitlement the docs name, gates *external* agents\n(Claude Code, Codex, ...), not this one, and nothing here needs it."],[237,"`workspace` (`templates/workspace/`) is the plain dev container."],[943,"`workspace` addresses docker, so run it from a **host** shell. A terminal inside a\nworkspace is not a host shell: the `docker` CLI there is only installed so you can\ndrive that workspace's own DinD sidecar, so `docker ps` answers empty and an empty\n`docker ps` means \"wrong vantage\", not \"stack down\"."],[239,"`workspace` goes first, `devcontainer` last: a dependent template pushes last so a\npartial failure leaves the base template usable."],[2047,"`workspace` installs code-server only while that pinned release is missing from\n  `/home/coder/.local/share/code-server` (the guard names\n  `lib/code-server-<version>/bin/code-server`, the directory the installer unpacks, not\n  the `bin/code-server` symlink, which points at the last release installed and would\n  hold a warm volume on its first one) and starts it with `--auth none --port 13337\n  --app-name \"VS Code Web\" --ignore-last-opened <folder>`, passing no settings payload."],[781,"`workspace` keeps the clone tree under `/home/coder/workspace/<repo-name>`;\n`devcontainer` keeps it under `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo-name>`."],[904,"`workspace` re-materialises the stack-owned config inside the targets you name."],[2050,"`workspace` uses `/home/coder/workspace/<repo>` (and `/home/coder` with no\nrepository); `devcontainer` uses `/workspaces/<repo>` inside a built dev container\nand `/srv/coder-devcontainers/<workspace-id>/<repo>` on the plain container."],[285,"`workspace`: **AI workspace** (golden image, the selected default), **Minimal shell**\n(bare `ubuntu:24.04`, for when the golden image is mid-rebuild), **AI workspace + DinD**\n(golden image plus a privileged Docker daemon)."],[4815,"`X-Coder-Request-Id` on the response, beside `X-Coder-Build-Version`, means Coder's\nown Go proxy answered, so Traefik and Cloudflare let the request through and the\nfault sits behind them. Nothing on this box sets `Via` or `CF-RAY`."],[4816,"`X-Evo-Proxy` names the `server_name` that matched here: `coder`, `litellm`, `kasm`,\n`homepage` or `hermes`. Its absence means the request never reached this box's nginx."],[1610,"`zod` already validates the frontmatter at the content boundary in\n`docs-site/lib/content.ts`, so parsed pages arrive as typed `PageNode`s rather than\nas `unknown`."],[2858,"<Card title=\"Contribute\" href=\"/contribute/devcontainer/\" description=\"The golden image, the contributor container, and the Bazel gates a change runs through.\" />"],[2855,"<Card title=\"Getting started\" href=\"/getting-started/\" description=\"First boot, the order the services come up in, and what to check once Coder answers.\" />"],[2856,"<Card title=\"Learn the stack\" href=\"/learn/\" description=\"Templates, model routing, the workspace agent, Hermes, conventions, branching.\" />"],[2857,"<Card title=\"Operate the box\" href=\"/operate/\" description=\"Dashboard and desktops, config refresh, public access, troubleshooting.\" />"],[2896,"> **Not the same as the `/ez-learn` command.** This tab is the concepts index for people\n> reading the docs. `/ez-learn` typed at the Grok Build prompt turns the session you are in\n> into durable memory - flush it into a session log, dream those logs into organized memory,\n> remember one note, or browse and clear the store; it is this stack's copy of the upstream\n> `/flush`, `/dream`, `/remember` and `/memory`.\n>\n> Skill authoring is its siblings:\n>\n> - `/ez-skillify` captures the session that just worked\n> - `/ez-create-skill` scaffolds one from a description\n>\n> `grok inspect` lists the three once their seed has landed. A missing one is the skill-seed\n> entry in troubleshooting.md."],[5367,"| #   | Criterion                                     | How you prove it                                             |\n| --- | --------------------------------------------- |