ner logs below,\nnot from this check."],[3582,"Its traffic is measured anyway, because every request reaches it through LiteLLM,\nwhich does publish. What every one of those containers does contribute is its log\nstream, which is where a SearXNG rate limit or a Kasm start failure lands."],[4945,"Its verdict for this fault (`scripts/subagent-check.sh`):"],[1493,"joins `//:stack_data` by label"],[5319,"K3s / Kubernetes"],[1820,"Kasm is a privileged Docker-in-Docker container, and privileged whether or not the\nobservability bundle is on. The other privileged containers are `cadvisor`, which comes\nup only with the `observability` profile\n(observability.md), and the opt-in workspace DinD sidecar. Keep Kasm\nLAN-only unless you have decided who may reach the public name\n(see SECURITY.md)."],[4217,"Kasm is the only hop that speaks TLS to its origin. `proxy_ssl_verify` is off there\nbecause the certificate is the one Kasm's installer generates and reissues. Hermes,\nlike Homepage, Coder and LiteLLM, is plain HTTP at the origin (`http://hermes:9119`)."],[4288,"Kasm stores its connection endpoints in its own setup database (first-boot wizard or\nadmin UI), so the endpoint has to change there too or streams keep dialing the LAN\naddress."],[4570,"Kasm wizard gone after install -> expected"],[1746,"Kasm Workspaces"],[1752,"Kasm's wizard-and-UI port pair, and what resetting it destroys"],[776,"Keep it that way unless a workspace genuinely needs two trees open at once."],[5447,"Keep the existing gates green and extend what exists. No new CI system, no Kubernetes, no\nflag platform, no new dependency."],[471,"Keep the other licensed flags out of the CE stack too."],[1417,"Keep the uncached pass"],[3042,"Keep what one workspace puts on one box at or under that box's `PARALLEL` (5). A pinned\nlane sends the whole fan-out to the box its session key resolves to, so the width worth\nasking for there is 4: one box's streams, minus the parent's own request."],[4479,"Keep your own copy, or give the file back"],[4120,"Keep:"],[827,"Keeping a checkout's config current"],[829,"Keeping a checkout's config current"],[1159,"Keeping it maintained"],[1185,"Keeping the container maintained as the project moves"],[890,"keeps every value you have set"],[4627,"keeps MagicDNS for `ts.net` via `resolvectl` split-DNS"],[2379,"Keyed by **owner id**, never host-wide and never cross-user: another user's\nworkspace mounts a differently named volume, and a stop or start of a *different\nuser's* workspace never sees the volume at all."],[3870,"Keys the vault does not hold"],[144,"Knobs and skips"],[2927,"Knobs that look right and do nothing"],[4595,"Knowing the cost of a fix before running it, including what it destroys"],[37,"Knowing which green check actually gates a merge into `main`"],[2895,"Knowing which page owns a concept when two of them seem to"],[1847,"Knowing which step to re-run when a later one says a prerequisite is missing"],[2015,"Knowing why the native `web_search` tool is parked and the SearXNG lane answers"],[4442,"Land the fix"],[2326,"Land the rule in the seed (with the Bats case\nin `tests/bats/stack-hooks.bats` that denies it), or delete the marker from that one file to keep\na copy of your own, which the boot then leaves alone. A line that does not compile as an ERE is\nskipped with a\nsingle note on stderr rather than blocking every shell call, which is why the file's own header\ncarries the one-line compile check to run before you commit a rule:\n`LC_ALL=C grep -E -q -- '<pattern>' </dev/null`."],[38,"Landing one change as a chain of layers a reviewer reads alone, without bypassing the gate"],[1925,"Lane choice and the review panel"],[3426,"Lane ids are not declared here. `scripts/new-repo.sh` discovers them from the fetched tree's\n`.template-lanes/` directory at run time, so a lane the scaffold repository adds needs no change\nin this repo. The four below are what it ships today:"],[3376,"Lanes"],[1638,"Layout"],[2880,"Learn the stack"],[