68-token group - and is answered\nfrom a truncated reading, which is the outcome the fork exists to prevent."],[1384,"the label has to be the whole paragraph, bold, and spelled `What's on this page` and\n`What this enables`"],[1850,"The LAN address you will browse to. `CODER_ACCESS_URL` has to be an `https://` URL that\nis not `localhost`, or a workspace cannot reach LiteLLM."],[4234,"The LAN URL `http://<STACK_LAN_HOST>:9119` stays. HTTP basic auth on the dashboard stays\ntoo; Authelia is the extra public gate. See hermes.md."],[3134,"The lane decides placement, not width - what a pinned lane changes is where those 15\nland, and one box only ever admits `PARALLEL` (5) of them."],[2219,"The lane is therefore a config decision: the guide documents `[subagents.models]` as the per-type\noverride that \"applies for any parent\", and documents the spawn call's own `model`\nargument as the agent's choice of one child's model, without saying which of the two wins\nwhen both name one. Set the pin when the lane matters."],[2811,"The last four are what make the terminal door worth opening: they carry the mentoring\nstance - inspect, explain, propose, then act on the yes."],[817,"The last line is `push would WORK` or\n`push would FAIL`, keyed to the credential git would really send, and check exits\nnon-zero on FAIL."],[3358,"The last one is worth dwelling on: `check` can read every config file and still\nbe looking at an image GRUB cannot load, so an encrypted `/boot` is reported as\na warning to verify by hand rather than cleared."],[2692,"The last two are the ones that keep the phone story honest: an open door nobody\nconfigured, and a closed door nobody can find, are the same class of surprise."],[1811,"The layout follows the upstream README example - logo plus greeting top-left, boxed\nresource tiles, clock, weather and search top-right, cards on a wallpaper."],[1660,"The layout: suites, helpers, stubs and fixtures"],[2373,"The leaf is the container hostname with anything