r profile, and the one naming\nneither is the exit code 1 case above,\nwhere the file exists and tmux refuses."],[314,"The same move later made room for the rulebook: `GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md` is the largest\n  payload the twins inline, and it left the startup argument for\n  `coder_script.agents_seed` when that twin stood 196 bytes from the budget and one more\n  instruction section could not land."],[4839,"The same probe in the other spelling: `test -x ~/.grok/bin/grok`."],[145,"The same push, by hand"],[4636,"The same read would also have executed anything written on a line of its own in that\nfile, and it exported every other key there (`LITELLM_MASTER_KEY` included) into the\nenvironment of the `docker exec` calls."],[910,"The same rule applies as in `refresh-configs`: a file is replaced only while it\nstill carries its stack marker:"],[60,"The same rule is in `templates/common/GROK_BUILD_AGENTS.md`, which is the text every workspace\nseeds into `~/.grok/AGENTS.md`, so a commit written by an agent and one written by hand read the\nsame way in `git log`."],[651,"The same stage deletes a leftover `skills/learn/SKILL.md` from\n  before the rename, but only while it still carries the stack marker; a marker-free one is\n  user-owned and stays. Deleting a seeded copy never keeps your own - the boot writes it\n  back - so only dropping that marker does."],[680,"The same suite reads every\n`templatefile()` call in both twins, takes the keys each map declares and the names each\nbody interpolates, and fails on either direction of drift. Run the checker directly to\nsee the offending body and line:\n`python3 scripts/lib/tftpl_interpolation.py templates --strict`."],[2091,"The same sweep repeated at 120 columns came out close to linear at about 3 KB of\nserver per history line - 10 MB at 2000, 35 MB at 10000, 160 MB at 50000, 308 MB at\n100000 - so this is the one terminal number that turns a chatty pane into real\ncontainer memory, and it is a deliberate trade: the deep sto